// Derivative work based on LuxAlgo "Smart Money Concepts".
// FIX PASS 1-3: compile blockers, array-mutation-during-iteration, label churn,
//               order block status transitions.
// FIX PASS 4:   rendering cleanup and memory bounds.
// FIX PASS 5:   displacement filter, non-repainting FVG mode, alert semantics.
// FEATURE 24:   liquidity sweep detection on EQH/EQL.
// FEATURE 26:   higher timeframe bias filter.
// FEATURE MA:   moving averages (SMA/EMA x3, Kaufman KAMA).
// FEATURE MFI:  Money Flow Index overbought / oversold markers.
// FEATURE REG:  market regime detection. REPLACED in v4.4 by Wyckoff phase detection [W1].
// FEATURE 23:   alternative order block detection.
// FEATURE 25:   inverse fair value gaps. See CHANGELOG below.
// PASS 7 (IDX): accuracy fixes and Indonesia Stock Exchange adaptation. See CHANGELOG below.
// PASS 8 (IDX): retest signals, non-repainting HTF bias, extra liquidity pools, confluence filters. See below.
// PASS 9 (IDX): Wyckoff phase detection replaces the market regime quadrants. See below.
// PASS 10 (IDX): chart readability pass. See CHANGELOG below.
// PASS 11 (IDX): reversal confluence (divergence, volume climax, score). See CHANGELOG below.
// PASS 12 (IDX): false signals from strong trends. See CHANGELOG below.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG v4.7 IDX vs v4.6 IDX  (all three items attack the same failure: a trend pinning the oscillators)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [B2] RSC NOW VOTES ON DIRECTION, NOT LEVEL. A leading stock sits at RSC 100 for weeks, so its level carries
//      no information in a trend and reading it as "overbought" is how a trend manufactures false reversals.
//      The new vote is the SLOPE of RSC over a window (default 10 bars, 5 RSC points): relative strength
//      improving while price still falls is early accumulation, and the mirror is early distribution.
//      Maximum score is therefore 7, not 6.
// [B3] ZONE RESET. KRI and MFI zones used to award a vote on every bar spent inside them, which is exactly
//      what a trend produces. A zone now counts only on the bar it is ENTERED, and only after it has been
//      outside for the reset window (default 5 bars). One excursion, one vote. 0 restores the old behaviour.
// [B4] TREND REGIME GATE. Using the efficiency ratio percentile the Wyckoff module already computes: above
//      the gate (default 80th percentile) price is travelling in a straight line, every oscillator is pinned,
//      and exhaustion evidence is at its least reliable. There the threshold rises (default +1) and, by
//      default, a DIVERGENCE becomes mandatory, since it is the only vote that compares two swings rather
//      than judging a single bar. Set the gate to 100 to disable it.
//      The marker tooltip shows the effective threshold and flags "(strong trend)" when the gate is active.
//
// HOW TO JUDGE THIS: note N, Win% and Exp R on the Confluence Rev rows in v4.6 first, then compare the same
// symbol here. If N falls but Win% and Exp R do not improve, these filters only removed signals and should be
// loosened (lower the gate penalty, or set Zone Reset to 0) rather than kept out of faith.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG v4.6 IDX vs v4.5 IDX
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [B1] REVERSAL CONFLUENCE, a composite signal that fires only when several independent readings agree:
//        CONTEXT  price in the lower half of its long-term range (or a Wyckoff accumulation phase) for a
//                 bullish signal, the mirror for bearish. Switchable.
//        SCORE    +1 KRI at its extreme, +1 MFI at its extreme, +1 volume climax (heavy volume closing back
//                 against the move), +2 DIVERGENCE (price made a lower low / higher high while MFI or RSC did
//                 not), +1 liquidity taken (sweep of the opposite side, or a Wyckoff spring / upthrust).
//                 Each vote counts if it happened within the window, default 10 bars.
//        TRIGGER  an internal or swing CHoCH in the reversal direction. Evidence alone never fires anything.
//      Default minimum score 3 of a possible 6.
//      NEW: a divergence engine over confirmed pivots (MFI and RSC against price) and a volume climax test.
//      Neither existed before; both are also readable on their own through the marker tooltip.
//      The marker is a label (▲ below / ▼ above the candle, slot 4 of the marker stack) and the alerts go
//      through alert() ("Any alert() function call"), because the script is at Pine's 64-plot limit.
//      MEASURED, NOT PROMISED: two new statistics rows (Confluence Rev) so the composite can be compared with
//      the CHoCH and retest rows under identical rules. Filters multiply, so fewer signals is not automatically
//      better signals; raise the threshold only if that row says the survivors earn it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG v4.5 IDX vs v4.4 IDX  (readability only: no detection, alert or statistic changed)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [T1] "Chart Density" preset (Smart Money Concepts group): Minimal / Standard (default) / Full. It can only
//      SUBTRACT drawings from your own switches, never add. Minimal hides internal structure drawings, EQH/EQL
//      drawings, confirmation zones, order block status text, Wyckoff range boxes, session shading, the premium
//      /discount zones and the statistics table. Everything still calculates, so alerts and the statistics
//      population are identical in every preset.
// [T2] Order block status is now TEXT INSIDE THE BOX (right-aligned, tucked against the far edge) instead of a
//      floating label. The pooled status labels are gone, which removes one drawing per block and frees the
//      500-label budget for structure labels.
// [T3] RSC is drawn as a ZONE CURVE instead of a run of per-bar dots: thin dots (style_circles) that appear
//      ONLY while RSC is at an extreme, and take a side to say which one - above the highs while the stock
//      outperforms the benchmark, below the lows while it lags, nothing in between. It follows the shape of
//      the candles (two EMA passes over the highs / lows, offset by 3 ATR) and never crosses them. The RSC dot
//      markers are removed; MFI keeps the marker slots to itself, one per side per bar.
// [T4] DEFAULT CHANGED: Wyckoff Phase Detection is now OFF.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

//@version=6
indicator('SMC Plus v4.7 IDX', 'SMC Plus v4.7 IDX', overlay = true, max_labels_count = 500, max_lines_count = 500, max_boxes_count = 500)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG v4.4 IDX vs v4.3 IDX
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [W1] The market regime module [K-REG] is replaced by Wyckoff phase detection: ACCUMULATION, MARKUP,
//      DISTRIBUTION, MARKDOWN.
//        Step 1, trend or trading range: the efficiency ratio percentile with hysteresis is kept from [K-REG].
//                Trending + rising = Markup, trending + falling = Markdown. The ATR volatility axis is removed.
//        Step 2, which trading range: four votes (+1 accumulation / -1 distribution): prior trend, location in
//                the long-term range, effort (up-close vs down-close volume), and a recent spring / upthrust.
//                The sum names the range when it starts; inside it the name only flips on a score of 2 or
//                more against it, so the label does not flicker.
//      Display: phase label at the right edge (hover for the votes), optional background tint, faint boxes
//      around each accumulation / distribution range (last 5 by default), optional Spring / UT markers.
//      Alerts: one per phase, plus one shared spring / upthrust alert. The old "Market Regime Changed" alert is gone;
//      alerts bound to it must be recreated.
//      STILL DISPLAY ONLY, like [K-REG]: no structure, order block, gap, sweep, filter or statistics path reads
//      the phase. Wyckoff's finer schematic events (PS, SC, AR, ST, SOS, LPS, BC, SOW, LPSY) are not labelled;
//      the four phases are a coarse, mechanical reading and deserve the same scepticism as any filter here.
// [W2] Wyckoff drawings moved to the bottom layer. Range boxes and the phase readout are allocated on the
//      first bar, before every other box pool, and only reused afterwards: Pine draws the earliest-created
//      box underneath, so order blocks, confirmation zones and gaps now sit on top of them. The phase readout
//      is text in a borderless box instead of a label (labels always render above boxes and lines); since
//      boxes have no tooltip, the four votes are printed on its second line.
// [RS1] Relative Strength Comparison vs a benchmark (IDX:LQ45 by default): close / benchmark close, normalised
//      to 0-100 by stochastic (default) or percentile rank over a 50-bar lookback, smoothed by 3. A small dot above
//      the candle marks RSC above 80 (strong), below the candle RSC under 20 (weak), placed one ATR beyond the
//      wick so it does not collide with the MFI triangles or ARA/ARB labels. Value in the Data Window, alerts
//      for both levels. Display only.
// [RS2] RSC markers changed from size.tiny diamonds (the smallest plotshape) to a '•' plotchar at size.tiny,
//      70% opaque, which renders noticeably smaller and keeps long runs of markers unobtrusive. The MFI
//      triangles get the same treatment: '▾' / '▴' plotchar glyphs at size.tiny, 70% opaque.
// [KRI1] Kairi Relative Index: KRI = (close - MA25) / MA25 x 100. A tiny "Flop" label above the candle when KRI
//      enters overbought (default +10%), "Flip" below when it enters oversold (default -10%); an Adaptive mode
//      uses the stock's own 95th / 5th percentile KRI over 250 bars instead. One label per excursion, last 40
//      kept, KRI value in the tooltip, alerts via alert() ("Any alert() function call") because the script is
//      at the 64-plot limit.
//      MARKER STACK: MFI, RSC and KRI now share one ATR-based step, so they sit in fixed slots from the wick
//      outwards (MFI triangle, RSC dot, KRI label) at any zoom. "Marker Spacing (x ATR)" sets the step.
// [C4b] IDX session shading softened: the three windows used blue / purple / orange, which repeated every day on
//      intraday charts. They now share one muted grey (#787b86) and differ only in transparency: pre-closing 92,
//      opening 94, session 2 96. DEFAULT CHANGED: "Shade IDX Session Windows" is now OFF.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG v4.3 IDX vs v4.2 IDX
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// --- PASS 8a: MEASUREMENT VALIDITY ---
// [R1] OB RETEST and FVG RETEST signals. A retest is a bar that wicks into a live zone and closes back out of it
//      on the bias side: the actual SMC entry, where BOS/CHoCH are late confirmations. Each zone fires at most
//      once. Four new statistics rows and four new alerts. Zones are never evaluated on their creation bar.
// [R3] BUG FIX, inherited from LuxAlgo: bearish fair value gaps stored their edges swapped (top = lower edge).
//      A bearish gap was therefore deleted as soon as price touched its lower edge instead of when price traded
//      through it, and the IFVG it flipped into was deleted again on the next bar. Both biases now store
//      top = upper edge. VISIBLE CHANGE: bearish FVGs and bullish IFVGs now survive as long as bullish ones do.
// [R2] HTF bias is now requested as `expr[1]` with lookahead_on, i.e. from CONFIRMED higher timeframe bars only.
//      v4.2 used lookahead_off with no offset, which is leak-free on history but reads the still-forming HTF bar
//      in real time, so live signals were filtered differently from the history the statistics were built on.
//      The bias now flips one HTF bar later, identically on history and live.
//      DEFAULT CHANGED: Bias Timeframe '240' -> 'W'. A 4H bar on IDX straddles the lunch break, and a 240
//      setting silently disabled the filter on the daily chart most IDX traders use.
//
// --- PASS 8b: CONFLUENCE ---
// [C1] Liquidity pools are no longer EQH/EQL only. Swing highs/lows, the previous day high/low (intraday) and
//      the previous week high/low (daily and below) can each be registered as pools, each on its own toggle.
//      Sweep labels name the source (PDH Sweep, PWL Sweep, ...). Default Max Tracked Pools 10 -> 20.
// [C2] Premium/Discount filter for order blocks (default Off): a bullish OB is only stored when its midpoint
//      sits at or below the equilibrium of the current swing dealing range, a bearish OB at or above it.
// [C3] Strict CHoCH (default Off). A CHoCH must close through the PROTECTED level, the extreme of the leg that
//      produced the last break in the current trend. A break of a minor pivot inside that leg is consumed
//      silently: no label, no alert, no order block, and the trend does not flip.
// [C4] IDX session windows on intraday charts: opening (09:00-09:30), session 2 open (13:30-14:00 Mon-Thu,
//      14:00-14:30 Fri) and pre-closing (15:50-16:00), Asia/Jakarta. Display only: background tint.
//
// --- PASS 8c: STATISTICS TABLE ---
// [T1] BUG FIX: "Hide Signal Types With No Data" left a grid of empty rows. Leftover rows were blanked by
//      writing '' into them, which creates empty cells that still render borders. The table is now cleared
//      with table.clear() before every redraw, so hidden rows do not exist at all.
//      New "Table Style" input, default Compact: 4 columns, short names, no grid, translucent background, with
//      MFE / timeout / win-loss-open moved into row tooltips. "Full" restores the 6-column layout.
//
// --- PASS 8d: LIQUIDITY POOL VISUALS ---
// [V1] Sweep labels no longer sit on the wick at the pool level. They are anchored at the sweeping bar's
//      high (buyside) or low (sellside) and point at it, so the text is just beyond the candle. Compact style
//      (default) shows the source only (PWH, PDL, SH, SL, BSL, SSL) with the details in a tooltip; Full keeps
//      "PWH Sweep". Several pools swept by one bar on one side share a single label ("PWH · SH") instead of
//      stacking. Open pool lines are dotted and 60% transparent by default, swept ones fainter still. Both
//      the style and the transparency are inputs.
//
// --- DEFAULTS (v4.3) ---
// [D43] PDH/PDL and PWH/PWL pools OFF (swing and EQH/EQL pools stay on). Keep Mitigated OBs Visible ON.
//       Fair Value Gaps ON with Non-Repainting HTF ON. MFI levels 85 / 15 (were 90 / 10). IDX Mode ON (was
//       Auto). Signal Statistics Table OFF: switch it on when A/B testing a filter.
//
// [L1] BUG FIX, found testing on IDX:GOTO: a stock parked at the Rp 50 floor was flagged ARB on every bar,
//      because the floor-clamped ARB level equalled the previous close. ARB now requires the limit to sit
//      below the previous close.
// [L2] ARA/ARB markers rebuilt for readability: size.tiny faded labels (plotshape text cannot be resized),
//      one label per streak ("ARB×3"), merged into a sweep label on the same bar and side, MFI triangle
//      suppressed on that side of a marked bar, and only the last N markers kept (input, default 30).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG v4.2 IDX vs v4.1
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// --- PASS 7a: ACCURACY ---
// [P1] Last Opposite Candle OB: the walk-back used to start at the breaking bar, so it usually picked a small
//      pullback candle in the MIDDLE of the impulse. It now locates the leg extreme first (the origin of the
//      impulse) and walks back from a few bars after it, which is the ICT definition: the last opposing
//      candle before the displacement.
// [P3] ATR displacement is now gap-aware. The body is measured from min/max(open, close[1]) instead of open,
//      so an IDX pre-opening gap followed by a small body is no longer rejected as "no displacement".
// [P4] The volatility ATR length is an input, and it falls back to ATR(14) while ATR(n) is still na. Before
//      this, a stock with fewer than 200 bars (every recent IPO on the daily chart) detected no EQH/EQL at
//      all and rejected every break under the ATR displacement filter.
// [P5] Order block INVALIDATION now has its own input, separate from mitigation, and defaults to Close.
//      A wick through the block now only mitigates it; the block is invalidated when price CLOSES beyond it.
//      VISIBLE CHANGE: fewer order blocks disappear on opening / pre-closing spikes. Set it to High/Low to
//      restore the v4.1 behaviour.
//
// --- PASS 7b: INDONESIA STOCK EXCHANGE ---
// [IDX1] IDX tick size (fraksi harga) function. Auto-enabled on IDX symbols (syminfo.prefix == 'IDX').
//        EQH/EQL tolerance = max(threshold x ATR, N ticks); FVGs narrower than N ticks are ignored.
// [IDX2] ARA / ARB detection against the previous DAILY close, with the ARA tiers and the ARB percentage as
//        inputs. Limit bars are marked on the chart, and limit-LOCKED bars are excluded from order block
//        candle selection and from liquidity sweep piercing/rejection.
// [IDX3] Signal statistics: Long-Only mode (default on IDX) re-labels bearish rows as EXIT signals, keeps
//        them out of the ALL total and charges them no fees. Stops that gap through are filled at the open,
//        an ARB-locked stop bar is filled at the close, round-trip fees are charged in R, and entries on a
//        bar locked at the limit in the trade direction are not taken (they could not have been filled).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CHANGELOG vs v3.1
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [A1] Removed corrupted / duplicated block that started mid-expression after `p_ivot := internal ? internalLow : swingLow`.
// [A2] Undefined `isCH` replaced with `tag != CHOCH` / `tag == CHOCH`, matching the bearish branch.
// [A3] Half-written IFVG code removed (referenced 6 undeclared identifiers). Reverted to plain invalidation-delete.
// [B4] All array removals now iterate backwards, so elements are no longer skipped.
// [B5] PARTIAL -> MITIGATED is now `else if`, so a block can no longer jump ACTIVE -> MITIGATED within one bar.
// [B6] Order blocks are no longer evaluated on the bar that created them (new `creationTime` field).
// [B7] Confluence filter parenthesis fixed: `math.min(close, open) - low` (lower wick) instead of `math.min(close, open - low)`.
// [B8] EQH/EQL alerts are now actually raised.
// [B10] Order block top/bottom clamped with math.max/min, so a volatility-swapped bar can no longer invert the box.
// [B11] Duplicate STATUS_* constants removed.
// [B12] deleteOrderBlocks() merged into updateOrderBlocks(); unreachable else-branch removed.
// [B13] Swing BOS/CHoCH alert descriptions corrected (were copies of the internal ones).
// [C14] Order block status labels were a reusable pool; [T2] moved the text inside the box entirely.
// [B*]  OB alerts now fire on status transition, so they also fire when "Keep Mitigated OBs Visible" is on.
//
// --- PASS 4 ---
// [D9]  Confirmation zones now work. The main OB box no longer uses extend.right (it ends at the last bar),
//       so the ACTIVE-only projection drawn to its right is finally visible. Confirmation boxes switched to
//       xloc.bar_index, which removes the weekend/session-gap error from their width entirely.
//       VISIBLE CHANGE: order block boxes no longer stretch past the right edge of the chart.
// [D9b] Remaining right-edge projections (MTF levels, strong/weak high/low, FVG extend) now use a stable
//       bar duration instead of `time - time[1]`, which was a 3-day gap after every weekend.
// [D15] fairValueGaps is capped by a new "Max FVG Count" input; trimmed gaps have their boxes deleted.
// [D16] parsedHighs/parsedLows/highs/lows/times are capped at MAX_STORED_BARS. Pine hard-fails at 100,000
//       array elements, so a long 1m chart used to break the script outright. Trimming shifts the arrays,
//       so `arrayOffset` now maps bar_index -> array position in storeOrderBlock().
//       Charts shorter than MAX_STORED_BARS never trim and behave exactly as before.
// [D17] Unused order block boxes and stale confirmation boxes are now hidden instead of keeping their last
//       coordinates (the ghost-box issue).
// [D21] drawLevels()/higherTimeframe() parameters renamed from `timeframe` to `tf`; they were shadowing the
//       built-in `timeframe` namespace they also call into.
//
// --- PASS 5 ---
// [E20] Internal structure length is now an input instead of a hardcoded 5.
// [E18] New "Non-Repainting HTF" toggle for fair value gaps. The original read the CURRENT, still-forming
//       HTF bar through lookahead_on, so historical FVGs were drawn with information unavailable in real
//       time. The toggle shifts the whole 3-bar pattern back one HTF bar so only CONFIRMED bars are used.
//       It is a no-op when the FVG timeframe is the chart timeframe, where no leak exists.
// [E22] Displacement filter for BOS/CHoCH. A structure break with no expansion is a liquidity grab rather
//       than a shift in ICT terms. Modes: ATR body expansion, FVG created by the break, or both.
//       DEFAULT IS OFF so behaviour is unchanged until you opt in. See the note on rejection semantics
//       above displayStructure().
// [E29] OB alerts split into MITIGATED (price reached the mean threshold) and BROKEN (price traded fully
//       through). Previously a 50% fill fired an alert worded "Price broke ...", which was simply wrong.
//       ALERT TITLES CHANGED: any TradingView alert already bound to the old titles must be recreated.
//
// --- FEATURE 24: LIQUIDITY SWEEPS ---
// [F24] Equal highs/lows are now registered as liquidity pools and tracked forward. A pool is SWEPT when
//       price pierces it with a wick and closes back on the original side within a rejection window; it is
//       discarded when price closes through and holds, because that is acceptance rather than a stop hunt.
// [F24] Sweep -> CHoCH confluence: a CHoCH against the swept side, within a configurable window after the
//       sweep, raises a distinct reversal alert. This is the actual ICT setup; the sweep alone is context.
//       Internal structure is evaluated before swing structure, so an internal CHoCH claims the sweep first.
// [F24] Sweep state lives in a `sweepState` UDT rather than plain globals because Pine functions may mutate
//       object fields but may NOT reassign global scalars.
// [F24] EQH/EQL detection now runs when EITHER "Equal High/Low" or "Liquidity Sweeps" is enabled, and the
//       EQH/EQL drawing is gated separately from the pool registration.
//       KNOWN LIMIT: a pivot is confirmed `equalHighsLowsLengthInput` bars late (default 3), so a sweep that
//       happens inside that confirmation window is missed. Pools are only watched from confirmation onward.
//
// --- FEATURE 26: HIGHER TIMEFRAME BIAS ---
// [G26] A bias is read from a higher timeframe and used to filter counter-bias signals. Two methods:
//       Structure (last pivot high/low broken by close) or EMA (close against an EMA).
// [G26] IMPORTANT SEMANTIC DIFFERENCE from the displacement filter [E22]: a counter-bias break is a REAL
//       break, it simply disagrees with the higher timeframe. So structure state ALWAYS updates here --
//       the pivot is marked crossed and the trend bias flips. Only presentation and signalling are
//       filtered. Rejecting the break outright would desynchronise internal trend state from price and
//       freeze the candle colouring.
//         Dim  -> counter-bias structure drawn transparently, alerts and order blocks unaffected.
//         Hide -> counter-bias structure not drawn, its structure alert suppressed, no order block stored,
//                 and a sweep reversal on that side suppressed too.
// [G26] The bias request uses lookahead_off with NO offset and is deliberately not configurable. A bias
//       filter that leaks future data would make every historical signal look better than it was, which
//       defeats the entire point. The forming HTF bar can still change until it closes, which is normal
//       live behaviour rather than a historical leak.
// [G26] The bias is computed in global scope BETWEEN function definitions, because displayStructure()
//       reads it and Pine requires a global to be declared before the function that references it.
// [G26] Self-disables when the selected timeframe is not strictly higher than the chart timeframe, so an
//       empty or lower setting is safe rather than silently meaningless.
//
// --- FEATURE MA: MOVING AVERAGES ---
// [H-MA] Three configurable SMA/EMA lines and Kaufman's KAMA.
// [H-MA] These are independent overlays. They feed nothing else: no structure detection, no order block,
//        no filter and no alert reads them. Adding one cannot change any existing signal.
// [H-MA] kama() carries `var` state and is therefore called UNCONDITIONALLY, with the show-toggle applied
//        to the plotted value instead. Calling a stateful function only on some bars would corrupt its
//        recursion.
// [H-MA] REMOVED IN v3.9: Ehlers' MAMA/FAMA, at request. The function, its six inputs and its two plots
//        are gone. Nothing else referenced them, so nothing downstream changed.
//
// --- FEATURE REG: MARKET REGIME DETECTION ---
// [K-REG] Two orthogonal axes, deliberately not two trend measures:
//           Axis 1, persistence: Kaufman's efficiency ratio, |net move| / sum of individual moves.
//                                Near 1 the market travels in a straight line, near 0 it churns.
//           Axis 2, magnitude:   ATR compared against its own recent distribution.
//         Crossing them gives four regimes rather than a single trend/range flag:
//           Trend Expansion  - BOS continuation and OB retests are at their most reliable
//           Trend Drift      - direction persists but the move is shrinking, watch for exhaustion
//           Volatile Range   - the worst of the four, signals are least reliable here
//           Compression      - accumulation, watch EQH/EQL, expansion is being built
// [K-REG] SELF-CALIBRATING BY DESIGN. Neither axis is compared against a fixed constant. Both are turned
//         into a percentile of their own history, so the module adapts across instruments and timeframes
//         instead of carrying a magic number like ADX's 25, which was calibrated on 1970s daily commodities.
// [K-REG] HYSTERESIS is mandatory, not decoration. A single threshold makes the regime flicker on and off
//         around the boundary and become unusable. The state enters at the upper percentile and only
//         leaves at the lower one, holding its previous value in between.
//         If Exit is set at or above Enter the band collapses and this degrades to a single threshold;
//         Enter is tested first, so it wins. Nothing breaks, it just stops being hysteresis.
// [K-REG] DISPLAY ONLY, on purpose. No structure, order block, gap, sweep or filter path reads the regime.
//         The script already carries a displacement filter and an HTF bias filter, and gating is
//         MULTIPLICATIVE: three filters each passing 60% leave roughly 22% of signals. Watch the regime as
//         context first, and only wire it into filtering if the bad signals really do cluster in one
//         quadrant on your own chart.
// [K-REG] Everything is computed on the chart timeframe, so there is no request.security and no repainting
//         surface. Only the label and optional background tint are drawn.
//
// --- FEATURE MFI: MONEY FLOW INDEX EXTREMES ---
// [J-MFI] Overbought and oversold markers drawn straight onto the candles: a down triangle above the bar
//         above the overbought level, an up triangle below the bar below the oversold level. Both levels
//         are inputs, defaulting to 90 and 10.
// [J-MFI] Defaults ON, since it was asked for as an active feature. It is display and alerts only and
//         feeds nothing else, so it cannot disturb any existing signal.
// [J-MFI] "Only Mark Zone Entry" defaults OFF, which marks every bar at the extreme. That is the literal
//         reading of "MFI above 90", but in a strong trend MFI can hold beyond the level for many bars and
//         produce an unbroken run of triangles. Switch it on for one marker per excursion.
// [J-MFI] MFI is volume weighted. On a symbol with no volume, ta.mfi returns na, na comparisons are false,
//         and no markers appear. That is silent rather than an error, so check the symbol if nothing shows.
//
// --- MA DEFAULTS (v3.8) ---
// [H-MA] KAMA now defaults ON at full opacity. MA 1-3 default to EMA with 70% transparency, so when they
//        are switched on they sit behind KAMA rather than competing with it. MA 1-3 themselves remain OFF.
//
// --- FEATURE 23: ALTERNATIVE ORDER BLOCK DETECTION ---
// [I23] "OB Detection Method" input. Extreme Candle is the original behaviour and stays the default.
//       Last Opposite Candle walks back from the breaking bar to the pivot and takes the most recent candle
//       whose body opposes the break, which is the classic ICT definition.
// [I23] The two methods use different price sources on purpose. Extreme Candle keeps parsedHigh/parsedLow,
//       whose deliberate high/low swap on volatile bars exists to steer that search away from spikes.
//       Last Opposite Candle needs the candle's real body and range, so it reads raw highs/lows.
// [I23] Requires open and close history, so `opens` and `closes` join the trimmed storage arrays [D16].
// [I23] Falls back to Extreme Candle when the leg contains no opposing candle at all.
//
// --- FEATURE 25: INVERSE FAIR VALUE GAPS ---
// [I25] An invalidated gap flips role instead of vanishing: a broken bullish FVG becomes bearish, and
//       vice versa. It is recoloured, its bias is flipped, and it is only deleted if it is invalidated a
//       second time in its new direction.
// [I25] New "Invalidation" input choosing wick or close. HIGHLOW keeps the original wick behaviour as the
//       default; CLOSE is the stricter reading and the one usually wanted for IFVG, since a wick through
//       a gap is not acceptance.
// [I25] Defaults ON, which changes nothing on its own because its parent, Fair Value Gaps, is still OFF.
//
// --- PASS 6: INPUTS THAT SILENTLY DID NOTHING ---
// [A1] "Order Block Mitigation" (Close / High-Low) was only read by the legacy deletion path, while the
//      status-based system it shipped enabled-by-default hardcoded raw high/low. The input therefore had
//      no effect at all under stock settings. calculateMitigationDepth() and the zone-entry test now use
//      the same bearish/bullish mitigation sources the legacy path uses.
//      NO CHANGE on the default High/Low setting: the sources resolve to high and low, which is what was
//      hardcoded. Switching to Close now actually makes mitigation require a close inside the block,
//      which is stricter and will leave order blocks ACTIVE through wicks that used to mitigate them.
// [A2] "Auto Threshold" for fair value gaps was inert on every timeframe above the chart timeframe. The
//      mean absolute bar delta summed only over HTF-change bars but divided by bar_index, a count of
//      chart bars, so the threshold was scaled down by the ratio between the two timeframes.
//      NO CHANGE when the FVG timeframe is left empty. On a higher FVG timeframe, expect noticeably
//      fewer gaps, which is the filtering that was supposed to be happening all along.
//
// --- FEATURE STAT: SIGNAL STATISTICS ---
// [L-STAT] A table measuring how each signal type actually resolved on this chart. Every signal is tracked
//          forward from its bar close against a volatility-normalised stop and target, and reported as
//          N / Win% / expectancy / mean favourable excursion / timeout rate.
// [L-STAT] PURPOSE IS A/B TESTING. The rows count only the signals that survive the CURRENT settings, so
//          toggling the displacement filter [E22] or the HTF bias filter [G26] and comparing the numbers
//          is the intended workflow. Every filter in this script is a hypothesis; this is the only thing
//          here that can falsify one.
// [L-STAT] DISPLAY ONLY, like [K-REG] and [J-MFI]. No structure, order block, gap, sweep or filter path
//          reads it, so switching it on cannot move a single existing signal.
// [L-STAT] The measurement is deliberately pessimistic where it is ambiguous: a bar that spans both the
//          stop and the target counts as a LOSS, trades open only on confirmed bars, and the entry bar
//          itself can neither win nor lose. See the section header for the full list and the reasoning.
// [L-STAT] NOT A BACKTEST. No spread, no slippage, no sizing, no trade management, and one ATR stop for
//          every signal type rather than the structural stop an SMC trader would use. It ranks signal
//          types against each other; it does not model an account.
//
// KNOWN, NOT IMPLEMENTED (feature backlog, from the original review):
//   #27 Killzone sessions (London / New York).
//   #28 Breaker blocks from invalidated order blocks.
//   #29 alert() with dynamic messages alongside alertcondition().
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//CONSTANTS & STRINGS & INPUTS
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
BULLISH_LEG = 1
BEARISH_LEG = 0

BULLISH = +1
BEARISH = -1

// Status-Based Mitigation System Constants
ACTIVE = 0
PARTIAL = 1
MITIGATED = 2
INVALID = 3

GREEN = #089981
RED = #F23645
BLUE = #2157f3
GRAY = #878b94
MONO_BULLISH = #b2b5be
MONO_BEARISH = #5d606b

HISTORICAL = 'Historical'
PRESENT = 'Present'

COLORED = 'Colored'
MONOCHROME = 'Monochrome'

ALL = 'All'
BOS = 'BOS'
CHOCH = 'CHoCH'

TINY = size.tiny
SMALL = size.small
NORMAL = size.normal

ATR = 'Atr'
RANGE = 'Cumulative Mean Range'

// [D16] Pine throws a runtime error past 100,000 array elements. A multi-year 1m chart exceeds that,
//       which used to kill the script outright. Charts shorter than this never trim.
MAX_STORED_BARS = 50000

CLOSE = 'Close'
HIGHLOW = 'High/Low'

// [E22] displacement filter modes
DISPLACEMENT_OFF = 'Off'
DISPLACEMENT_ATR = 'ATR Expansion'
DISPLACEMENT_FVG = 'FVG Created'
DISPLACEMENT_BOTH = 'ATR + FVG'

APPLY_BOTH = 'Both'
APPLY_INTERNAL = 'Internal Only'
APPLY_SWING = 'Swing Only'

// [G26] higher timeframe bias filter
HTF_OFF = 'Off'
HTF_DIM = 'Dim Opposing'
HTF_HIDE = 'Hide Opposing'

HTF_STRUCTURE = 'Structure'
HTF_EMA = 'EMA'

// [H-MA] moving average line types
MA_SMA = 'SMA'
MA_EMA = 'EMA'

// [I23] order block detection methods
OB_EXTREME = 'Extreme Candle'
OB_LAST_OPPOSITE = 'Last Opposite Candle'
// [P1] bars after the leg extreme still searched for the origin candle
OB_ORIGIN_LOOKAHEAD = 2

SOLID = '⎯⎯⎯'
DASHED = '----'
DOTTED = '····'

SMART_GROUP = 'Smart Money Concepts'

// [T1] chart density presets
DENSITY_MINIMAL = 'Minimal'
DENSITY_STANDARD = 'Standard'
DENSITY_FULL = 'Full'
FILTER_GROUP = 'Structure Filters'
INTERNAL_GROUP = 'Real Time Internal Structure'
SWING_GROUP = 'Real Time Swing Structure'
BLOCKS_GROUP = 'Order Blocks'
EQUAL_GROUP = 'EQH/EQL'
SWEEP_GROUP = 'Liquidity Sweeps'
HTF_GROUP = 'Higher Timeframe Bias'
MA_GROUP = 'Moving Averages'
MFI_GROUP = 'Money Flow Index'
RSC_GROUP = 'Relative Strength (RSC)'
KRI_GROUP = 'Kairi Relative Index (KRI)'
CONFLUENCE_GROUP = 'Reversal Confluence'

// [KRI1] KRI zone modes
KRI_FIXED = 'Fixed %'
KRI_ADAPTIVE = 'Adaptive'

// [RS1] RSC normalisation methods
RSC_STOCH = 'Stochastic'
RSC_PERCENTILE = 'Percentile Rank'
REGIME_GROUP = 'Wyckoff Phase'
STATS_GROUP = 'Signal Statistics'
IDX_GROUP = 'Indonesia (IDX)'

// [IDX1] market mode
IDX_AUTO = 'Auto'
IDX_ON = 'On'
IDX_OFF = 'Off'

// [W1] the four Wyckoff phases (replace the v4.3 persistence x volatility quadrants)
WY_ACCUMULATION = 'Accumulation'
WY_MARKUP = 'Markup'
WY_DISTRIBUTION = 'Distribution'
WY_MARKDOWN = 'Markdown'
GAPS_GROUP = 'Fair Value Gaps'
LEVELS_GROUP = 'Highs & Lows MTF'
ZONES_GROUP = 'Premium & Discount Zones'

modeTooltip = 'Allows to display historical Structure or only the recent ones'
styleTooltip = 'Indicator color theme'
showTrendTooltip = 'Display additional candles with a color reflecting the current trend detected by structure'
showInternalsTooltip = 'Display internal market structure'
internalFilterConfluenceTooltip = 'Filter non significant internal structure breakouts'
showStructureTooltip = 'Display swing market Structure'
showSwingsTooltip = 'Display swing point as labels on the chart'
showHighLowSwingsTooltip = 'Highlight most recent strong and weak high/low points on the chart'
showInternalOrderBlocksTooltip = 'Display internal order blocks on the chart\n\nNumber of internal order blocks to display on the chart'
showSwingOrderBlocksTooltip = 'Display swing order blocks on the chart\n\nNumber of internal swing blocks to display on the chart'
orderBlockFilterTooltip = 'Method used to filter out volatile order blocks \n\nIt is recommended to use the cumulative mean range method when a low amount of data is available'
orderBlockMitigationTooltip = 'Select what values to use for order block mitigation'
showEqualHighsLowsTooltip = 'Display equal highs and equal lows on the chart'
equalHighsLowsLengthTooltip = 'Number of bars used to confirm equal highs and equal lows'
equalHighsLowsThresholdTooltip = 'Sensitivity threshold in a range (0, 1) used for the detection of equal highs & lows\n\nLower values will return fewer but more pertinent results'
showFairValueGapsTooltip = 'Display fair values gaps on the chart'
fairValueGapsThresholdTooltip = 'Filter out non significant fair value gaps'
fairValueGapsTimeframeTooltip = 'Fair value gaps timeframe'
fairValueGapsExtendTooltip = 'Determine how many bars to extend the Fair Value Gap boxes on chart'
showPremiumDiscountZonesTooltip = 'Display premium, discount, and equilibrium zones on chart'

modeInput = input.string(HISTORICAL, 'Mode', group = SMART_GROUP, tooltip = modeTooltip, options = [HISTORICAL, PRESENT])
styleInput = input.string(COLORED, 'Style', group = SMART_GROUP, tooltip = styleTooltip, options = [COLORED, MONOCHROME])
showTrendInput = input(false, 'Color Candles', group = SMART_GROUP, tooltip = showTrendTooltip)
// [T1] one switch for how much the chart draws, on top of the individual toggles
densityInput = input.string(DENSITY_STANDARD, 'Chart Density', options = [DENSITY_MINIMAL, DENSITY_STANDARD, DENSITY_FULL], group = SMART_GROUP, tooltip = 'How much is drawn, applied ON TOP of the individual switches: it can only take things away, never add them.\n\nMinimal: swing structure, order blocks, sweeps and the candle markers only. Internal structure drawings, EQH/EQL drawings, confirmation zones, order block status text, Wyckoff range boxes, session shading and the statistics table are hidden. Everything still CALCULATES, so alerts and statistics are unaffected.\n\nStandard: your own switches decide (default).\n\nFull: same as Standard; kept as an explicit label.')

// [E22] Displacement filter. Default Off, so enabling it is an explicit A/B decision.
displacementFilterInput = input.string(DISPLACEMENT_OFF, 'Displacement Filter', group = FILTER_GROUP, options = [DISPLACEMENT_OFF, DISPLACEMENT_ATR, DISPLACEMENT_FVG, DISPLACEMENT_BOTH], tooltip = 'Requires a structure break to be accompanied by displacement.\n\nOff: every close beyond the level counts (original behaviour).\nATR Expansion: the breaking candle body must exceed Factor x ATR(200).\nFVG Created: the breaking candle must leave a 3-bar fair value gap.\nATR + FVG: both.\n\nA rejected break is discarded, and the level stays available for a later break that does qualify.')
displacementFactorInput = input.float(1.0, 'ATR Expansion Factor', group = FILTER_GROUP, minval = 0.1, maxval = 5.0, step = 0.1, tooltip = 'Minimum breaking-candle body as a multiple of ATR(200). Only used by the ATR modes.')
displacementApplyToInput = input.string(APPLY_BOTH, 'Apply Filter To', group = FILTER_GROUP, options = [APPLY_BOTH, APPLY_INTERNAL, APPLY_SWING], tooltip = 'Internal structure is the noisier of the two and usually benefits most from filtering.')
// [P4] was a hardcoded ATR(200)
volatilityAtrLengthInput = input.int(200, 'Volatility ATR Length', group = FILTER_GROUP, minval = 14, maxval = 500, tooltip = 'ATR used by the displacement filter, the EQH/EQL tolerance and the volatile-bar filter of order blocks.\n\n200 is the original value. On an IDX daily chart 50 adapts faster to a change in character. While this ATR is not yet available (a recent IPO), ATR(14) is used instead.')
// [C3] strict CHoCH, applied to both internal and swing structure
strictChochInput = input.bool(false, 'Strict CHoCH (Protected Level)', group = FILTER_GROUP, tooltip = 'Off: a CHoCH is any close through the most recent opposing pivot (original behaviour).\n\nOn: a CHoCH must close through the PROTECTED level: in a downtrend the highest high of the leg that produced the last bearish break, in an uptrend the lowest low of the leg that produced the last bullish break. Closing through a minor pivot short of that level is consumed silently: no label, no alert, no order block, and the trend does not flip.\n\nApplies to internal and swing structure. Compare the CHoCH rows of the statistics table with it on and off.')

// [G26] Higher timeframe bias. Default Off, like the displacement filter, because it suppresses signals.
htfBiasFilterInput = input.string(HTF_OFF, 'HTF Bias Filter', group = HTF_GROUP, options = [HTF_OFF, HTF_DIM, HTF_HIDE], tooltip = 'Filters structure that disagrees with a higher timeframe.\n\nDim: counter-bias structure is drawn transparently. Alerts and order blocks are untouched, so nothing is lost, it is only de-emphasised.\nHide: counter-bias structure is not drawn, its alert is suppressed, no order block is stored from it, and a sweep reversal on that side is suppressed.\n\nEither way the break itself still registers internally: the trend state stays in sync with price, only the presentation and the signalling are filtered.')
// [R2] default was '240', which straddles the IDX lunch break and disabled itself on a daily chart
htfBiasTimeframeInput = input.timeframe('W', 'Bias Timeframe', group = HTF_GROUP, tooltip = 'Must be strictly higher than the chart timeframe. The filter disables itself otherwise, so leaving this empty or setting it too low is safe rather than silently meaningless.\n\nIDX: W for a daily chart, D for intraday. Only CONFIRMED higher timeframe bars are used, so the bias flips one HTF bar after the HTF close that caused it, identically on history and live.')
htfBiasMethodInput = input.string(HTF_STRUCTURE, 'Bias Method', group = HTF_GROUP, options = [HTF_STRUCTURE, HTF_EMA], tooltip = 'Structure: bias flips when a close breaks the most recent higher timeframe pivot high or low. Closer to how the rest of this indicator thinks, and slower to flip.\n\nEMA: bias is simply close against an EMA. Smoother and more responsive, but has no structural meaning.')
htfBiasLengthInput = input.int(10, 'Bias Length', group = HTF_GROUP, minval = 2, maxval = 200, tooltip = 'Pivot lookback for the Structure method, EMA length for the EMA method.')
htfBiasDimOpacityInput = input.int(70, 'Dim Opacity', group = HTF_GROUP, minval = 0, maxval = 100, tooltip = 'Transparency applied to counter-bias structure in Dim mode.')
showHTFBiasLabelInput = input.bool(true, 'Show Bias Label', group = HTF_GROUP, tooltip = 'One label at the right edge showing the current higher timeframe bias.')

showInternalsInput = input(true, 'Show Internal Structure', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, tooltip = showInternalsTooltip)
// [E20] was hardcoded to 5 in the execution block
internalStructureLengthInput = input.int(5, 'Internal Structure Length', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, minval = 2, maxval = 50, tooltip = 'Pivot lookback used to detect internal structure. Keep it well below the swing length, otherwise internal and swing structure collapse onto each other and the internal filter that compares the two stops doing anything.')
showInternalBullInput = input.string(ALL, 'Bullish Structure', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, inline = 'ibull', options = [ALL, BOS, CHOCH])
internalBullColorInput = input(GREEN, '', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, inline = 'ibull')
showInternalBearInput = input.string(ALL, 'Bearish Structure', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, inline = 'ibear', options = [ALL, BOS, CHOCH])
internalBearColorInput = input(RED, '', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, inline = 'ibear')
internalFilterConfluenceInput = input(false, 'Confluence Filter', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, tooltip = internalFilterConfluenceTooltip)
internalStructureSize = input.string(TINY, 'Internal Label Size', group = INTERNAL_GROUP, options = [TINY, SMALL, NORMAL])

showStructureInput = input(true, 'Show Swing Structure', group = SWING_GROUP, tooltip = showStructureTooltip)
showSwingBullInput = input.string(ALL, 'Bullish Structure', group = SWING_GROUP, inline = 'bull', options = [ALL, BOS, CHOCH])
swingBullColorInput = input(GREEN, '', group = SWING_GROUP, inline = 'bull')
showSwingBearInput = input.string(ALL, 'Bearish Structure', group = SWING_GROUP, inline = 'bear', options = [ALL, BOS, CHOCH])
swingBearColorInput = input(RED, '', group = SWING_GROUP, inline = 'bear')
swingStructureSize = input.string(SMALL, 'Swing Label Size', group = SWING_GROUP, options = [TINY, SMALL, NORMAL])
showSwingsInput = input(false, 'Show Swings Points', group = SWING_GROUP, tooltip = showSwingsTooltip, inline = 'swings')
swingsLengthInput = input.int(50, '', group = SWING_GROUP, minval = 10, inline = 'swings')
showHighLowSwingsInput = input(true, 'Show Strong/Weak High/Low', group = SWING_GROUP, tooltip = showHighLowSwingsTooltip)

showInternalOrderBlocksInput = input(true, 'Internal Order Blocks', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = showInternalOrderBlocksTooltip, inline = 'iob')
internalOrderBlocksSizeInput = input.int(5, '', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, minval = 1, maxval = 20, inline = 'iob')
showSwingOrderBlocksInput = input(false, 'Swing Order Blocks', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = showSwingOrderBlocksTooltip, inline = 'ob')
swingOrderBlocksSizeInput = input.int(5, '', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, minval = 1, maxval = 20, inline = 'ob')
orderBlockFilterInput = input.string('Atr', 'Order Block Filter', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = orderBlockFilterTooltip, options = [ATR, RANGE])
orderBlockMitigationInput = input.string(HIGHLOW, 'Order Block Mitigation', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = orderBlockMitigationTooltip, options = [CLOSE, HIGHLOW])
// [P5] invalidation is now separate from mitigation. Close is the ICT reading: a wick into or through a block
//      is mitigation, only a close beyond it is acceptance.
orderBlockInvalidationInput = input.string(CLOSE, 'Order Block Invalidation', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, options = [CLOSE, HIGHLOW], tooltip = 'What removes an order block as INVALID.\n\nClose: price must close beyond the far edge of the block. A wick through it only mitigates it. Recommended, especially on IDX where opening and pre-closing auctions print long wicks.\n\nHigh/Low: a wick beyond the far edge is enough (v4.1 behaviour).')
// [I23] Extreme Candle is the original behaviour, kept as the default so nothing shifts unasked
obDetectionInput = input.string(OB_EXTREME, 'OB Detection Method', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, options = [OB_EXTREME, OB_LAST_OPPOSITE], tooltip = 'How the order block candle is picked out of the leg between the pivot and the break.\n\nExtreme Candle: the highest high (bearish) or lowest low (bullish) in the leg. Volatile bars are steered around by the same high/low swap the volatility filter uses.\n\nLast Opposite Candle: the last candle whose body opposes the break at the ORIGIN of the impulse, found by walking back from just after the leg extreme. This is the classic ICT reading and uses the real candle range rather than the filtered one. Falls back to Extreme Candle if the leg has no opposing candle.\n\nThe two often disagree. Compare them on your own chart rather than assuming either is better.')
// [C2] premium/discount placement filter
obPremiumDiscountFilterInput = input.bool(false, 'Premium/Discount Filter', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Only store a bullish order block when its midpoint is at or below the equilibrium of the current swing range (discount), and a bearish one when it is at or above it (premium).\n\nThe range is the trailing swing high and low, the same one the Premium & Discount zones draw. Off by default: like every filter here it is a hypothesis, so compare the OB Retest rows of the statistics table with it on and off.')
partialMitigationThresholdInput = input.float(0.5, 'Partial Mitigation Threshold', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Threshold (0-1) for partial mitigation status', minval = 0.1, maxval = 0.9, step = 0.1)
enableStatusBasedSystemInput = input.bool(true, 'Enable Status-Based System', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Enable advanced status tracking instead of deletion-based system')
showConfirmationZonesInput = input.bool(true, 'Show Confirmation Zones', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Show extended zones for untouched Order Blocks')
confirmationZoneExtensionInput = input.int(50, 'Confirmation Zone Extension', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Number of bars to extend confirmation zones', minval = 10, maxval = 200)
confirmationZoneOpacityInput = input.int(85, 'Confirmation Zone Opacity', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Opacity for confirmation zones (0-100)', minval = 0, maxval = 100)
keepMitigatedOBInput = input.bool(true, 'Keep Mitigated OBs Visible', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Show mitigated order blocks with reduced opacity')
showOBStatusLabelsInput = input.bool(true, 'Show OB Status Labels', group = BLOCKS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Show status labels on order blocks')
internalBullishOrderBlockColor = input.color(color.new(#3179f5, 80), 'Internal Bullish OB', group = BLOCKS_GROUP)
internalBearishOrderBlockColor = input.color(color.new(#f77c80, 80), 'Internal Bearish OB', group = BLOCKS_GROUP)
swingBullishOrderBlockColor = input.color(color.new(#1848cc, 80), 'Bullish OB', group = BLOCKS_GROUP)
swingBearishOrderBlockColor = input.color(color.new(#b22833, 80), 'Bearish OB', group = BLOCKS_GROUP)

showEqualHighsLowsInput = input(true, 'Equal High/Low', group = EQUAL_GROUP, tooltip = showEqualHighsLowsTooltip)
equalHighsLowsLengthInput = input.int(3, 'Bars Confirmation', group = EQUAL_GROUP, tooltip = equalHighsLowsLengthTooltip, minval = 1)
equalHighsLowsThresholdInput = input.float(0.1, 'Threshold', group = EQUAL_GROUP, tooltip = equalHighsLowsThresholdTooltip, minval = 0, maxval = 0.5, step = 0.1)
equalHighsLowsSizeInput = input.string(TINY, 'Label Size', group = EQUAL_GROUP, options = [TINY, SMALL, NORMAL])

// [F24] Liquidity sweeps. Purely additive drawings, so this defaults on, unlike the displacement filter
//       which suppresses existing signals and therefore defaults off.
showLiquiditySweepsInput = input.bool(true, 'Track Liquidity Sweeps', group = SWEEP_GROUP, tooltip = 'Treats each equal high / equal low as a resting liquidity pool and watches whether price raids it.\n\nA pool is SWEPT when price wicks through and closes back on the original side. A pool that price closes through and holds beyond is discarded, because that is acceptance rather than a stop hunt.\n\nRequires equal high/low detection, which this option enables on its own if the EQH/EQL section is switched off.')
// [C1] pool sources. EQH/EQL are always a source whenever sweeps are tracked.
sweepSwingPoolsInput = input.bool(true, 'Swing Highs/Lows', group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'poolsrc1', tooltip = 'Additional liquidity pools besides equal highs/lows.\n\nSwing: every confirmed swing pivot (Swing Structure length).\nPDH/PDL: previous day high/low, intraday charts only.\nPWH/PWL: previous week high/low, daily and intraday charts.')
sweepDailyPoolsInput = input.bool(false, 'PDH/PDL', group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'poolsrc1')
sweepWeeklyPoolsInput = input.bool(false, 'PWH/PWL', group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'poolsrc1')
liquiditySweepMaxInput = input.int(20, 'Max Tracked Pools', group = SWEEP_GROUP, minval = 1, maxval = 50, tooltip = 'Oldest pools are dropped first, along with their drawings. Shared by every pool source.')
sweepWindowInput = input.int(2, 'Rejection Window (bars)', group = SWEEP_GROUP, minval = 0, tooltip = 'How many bars price has to close back on the original side after piercing a pool.\n\n0 requires the rejection on the piercing bar itself, which is the strictest reading. Larger values accept slower reversals but also let more genuine breakouts be mislabelled as sweeps.')
sweepReversalWindowInput = input.int(10, 'Reversal Confluence Window (bars)', group = SWEEP_GROUP, minval = 1, tooltip = 'A CHoCH against the swept side within this many bars of a sweep raises the "Sweep Reversal" alert. This pairing is the actual setup; a sweep on its own is only context.')
showSweepLabelsInput = input.bool(true, 'Show Sweep Labels', group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'sweeplbl')
// [V1] sweep label and pool line appearance
sweepLabelStyleInput = input.string('Compact', '', options = ['Compact', 'Full'], group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'sweeplbl', tooltip = 'Compact: source only (PWH, PDL, SH, SL, BSL, SSL), faded, placed just beyond the sweeping wick instead of on it. Hover the label for details.\n\nFull: "PWH Sweep" style text.\n\nIn both styles, several pools swept by the same bar on the same side share ONE label, e.g. "PWH · SH".')
poolLineStyleInput = input.string(DOTTED, 'Pool Lines', options = [SOLID, DASHED, DOTTED], group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'poolline')
poolLineTransparencyInput = input.int(60, 'Transparency', minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'poolline', tooltip = 'Transparency of pools still waiting to be swept. Swept pools are drawn fainter still, and sweep labels use the same fading.')
liquidityBuysideColorInput = input.color(RED, 'Buyside Pool (EQH)', group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'sweepcol')
liquiditySellsideColorInput = input.color(GREEN, 'Sellside Pool (EQL)', group = SWEEP_GROUP, inline = 'sweepcol')

showFairValueGapsInput = input(true, 'Fair Value Gaps', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = showFairValueGapsTooltip)
fairValueGapsThresholdInput = input(true, 'Auto Threshold', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = fairValueGapsThresholdTooltip)
fairValueGapsTimeframeInput = input.timeframe('', 'Timeframe', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = fairValueGapsTimeframeTooltip)
// [E18] guards against the lookahead leak on higher timeframes
fairValueGapsNonRepaintingInput = input.bool(true, 'Non-Repainting HTF', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = 'When the FVG timeframe is higher than the chart timeframe, the original logic reads the current, still-forming HTF bar. Historical gaps therefore appear with information that was not available in real time.\n\nEnabling this uses only confirmed HTF bars, so gaps appear one HTF bar later but never repaint.\n\nHas no effect when the FVG timeframe is left empty (chart timeframe), where no leak exists.')
fairValueGapsBullColorInput = input.color(color.new(#00ff68, 70), 'Bullish FVG', group = GAPS_GROUP)
fairValueGapsBearColorInput = input.color(color.new(#ff0008, 70), 'Bearish FVG', group = GAPS_GROUP)
fairValueGapsExtendInput = input.int(1, 'Extend FVG', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = fairValueGapsExtendTooltip, minval = 0)
// [D15] the fairValueGaps array used to grow without limit, eventually outliving the 500 box budget
fairValueGapsMaxInput = input.int(20, 'Max FVG Count', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Maximum number of unmitigated fair value gaps kept on the chart. Oldest gaps are discarded first.', minval = 1, maxval = 100)
// [I25] inverse fair value gaps
fairValueGapsInvalidationInput = input.string(HIGHLOW, 'Invalidation', group = GAPS_GROUP, options = [CLOSE, HIGHLOW], tooltip = 'What counts as trading through a gap.\n\nHigh/Low: a wick through the gap is enough. This is the original behaviour and the default.\nClose: the bar must close through it. Stricter, and the usual reading for inverse gaps, since a wick is not acceptance.')
showIFVGInput = input.bool(true, 'Inverse FVG (IFVG)', group = GAPS_GROUP, tooltip = 'When a gap is traded through it flips role rather than disappearing: a broken bullish gap becomes bearish resistance, a broken bearish gap becomes bullish support. The zone is recoloured and only removed if it is invalidated a second time in its new direction.\n\nThis has no effect while Fair Value Gaps itself is switched off.')
ifvgBullishColorInput = input.color(color.new(#00ff68, 55), 'Bullish IFVG', group = GAPS_GROUP)
ifvgBearishColorInput = input.color(color.new(#ff0008, 55), 'Bearish IFVG', group = GAPS_GROUP)

showDailyLevelsInput = input(false, 'Daily', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'daily')
dailyLevelsStyleInput = input.string(SOLID, '', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'daily', options = [SOLID, DASHED, DOTTED])
dailyLevelsColorInput = input(BLUE, '', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'daily')
showWeeklyLevelsInput = input(false, 'Weekly', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'weekly')
weeklyLevelsStyleInput = input.string(SOLID, '', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'weekly', options = [SOLID, DASHED, DOTTED])
weeklyLevelsColorInput = input(BLUE, '', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'weekly')
showMonthlyLevelsInput = input(false, 'Monthly', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'monthly')
monthlyLevelsStyleInput = input.string(SOLID, '', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'monthly', options = [SOLID, DASHED, DOTTED])
monthlyLevelsColorInput = input(BLUE, '', group = LEVELS_GROUP, inline = 'monthly')

showPremiumDiscountZonesInput = input(false, 'Premium/Discount Zones', group = ZONES_GROUP, tooltip = showPremiumDiscountZonesTooltip)
premiumZoneColorInput = input.color(RED, 'Premium Zone', group = ZONES_GROUP)
equilibriumZoneColorInput = input.color(GRAY, 'Equilibrium Zone', group = ZONES_GROUP)
discountZoneColorInput = input.color(GREEN, 'Discount Zone', group = ZONES_GROUP)

// [H-MA] Moving averages. Every one of these defaults to OFF: the indicator is unchanged until enabled.
//        Each row is: enable / type / length / colour.
maSourceInput = input.source(close, 'Source', group = MA_GROUP, tooltip = 'Shared by the three SMA/EMA lines and by KAMA.')

showMA1Input = input.bool(false, 'MA 1', group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma1')
ma1TypeInput = input.string(MA_EMA, '', options = [MA_SMA, MA_EMA], group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma1')
ma1LengthInput = input.int(20, '', minval = 1, maxval = 5000, group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma1')
ma1ColorInput = input.color(color.new(#2962ff, 70), '', group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma1')

showMA2Input = input.bool(false, 'MA 2', group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma2')
ma2TypeInput = input.string(MA_EMA, '', options = [MA_SMA, MA_EMA], group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma2')
ma2LengthInput = input.int(50, '', minval = 1, maxval = 5000, group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma2')
ma2ColorInput = input.color(color.new(#ff9800, 70), '', group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma2')

showMA3Input = input.bool(false, 'MA 3', group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma3')
ma3TypeInput = input.string(MA_EMA, '', options = [MA_SMA, MA_EMA], group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma3')
ma3LengthInput = input.int(200, '', minval = 1, maxval = 5000, group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma3')
ma3ColorInput = input.color(color.new(#e91e63, 70), '', group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'ma3')

showKamaInput = input.bool(true, 'KAMA', group = MA_GROUP, tooltip = "Kaufman's Adaptive Moving Average.\n\nThe efficiency ratio compares net movement against total movement over the lookback, then scales the smoothing between the fast and slow bounds. It tracks price closely in a clean trend and flattens out in chop.")
kamaLengthInput = input.int(10, 'KAMA Efficiency Length', minval = 1, maxval = 500, group = MA_GROUP)
kamaFastInput = input.int(2, 'KAMA Fast', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'kamafs')
kamaSlowInput = input.int(30, 'KAMA Slow', minval = 1, maxval = 500, group = MA_GROUP, inline = 'kamafs')
kamaColorInput = input.color(color.new(#ffeb3b, 0), 'KAMA', group = MA_GROUP)

// [J-MFI] Money Flow Index extremes, marked directly on the candles.
showMfiSignalsInput = input.bool(true, 'MFI Overbought / Oversold Signals', group = MFI_GROUP, tooltip = 'Marks bars where the Money Flow Index sits at an extreme: a down triangle above the bar when overbought, an up triangle below the bar when oversold.\n\nMFI is volume weighted, so it returns nothing on symbols that carry no volume data. The markers simply will not appear there rather than erroring.')
mfiLengthInput = input.int(14, 'MFI Length', minval = 1, maxval = 500, group = MFI_GROUP)
mfiSourceInput = input.source(hlc3, 'MFI Source', group = MFI_GROUP, tooltip = 'Typical price (hlc3) is the standard input for money flow.')
mfiOverboughtInput = input.float(85, 'Overbought Above', minval = 50, maxval = 100, step = 1, group = MFI_GROUP, inline = 'mfilvl')
mfiOversoldInput = input.float(15, 'Oversold Below', minval = 0, maxval = 50, step = 1, group = MFI_GROUP, inline = 'mfilvl')
mfiOverboughtColorInput = input.color(RED, 'Overbought', group = MFI_GROUP, inline = 'mficol')
mfiOversoldColorInput = input.color(GREEN, 'Oversold', group = MFI_GROUP, inline = 'mficol')
mfiOnlyOnEntryInput = input.bool(false, 'Only Mark Zone Entry', group = MFI_GROUP, tooltip = 'Off: every bar sitting at the extreme is marked. This is the literal reading of "MFI above 90" and the default.\n\nOn: only the first bar of each excursion is marked. Much cleaner in strong trends, where MFI can hold beyond the level for many bars and produce a solid run of triangles.')

// [RS1] Relative Strength Comparison against a benchmark. Display and alerts only.
showRscInput = input.bool(true, 'Relative Strength Comparison', group = RSC_GROUP, tooltip = 'RSC compares this stock with a benchmark: the ratio close / benchmark close. A rising ratio means the stock is outperforming the benchmark, a falling one that it is lagging, regardless of whether the market itself is up or down.\n\nThe raw ratio has no fixed scale, so it is normalised to 0-100 against its own recent history (see Normalisation), which is what makes the 80 / 20 levels meaningful on any stock.\n\nA small dot ABOVE the candle marks strong relative strength (RSC above the upper level), a dot BELOW the candle weak relative strength (RSC below the lower level). The value itself is shown in the Data Window.')
rscBenchmarkInput = input.symbol('IDX:LQ45', 'Benchmark', group = RSC_GROUP, tooltip = 'IDX LQ45 by default. IDX:COMPOSITE (IHSG) or a sector index also work.')
rscMethodInput = input.string(RSC_STOCH, 'Normalisation', options = [RSC_STOCH, RSC_PERCENTILE], group = RSC_GROUP, tooltip = 'Stochastic: where the ratio sits between its lowest and highest value of the lookback. 100 = the strongest the stock has been relative to the benchmark in that window.\n\nPercentile Rank: the share of lookback bars on which the ratio was lower than now. Less sensitive to a single extreme bar.')
rscLengthInput = input.int(50, 'Lookback', minval = 5, maxval = 500, group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsclen', tooltip = '50 bars is roughly one quarter on a daily chart. Smoothing is a simple moving average of the normalised value.')
rscSmoothInput = input.int(3, 'Smoothing', minval = 1, maxval = 20, group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsclen')
rscUpperInput = input.float(80, 'Strong Above', minval = 50, maxval = 100, step = 1, group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsclvl')
rscLowerInput = input.float(20, 'Weak Below', minval = 0, maxval = 50, step = 1, group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsclvl')
rscStrongColorInput = input.color(#2962ff, 'Strong', group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsccol')
rscWeakColorInput = input.color(#ff6d00, 'Weak', group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsccol')
rscOnlyOnEntryInput = input.bool(false, 'Alert On Zone Entry Only', group = RSC_GROUP, tooltip = 'Applies to the RSC alerts only. The zone curve is always drawn for the whole excursion.\n\nOff: the alert condition is true on every bar beyond the level.\n\nOn: only on the first bar of each excursion.')
// [T3] RSC as a curve rather than a run of dots
rscCurveInput = input.bool(true, 'Draw RSC Zone Curve', group = RSC_GROUP, tooltip = 'Relative strength is a STATE that lasts for weeks, not an event on one bar, so a run of per-bar dots says the same thing over and over.\n\nOn (default): a thin dotted curve is drawn ONLY while RSC is in a zone, and it takes a side:\n- ABOVE the candles while RSC is above the strong level, following the shape of the highs\n- BELOW the candles while RSC is under the weak level, following the lows\n\nNothing is drawn in between, so silence means "no extreme". Nothing is added per bar either, and the candles are never crossed.')
rscCurveLengthInput = input.int(20, 'Curve Length', minval = 2, maxval = 200, group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsccurve', tooltip = 'EMA length of the highs / lows the curve follows. 20 tracks the shape of the candles closely; longer is smoother.\n\nOffset is the clearance from the wick, as a multiple of ATR(14).')
rscCurveOffsetInput = input.float(3.0, 'Offset', minval = 0.1, maxval = 10.0, step = 0.1, group = RSC_GROUP, inline = 'rsccurve')
rscCurveSmoothInput = input.int(5, 'Curve Smoothing', minval = 1, maxval = 50, group = RSC_GROUP, tooltip = 'Second smoothing pass over the curve. The first pass follows the highs / lows, this one takes the kinks out of it so the dotted line reads as one calm arc rather than a jagged copy of the wicks. 1 disables it.')

// [KRI1] Kairi Relative Index. Display and alert() only.
showKriInput = input.bool(true, 'Kairi Relative Index (KRI)', group = KRI_GROUP, tooltip = 'KRI = (close - moving average) / moving average x 100: how far, in percent, price has stretched away from its average. A large positive KRI is overextended to the upside (overbought), a large negative one to the downside (oversold).\n\n"Flop" (above the candle) marks the bar KRI ENTERS the overbought zone, "Flip" (below the candle) the bar it enters the oversold zone. One label per excursion, not one per bar. Hover a label for the KRI value.\n\nAlerts use alert(): create an alert on this indicator and choose "Any alert() function call". The script is at Pine\'s 64-plot limit, so no alertcondition could be added.')
kriLengthInput = input.int(25, 'MA Length', minval = 2, maxval = 500, group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'krima', tooltip = '25 is the classic Japanese "25-day kairi" for stocks.')
kriMaTypeInput = input.string(MA_SMA, '', options = [MA_SMA, MA_EMA], group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'krima')
kriModeInput = input.string(KRI_FIXED, 'Zones', options = [KRI_FIXED, KRI_ADAPTIVE], group = KRI_GROUP, tooltip = 'Fixed: overbought / oversold at the percentages below.\n\nAdaptive: the zones are this stock\'s own extreme KRI readings, the upper and lower percentile of KRI over the lookback. Useful because a quiet blue chip rarely stretches 10% from its average while a small cap does it every month.')
kriOverboughtInput = input.float(10, 'Overbought Above %', minval = 0.5, maxval = 100, step = 0.5, group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'krifix')
kriOversoldInput = input.float(-10, 'Oversold Below %', minval = -100, maxval = -0.5, step = 0.5, group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'krifix')
kriPercentileInput = input.float(95, 'Adaptive Percentile', minval = 80, maxval = 99.5, step = 0.5, group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'kriadp', tooltip = '95 = overbought is the top 5% of KRI readings and oversold the bottom 5%, over the lookback.')
kriAdaptiveLookbackInput = input.int(250, 'Lookback', minval = 50, maxval = 2000, group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'kriadp')
kriFlopColorInput = input.color(#e91e63, 'Flop', group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'kricol')
kriFlipColorInput = input.color(#00897b, 'Flip', group = KRI_GROUP, inline = 'kricol')
kriMaxLabelsInput = input.int(40, 'Keep Last Labels', minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = KRI_GROUP, tooltip = 'Older Flip / Flop labels are removed so they cannot crowd structure labels out of the 500-label budget.')

// [KRI1] one spacing for all three candle markers, so they always stack in the same order at any zoom
markerSpacingInput = input.float(0.35, 'Marker Spacing (x ATR)', minval = 0.1, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.05, group = KRI_GROUP, tooltip = 'Distance between the stacked candle markers, as a multiple of ATR(14). From the wick outwards: MFI triangle, RSC dot, KRI Flip / Flop label.')

// [B1] Reversal confluence. The whole point of this block is to fire RARELY: MFI, RSC and KRI each say
//      something different about exhaustion, and a reversal that only one of them sees is usually noise.
showConfluenceInput = input.bool(true, 'Reversal Confluence Signal', group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, tooltip = 'A composite reversal signal built from what is already on the chart. It fires only when ALL of the following hold on the same bar:\n\n1. CONTEXT: the market is in the right half of its range for the trade (or in the matching Wyckoff phase).\n2. SCORE: enough recent evidence of exhaustion, see the votes below.\n3. TRIGGER: an internal or swing CHoCH in the reversal direction.\n\nVotes (each within the recency window):\n  +1 KRI at its extreme (price stretched from its average)\n  +1 MFI at its extreme (money flow exhausted)\n  +1 volume climax (heavy volume closing against the move)\n  +2 DIVERGENCE: price made a lower low / higher high while MFI or RSC did not\n  +1 liquidity taken: a sweep of the opposite side, or a Wyckoff spring / upthrust\n\nDivergence is weighted double because it is the only vote that compares two swings instead of judging one bar.\n\nThis is measured in the statistics table like every other signal type: switch the table on and compare its row against CHoCH and OB Retest before trusting it.')
confluenceThresholdInput = input.int(3, 'Minimum Score', minval = 1, maxval = 7, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confscore', tooltip = 'Score needed besides the context and the trigger. Filters multiply: every vote you demand cuts the number of signals, so raise this only if the statistics row says the survivors are better.\n\nWindow: how many bars back a vote still counts.')
confluenceWindowInput = input.int(10, 'Window', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confscore')
confluenceContextInput = input.bool(true, 'Require Context', group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, tooltip = 'On: a bullish signal needs price in the lower half of the long-term range or a Wyckoff accumulation phase, and the mirror for bearish. This is what stops the script calling a bottom at the top of a range.')
divPivotLengthInput = input.int(5, 'Divergence Pivot Length', minval = 2, maxval = 50, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confdiv', tooltip = 'Bars either side of a swing point. A divergence is only recognised once the pivot is confirmed, so it always arrives this many bars late, which is the honest cost of using confirmed swings.\n\nMax Gap: how far apart the two compared swings may be.')
divMaxGapInput = input.int(60, 'Max Gap', minval = 5, maxval = 400, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confdiv')
climaxVolumeMultInput = input.float(2.0, 'Climax Volume x Average', minval = 1.1, maxval = 10.0, step = 0.1, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confvol', tooltip = 'A climax bar is heavy volume that closes AGAINST the move it came from: a selling climax trades a lot and closes in the upper part of its range. Symbols without volume simply never score this vote.')
climaxLookbackInput = input.int(20, 'Average Over', minval = 5, maxval = 200, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confvol')
confluenceBullColorInput = input.color(#00897b, 'Bullish', group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confcol')
confluenceBearColorInput = input.color(#d81b60, 'Bearish', group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confcol')
confluenceMaxLabelsInput = input.int(40, 'Keep Last Labels', minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP)
// [B2] RSC votes on direction, not level
rscSlopeLengthInput = input.int(10, 'RSC Slope Window', minval = 2, maxval = 100, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confrsc', tooltip = 'A leading stock sits at RSC 100 for weeks, so the LEVEL stops being information in a trend, and reading it as "overbought" is how a trend manufactures false reversals.\n\nThe vote therefore looks at the TURN: how much RSC has changed over this many bars, in RSC points. Relative strength improving while price is still falling is early accumulation; the mirror is early distribution.\n\nThreshold: how many points of change are needed. Lower = more votes.')
rscSlopeThresholdInput = input.float(5, 'Threshold', minval = 0, maxval = 50, step = 1, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'confrsc')
// [B3] zone reset
confluenceZoneResetInput = input.int(5, 'Zone Reset (bars)', minval = 0, maxval = 100, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, tooltip = 'An oscillator that stays in its zone for twenty bars used to hand out a free vote on every one of them, which is what happens in a strong trend.\n\nA zone now counts only on the bar it is ENTERED, and only after it has been OUTSIDE for at least this many bars. One excursion, one vote.\n\n0 restores the old behaviour of every entry counting, however brief the exit.')
// [B4] trend regime gate
confluenceTrendGateInput = input.int(80, 'Strong Trend Above Percentile', minval = 50, maxval = 100, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'conftrend', tooltip = 'Uses the efficiency ratio percentile the Wyckoff module already computes: how straight the path of price is, ranked against this instrument\'s own history.\n\nAbove this percentile the market is travelling, every oscillator stays pinned, and exhaustion evidence is at its least reliable. The bar is then raised by the penalty beside it.\n\n100 disables the gate.')
confluenceTrendPenaltyInput = input.int(1, 'Extra Score', minval = 0, maxval = 4, group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, inline = 'conftrend')
confluenceRequireDivInTrendInput = input.bool(true, 'Require Divergence In A Strong Trend', group = CONFLUENCE_GROUP, tooltip = 'In a strong trend, only a divergence may open a counter-trend signal. It is the one vote that compares two swings instead of judging a single bar, and the only one that is not pinned by the trend itself.')

// [W1] Wyckoff phase. Display and alerts only: nothing in the signal path reads it.
showRegimeInput = input.bool(false, 'Wyckoff Phase Detection', group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'Classifies the market into the four Wyckoff phases.\n\nStep 1, trend or trading range: Kaufman\'s efficiency ratio, ranked against its own history with hysteresis. Trending + rising = MARKUP, trending + falling = MARKDOWN.\n\nStep 2, which kind of trading range: four votes, +1 for accumulation or -1 for distribution:\n- Prior trend: a range after a markdown leans accumulation, after a markup distribution\n- Location: lower part of the long-term range leans accumulation, upper part distribution\n- Effort (volume): more volume on up-closes than down-closes leans accumulation\n- Events: a recent SPRING (dip below the range low that closes back inside) leans accumulation, an UPTHRUST distribution\n\nThe sum picks the phase when a range starts. Inside a range the label only switches when the evidence clearly turns (score of 2 or more against it), so it does not flicker.\n\nContext only: it does not filter, dim or suppress any signal. The phase readout at the right edge prints the score and the four votes behind it: P prior trend, L location, V volume effort, E spring/upthrust event.')
regimeErLengthInput = input.int(20, 'Efficiency Ratio Length', minval = 2, maxval = 200, group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'Lookback for the net-move over total-move calculation, and for the direction of a trend. Shorter reacts faster and is noisier.')
regimeErSmoothInput = input.int(5, 'Efficiency Ratio Smoothing', minval = 1, maxval = 50, group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'The raw efficiency ratio is jumpy on short lookbacks and needs smoothing before it can be ranked.')
regimeLookbackInput = input.int(100, 'Percentile Lookback', minval = 20, maxval = 1000, group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'The efficiency ratio is ranked against its own history over this many bars rather than against a fixed constant, so the same settings behave sensibly across instruments and timeframes.')
regimeEnterInput = input.int(60, 'Trend Above Percentile', minval = 50, maxval = 95, group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'hyst')
regimeExitInput = input.int(40, 'Range Below Percentile', minval = 5, maxval = 50, group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'hyst')
wyRangeLengthInput = input.int(20, 'Trading Range Lookback', minval = 5, maxval = 200, group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'Bars that define the current trading range. Its high and low are the levels a spring or an upthrust must pierce, and the same window measures the up-volume vs down-volume balance.')
wyLocationLengthInput = input.int(200, 'Location Lookback', minval = 50, maxval = 1000, group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'Long-term range used to judge whether a trading range sits low (accumulation zone) or high (distribution zone). Bottom 40% votes accumulation, top 40% distribution.')
wyEventMemoryInput = input.int(15, 'Spring / Upthrust Memory (bars)', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'How long a spring or an upthrust keeps voting for its phase.')
showWyEventsInput = input.bool(false, 'Mark Springs / Upthrusts', group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'Tiny "Spring" / "UT" labels on the bars that produced one while price was in a trading range. Off by default: liquidity sweep labels already mark most of them.')
showWyRangeBoxInput = input.bool(true, 'Draw Trading Range Boxes', group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'wybox', tooltip = 'A faint box around each accumulation or distribution range, labelled with its phase. Only the most recent ones are kept.')
wyMaxBoxesInput = input.int(5, 'Keep last', minval = 1, maxval = 50, group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'wybox')
showRegimeLabelInput = input.bool(true, 'Show Phase Label', group = REGIME_GROUP)
showRegimeBackgroundInput = input.bool(false, 'Tint Background', group = REGIME_GROUP, tooltip = 'Off by default because a full-chart tint competes with the order block and gap zones.')
regimeBackgroundOpacityInput = input.int(93, 'Background Opacity', minval = 80, maxval = 100, group = REGIME_GROUP)
wyAccumulationColorInput = input.color(#42a5f5, 'Accumulation', group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'wycol1')
wyMarkupColorInput = input.color(#26a69a, 'Markup', group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'wycol1')
wyDistributionColorInput = input.color(#ffa726, 'Distribution', group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'wycol2')
wyMarkdownColorInput = input.color(#ef5350, 'Markdown', group = REGIME_GROUP, inline = 'wycol2')

// [L-STAT] Signal statistics. Measurement only: nothing here feeds a signal, a drawing or an alert.
statsEnabledInput = input.bool(false, 'Signal Statistics Table', group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Tracks every structure and sweep-reversal signal forward to a mechanical resolution and reports how each signal type actually performed on this chart.\n\nThis is NOT a backtest. There is no spread, no slippage, no position sizing and no trade management, and every signal type is measured against the same volatility-normalised stop so that the rows are comparable to each other. It measures SIGNAL QUALITY, not strategy P&L.\n\nIts purpose is A/B testing: the rows count the signals that survive your CURRENT settings, so toggling the displacement filter or the HTF bias filter and watching these numbers move is the intended workflow.')
statsAtrLengthInput = input.int(14, 'Risk ATR Length', minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'ATR used to size the risk unit. Deliberately shorter than the ATR(200) used elsewhere in the script, so the stop reflects volatility around the signal rather than the volatility of the whole chart.')
statsRiskAtrMultInput = input.float(1.0, 'Stop Distance (x ATR)', minval = 0.1, maxval = 10.0, step = 0.1, group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'One risk unit, 1R, as a multiple of ATR.\n\nAn ATR stop is used rather than a structural one on purpose: a structural stop makes R mean something different for every signal, which would make the rows incomparable. The cost is that these numbers are not what a structural-stop trader would experience.')
statsTargetRInput = input.float(2.0, 'Target (R)', minval = 0.5, maxval = 20.0, step = 0.5, group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Reward multiple that counts as a win. The break-even win rate is 1 / (1 + Target), so the Win% column is coloured against that line rather than against 50%.')
statsHorizonInput = input.int(50, 'Horizon (bars)', minval = 5, maxval = 1000, group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'How long a signal is given to reach the target or the stop. A signal that does neither within this many bars is closed at the market and counted as a timeout.\n\nA high timeout rate is itself a finding: it means the signal type does not produce movement, which is worse than being wrong quickly.')
statsMaxOpenInput = input.int(300, 'Max Concurrent Tracked', minval = 10, maxval = 1000, group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Safety valve against pathological settings, for example a very long horizon combined with a very short internal structure length. A signal arriving while this many are already open is IGNORED ENTIRELY rather than counted, so the table never reports a rate over a population it did not actually measure.')
statsTablePositionInput = input.string('Top Right', 'Table Position', options = ['Top Right', 'Middle Right', 'Bottom Right', 'Top Left', 'Bottom Left', 'Bottom Center'], group = STATS_GROUP)
statsTableSizeInput = input.string(TINY, 'Table Text Size', options = [TINY, SMALL, NORMAL], group = STATS_GROUP)
// [T1] compact is the default so the table competes less with the chart
statsTableStyleInput = input.string('Compact', 'Table Style', options = ['Compact', 'Full'], group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Compact: Signal, N, Win% and expectancy (R) only, short row names, no grid lines, translucent background. Hover a row name for MFE, timeout rate and the win/loss/open breakdown. Hover the header for the methodology.\n\nFull: all six columns with grid lines (v4.2 layout).\n\nRow names in Compact: i = internal, RT = retest, ✕ = exit signal in Long-Only mode.')
statsHideEmptyRowsInput = input.bool(true, 'Hide Signal Types With No Data', group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Rows stay blank when their signal type is switched off or has not fired yet.')
// [IDX3] long-only accounting and costs
statsLongOnlyInput = input.string(IDX_AUTO, 'Long-Only Mode', options = [IDX_AUTO, IDX_ON, IDX_OFF], group = STATS_GROUP, tooltip = 'Retail short selling is practically unavailable on IDX.\n\nOn: bearish rows are measured as EXIT signals: a win means price fell the target distance after the exit (a loss avoided), a loss means price rallied 1R (an early exit). They are excluded from the ALL row and charged no fees.\n\nAuto: On for IDX symbols, Off elsewhere.')
statsBuyFeeInput = input.float(0.15, 'Buy Fee %', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.01, group = STATS_GROUP, inline = 'fees', tooltip = 'Round-trip costs are converted to R and subtracted from every closed long (and short) trade. Typical IDX retail: ~0.15% buy, ~0.25% sell including the 0.1% final tax. Check your broker.')
statsSellFeeInput = input.float(0.25, 'Sell Fee %', minval = 0.0, maxval = 2.0, step = 0.01, group = STATS_GROUP, inline = 'fees')

// [IDX1][IDX2] Indonesia Stock Exchange specifics
idxModeInput = input.string(IDX_ON, 'IDX Mode', options = [IDX_AUTO, IDX_ON, IDX_OFF], group = IDX_GROUP, tooltip = 'Enables the IDX tick size (fraksi harga) and ARA/ARB logic.\n\nAuto: on when the symbol prefix is IDX.')
eqhMinTicksInput = input.int(2, 'EQH/EQL Min Tolerance (ticks)', minval = 0, maxval = 20, group = IDX_GROUP, tooltip = 'Equal highs/lows tolerance becomes max(Threshold x ATR, this many ticks). On a Rp 50-500 stock 0.1 x ATR is often smaller than a single tick, which made EQH/EQL require an exact match.')
fvgMinTicksInput = input.int(2, 'FVG Min Width (ticks)', minval = 0, maxval = 20, group = IDX_GROUP, tooltip = 'Fair value gaps narrower than this many ticks are ignored. A one-tick gap on IDX is an artefact of the price grid, not an imbalance.')
showLimitBarsInput = input.bool(true, 'Mark ARA / ARB Bars', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'limmark', tooltip = 'Marks bars that reached the auto rejection limit, measured against the previous daily close. Intraday and daily charts only.\n\nOne small label per streak: consecutive limit days read "ARB×3". Hover a label for the limit price. On a marked bar the MFI triangle on that side is not drawn, so the two never overlap.\n\nThe limit price is rounded to the tick grid, so treat the mark as an approximation. Acceleration board and watch-list (FCA) stocks use different limits and are not modelled.')
// [L2] bounded so limit markers cannot crowd structure labels out of the 500-label budget
maxLimitLabelsInput = input.int(30, 'Keep last', minval = 1, maxval = 200, group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'limmark')
excludeLimitBarsInput = input.bool(true, 'Exclude Limit-Locked Bars', group = IDX_GROUP, tooltip = 'A bar that CLOSES at ARA or ARB has no two-sided liquidity. It is skipped when choosing the order block candle, and it cannot pierce or confirm a liquidity sweep.')
araTier1Input = input.float(35, 'ARA % (Rp 50-200)', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'ara')
araTier2Input = input.float(25, '(200-5000)', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'ara')
araTier3Input = input.float(20, '(>5000)', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'ara')
arbPercentInput = input.float(15, 'ARB %', minval = 1, maxval = 100, group = IDX_GROUP, tooltip = 'BEI has changed the ARB rule several times. Check the current regulation and set it here.')
araColorInput = input.color(color.new(#00e676, 0), 'ARA', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'limcol')
arbColorInput = input.color(color.new(#ff1744, 0), 'ARB', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'limcol')
// [C4] IDX session windows, display only
showIdxSessionsInput = input.bool(false, 'Shade IDX Session Windows', group = IDX_GROUP, tooltip = 'Intraday charts only, Asia/Jakarta time.\n\nOff by default; switch on when reading intraday charts.\n\nOpening: 09:00-09:30, right after the pre-opening auction, where most of the day\'s gap and stop runs happen.\nSession 2 open: 13:30-14:00 Mon-Thu, 14:00-14:30 Fri.\nPre-closing: 15:50-16:00, the closing auction that sets the official close.\n\nAll three use one muted grey by default and differ only in strength (pre-closing strongest, session 2 faintest), so the chart does not flash three colours every day. Each colour can still be changed.\n\nContext only: nothing is filtered by it.')
idxOpenSessionInput = input.session('0900-0930', 'Opening', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess1')
idxOpenColorInput = input.color(color.new(#787b86, 94), '', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess1')
idxSession2InputMonThu = input.session('1330-1400', 'Session 2 Mon-Thu', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess2')
idxSession2InputFri = input.session('1400-1430', 'Fri', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess2')
idxSession2ColorInput = input.color(color.new(#787b86, 96), '', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess2')
idxCloseSessionInput = input.session('1550-1600', 'Pre-closing', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess3')
idxCloseColorInput = input.color(color.new(#787b86, 92), '', group = IDX_GROUP, inline = 'sess3')

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//CHART DENSITY
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [T1] The density preset can only SUBTRACT from the individual switches, and only from DRAWINGS. Detection,
//      alerts and the statistics table population are untouched, so switching to Minimal cannot change a signal
//      or a measured number, only what is on screen.
bool densityMinimal = densityInput == DENSITY_MINIMAL

bool showInternalsEffective = showInternalsInput and not densityMinimal
bool showEqualHighsLowsEffective = showEqualHighsLowsInput and not densityMinimal
bool showConfirmationZonesEffective = showConfirmationZonesInput and not densityMinimal
bool showOBStatusEffective = showOBStatusLabelsInput and not densityMinimal
bool showWyRangeBoxEffective = showWyRangeBoxInput and not densityMinimal
bool showIdxSessionsEffective = showIdxSessionsInput and not densityMinimal
bool statsTableEffective = statsEnabledInput and not densityMinimal
bool showPremiumDiscountEffective = showPremiumDiscountZonesInput and not densityMinimal

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//DATA STRUCTURES & VARIABLES
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// @type                            UDT representing alerts as bool fields
type alerts
	bool internalBullishBOS = false
	bool internalBearishBOS = false
	bool internalBullishCHoCH = false
	bool internalBearishCHoCH = false
	bool swingBullishBOS = false
	bool swingBearishBOS = false
	bool swingBullishCHoCH = false
	bool swingBearishCHoCH = false
	// [E29] "...OrderBlock" now means MITIGATED (price reached the mean threshold)
	bool internalBullishOrderBlock = false
	bool internalBearishOrderBlock = false
	bool swingBullishOrderBlock = false
	bool swingBearishOrderBlock = false
	// [E29] "...OrderBlockBroken" means price traded fully through the block
	bool internalBullishOrderBlockBroken = false
	bool internalBearishOrderBlockBroken = false
	bool swingBullishOrderBlockBroken = false
	bool swingBearishOrderBlockBroken = false
	bool equalHighs = false
	bool equalLows = false
	bool bullishFairValueGap = false
	bool bearishFairValueGap = false
	// [I25] inverse fair value gaps
	bool bullishIFVG = false
	bool bearishIFVG = false
	// [F24] liquidity sweeps
	bool buysideLiquiditySweep = false
	bool sellsideLiquiditySweep = false
	bool bullishSweepReversal = false
	bool bearishSweepReversal = false
	// [R1] zone retests: wick into a live zone, close back out on the bias side
	bool bullishOBRetest = false
	bool bearishOBRetest = false
	bool bullishFVGRetest = false
	bool bearishFVGRetest = false
	// [B1] composite reversal signal: context + exhaustion + divergence + liquidity, confirmed by a CHoCH
	bool bullishConfluence = false
	bool bearishConfluence = false

// @type                            UDT representing last swing extremes (top & bottom)
type trailingExtremes
	float top
	float bottom
	int barTime
	int barIndex
	int lastTopTime
	int lastBottomTime

// @type                            UDT representing Fair Value Gaps
type fairValueGap
	float top
	float bottom
	int bias
	box topBox
	box bottomBox
	// [I25] true once the gap has flipped role. Added last so the positional fairValueGap.new() calls
	//       in drawFairValueGaps() keep working unchanged.
	bool inverted = false
	// [R1] true once this gap has produced a retest signal in its CURRENT role. Reset when it inverts.
	bool retested = false

// @type                            UDT representing trend bias
// @field protectedHigh             [C3] highest high of the leg that produced the last bearish break
// @field protectedLow              [C3] lowest low of the leg that produced the last bullish break
// @field *Time / *Index            [C3] where that extreme sits, used to draw a strict CHoCH and to bound its OB leg
type trend
	int bias
	float protectedHigh = na
	int protectedHighTime = na
	int protectedHighIndex = na
	float protectedLow = na
	int protectedLowTime = na
	int protectedLowIndex = na

// @type                            UDT representing Equal Highs Lows display
type equalDisplay
	line l_ine = na
	label l_abel = na

// @type                            UDT representing a pivot point (swing point)
type pivot
	float currentLevel
	float lastLevel
	bool crossed
	int barTime = time
	int barIndex = bar_index

// @type                            UDT representing an order block
// @field barHigh                   bar high (always >= barLow, see [B10])
// @field barLow                    bar low
// @field barTime                   time of the order block CANDLE
// @field bias                      BULLISH or BEARISH
// @field status                    current status: ACTIVE, PARTIAL, MITIGATED, INVALID
// @field mitigationDepth           running maximum penetration depth, 0 to 1+
// @field zoneEntered               true once price first enters the OB zone
// @field entryTime                 time when price first entered the zone
// @field partialMitigationLevel    ICT mean threshold of the block. Computed but not yet drawn.
// @field creationTime              time of the BAR THAT CREATED the block. [B6] used to skip same-bar evaluation.
type orderBlock
	float barHigh
	float barLow
	int barTime
	int bias
	int status = 0 // literal 0 == ACTIVE, UDT defaults must be literals
	float mitigationDepth = 0.0
	bool zoneEntered = false
	int entryTime = na
	float partialMitigationLevel = na
	int creationTime = na
	// [R1] true once this block has produced its retest signal
	bool retested = false

// @type                            [F24] UDT representing a resting liquidity pool taken from an EQH / EQL
// @field level                     price of the pool
// @field barTime                   time of the pivot the pool sits on
// @field barIndex                  bar_index at which the pool became watchable (its confirmation bar)
// @field isHigh                    true = buyside liquidity above equal highs, false = sellside below equal lows
// @field pierced                   true once price has traded beyond the level
// @field piercedIndex              bar_index of the piercing bar, used for the rejection window
// @field swept                     true once the raid is confirmed by a close back on the original side
type liquidityLevel
	float level
	int barTime
	int barIndex
	bool isHigh
	bool pierced = false
	int piercedIndex = na
	bool swept = false
	line l_ine = na
	label l_abel = na
	// [C1] pool source shown in the sweep label; '' falls back to BSL / SSL
	string tag = ''

// @type                            [F24] most recent sweep on each side, for the CHoCH confluence check.
//                                  This has to be a UDT: Pine functions may mutate object fields but may
//                                  NOT reassign a global scalar, and the check runs inside displayStructure().
// @field lastBuysideIndex          bar_index of the last buyside (EQH) sweep, na once consumed
// @field lastSellsideIndex         bar_index of the last sellside (EQL) sweep, na once consumed
// @field buysideLabel / sellsideLabel      [V1] sweep label of the last bar that swept that side, so a second
//                                          pool swept by the SAME bar is appended to it instead of stacking
// @field buysideLabelIndex / sellsideLabelIndex  bar_index that label belongs to
type sweepState
	int lastBuysideIndex = na
	int lastSellsideIndex = na
	label buysideLabel = na
	int buysideLabelIndex = na
	label sellsideLabel = na
	int sellsideLabelIndex = na

// @type                            [L-STAT] one signal being tracked forward to a mechanical resolution.
//                                  See the SIGNAL STATISTICS section for how these are opened and resolved.
// @field typeIndex                 which STAT_* row this signal belongs to
// @field bias                      BULLISH or BEARISH
// @field entry                     close of the bar the signal fired on
// @field stop                      entry -/+ one risk unit
// @field target                    entry +/- statsTargetRInput risk units
// @field riskUnit                  price distance of 1R, frozen at entry so later volatility cannot rescale it
// @field openIndex                 bar_index at entry. Evaluation starts strictly after this bar.
// @field maxFavorableR             running maximum favourable excursion, in R
type openTrade
	int typeIndex
	int bias
	float entry
	float stop
	float target
	float riskUnit
	int openIndex
	float maxFavorableR = 0.0

// @variable                        current swing pivot high
var pivot swingHigh = pivot.new(na, na, false)
// @variable                        current swing pivot low
var pivot swingLow = pivot.new(na, na, false)
// @variable                        current internal pivot high
var pivot internalHigh = pivot.new(na, na, false)
// @variable                        current internal pivot low
var pivot internalLow = pivot.new(na, na, false)
// @variable                        current equal high pivot
var pivot equalHigh = pivot.new(na, na, false)
// @variable                        current equal low pivot
var pivot equalLow = pivot.new(na, na, false)
// @variable                        swing trend bias
var trend swingTrend = trend.new(0)
// @variable                        internal trend bias
var trend internalTrend = trend.new(0)
// @variable                        equal high display
var equalDisplay equalHighDisplay = equalDisplay.new()
// @variable                        equal low display
var equalDisplay equalLowDisplay = equalDisplay.new()
// @variable                        storage for fairValueGap UDTs
var array<fairValueGap> fairValueGaps = array.new<fairValueGap>()
// @variable                        [F24] tracked liquidity pools, oldest first
var array<liquidityLevel> liquidityLevels = array.new<liquidityLevel>()
// @variable                        [F24] most recent sweep on each side
var sweepState sweeps = sweepState.new()
// @variable                        storage for parsed highs
var array<float> parsedHighs = array.new<float>()
// @variable                        storage for parsed lows
var array<float> parsedLows = array.new<float>()
// @variable                        storage for raw highs
var array<float> highs = array.new<float>()
// @variable                        storage for raw lows
var array<float> lows = array.new<float>()
// @variable                        [I23] storage for raw opens, needed to read candle direction
var array<float> opens = array.new<float>()
// @variable                        [I23] storage for raw closes, needed to read candle direction
var array<float> closes = array.new<float>()
// @variable                        [IDX2] storage for limit-locked flags (bar closed at ARA or ARB)
var array<bool> limitLockedBars = array.new<bool>()
// @variable                        storage for bar time values
var array<int> times = array.new<int>()
// @variable                        last trailing swing high and low
var trailingExtremes trailing = trailingExtremes.new()
// @variable                        storage for orderBlock UDTs (swing order blocks)
var array<orderBlock> swingOrderBlocks = array.new<orderBlock>()
// @variable                        storage for orderBlock UDTs (internal order blocks)
var array<orderBlock> internalOrderBlocks = array.new<orderBlock>()
// @variable                        storage for swing order blocks boxes
var array<box> swingOrderBlocksBoxes = array.new<box>()
// @variable                        storage for internal order blocks boxes
var array<box> internalOrderBlocksBoxes = array.new<box>()
// @variable                        storage for swing order blocks confirmation zones
var array<box> swingOrderBlocksConfirmationBoxes = array.new<box>()
// @variable                        storage for internal order blocks confirmation zones
var array<box> internalOrderBlocksConfirmationBoxes = array.new<box>()
// @variable                        color for swing bullish structures
var swingBullishColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? MONO_BULLISH : swingBullColorInput
// @variable                        color for swing bearish structures
var swingBearishColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? MONO_BEARISH : swingBearColorInput
// @variable                        color for bullish fair value gaps
var fairValueGapBullishColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? color.new(MONO_BULLISH, 70) : fairValueGapsBullColorInput
// @variable                        color for bearish fair value gaps
var fairValueGapBearishColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? color.new(MONO_BEARISH, 70) : fairValueGapsBearColorInput
// @variable                        color for premium zone
var premiumZoneColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? MONO_BEARISH : premiumZoneColorInput
// @variable                        color for discount zone
var discountZoneColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? MONO_BULLISH : discountZoneColorInput
// @variable                        bar index on current script iteration
varip int currentBarIndex = bar_index
// @variable                        bar index on last script iteration
varip int lastBarIndex = bar_index
// @variable                        alerts in current bar
alerts currentAlerts = alerts.new()
// @variable                        time at start of chart
var initialTime = time
// @variable                        [D16] bar_index of the element currently held at array position 0.
//                                  Stays 0 until the storage arrays start being trimmed.
var int arrayOffset = 0
// @variable                        [D9b] stable duration of one bar in ms. `time - time[1]` was used before,
//                                  which measures a 3-day gap on the first bar after a weekend and blew up
//                                  every right-edge projection that multiplied it.
var int barDurationMs = math.max(1, nz(timeframe.in_seconds(), 60)) * 1000

// [W2] Wyckoff drawings live in the BOTTOM layer. Pine stacks drawings by type (boxes under lines under labels)
//      and, within a type, by creation order: the earliest-created box is drawn underneath. So the Wyckoff range
//      boxes and the phase text box are allocated here, on the first bar and BEFORE every other box pool below
//      (order blocks, confirmation zones), and are then only ever reused, never re-created. Fair value gap and
//      premium/discount boxes are created later still, so they sit above them too. The phase readout is a
//      borderless box with text rather than a label, because any label would render above all boxes and lines.
var array<box> wyBoxes = array.new<box>()
var box wyPhaseTextBox = na
if barstate.isfirst and showRegimeInput
    if showWyRangeBoxEffective
        for index = 1 to wyMaxBoxesInput by 1
            wyBoxes.push(box.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = color(na), bgcolor = color(na), border_style = line.style_dotted, text_size = size.tiny, text_halign = text.align_left, text_valign = text.align_top))
    if showRegimeLabelInput
        wyPhaseTextBox := box.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time, border_color = color.new(color.gray, 100), border_width = 0, bgcolor = color.new(color.gray, 100), text_size = size.small, text_halign = text.align_left, text_valign = text.align_center, text_wrap = text.wrap_none)

// we create the needed drawing objects at the first execution
// [T2] the status text lives inside the order block box now, so no label pool is allocated here
// [D9] the main boxes no longer use extend.right. They ended at last_bar_time anyway and the infinite
//      extension covered the confirmation zones completely, making that whole feature invisible.
// [D9] confirmation boxes use xloc.bar_index so their width is an exact bar count, immune to session gaps.
if barstate.isfirst
    if showSwingOrderBlocksInput
        for index = 1 to swingOrderBlocksSizeInput by 1
            swingOrderBlocksBoxes.push(box.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time))
            swingOrderBlocksConfirmationBoxes.push(box.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = color(na), bgcolor = color(na)))
    if showInternalOrderBlocksInput
        for index = 1 to internalOrderBlocksSizeInput by 1
            internalOrderBlocksBoxes.push(box.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time))
            internalOrderBlocksConfirmationBoxes.push(box.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_index, border_color = color(na), bgcolor = color(na)))

// @variable                        source to use in bearish order blocks mitigation
bearishOrderBlockMitigationSource = orderBlockMitigationInput == CLOSE ? close : high
// @variable                        source to use in bullish order blocks mitigation
bullishOrderBlockMitigationSource = orderBlockMitigationInput == CLOSE ? close : low
// @variable                        [I25] source used when price trades DOWN through a gap
fairValueGapDownSource = fairValueGapsInvalidationInput == CLOSE ? close : low
// @variable                        [I25] source used when price trades UP through a gap
fairValueGapUpSource = fairValueGapsInvalidationInput == CLOSE ? close : high
// @variable                        [P5] sources used to INVALIDATE an order block (far edge crossed)
bearishOrderBlockInvalidationSource = orderBlockInvalidationInput == CLOSE ? close : high
bullishOrderBlockInvalidationSource = orderBlockInvalidationInput == CLOSE ? close : low
// @variable                        default volatility measure
// [P4] length is an input, and ATR(14) stands in while the long ATR is still na (recent IPOs). Without the
//      fallback every comparison against it was false: no EQH/EQL, and every break rejected by the ATR filter.
atrMeasure = nz(ta.atr(volatilityAtrLengthInput), ta.atr(14))
// @variable                        parsed volatility measure by user settings
volatilityMeasure = orderBlockFilterInput == ATR ? atrMeasure : ta.cum(ta.tr) / bar_index
// @variable                        true if current bar is a high volatility bar
highVolatilityBar = high - low >= 2 * volatilityMeasure
// @variable                        parsed high
parsedHigh = highVolatilityBar ? low : high
// @variable                        parsed low
parsedLow = highVolatilityBar ? high : low

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [IDX1][IDX2] Indonesia Stock Exchange: tick size and auto rejection limits
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// @function            [IDX1] IDX tick size (fraksi harga) for a price level
// @returns             float
idxTick(float p) =>
    p < 200 ? 1.0 : p < 500 ? 2.0 : p < 2000 ? 5.0 : p < 5000 ? 10.0 : 25.0

// @variable            [IDX1] true when the IDX-specific logic applies to this symbol
bool idxMode = idxModeInput == IDX_ON or idxModeInput == IDX_AUTO and syminfo.prefix == 'IDX'

// [IDX2] ARA/ARB are defined against the previous DAILY close. `close[1]` with lookahead_on is the standard
//        non-repainting read of the prior session, and equals close[1] on a 1D chart. Weekly and multi-day
//        charts are skipped: a bar there spans several limit windows.
float idxPrevDailyClose = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, 'D', close[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
bool idxLimitActive = idxMode and (timeframe.isintraday or timeframe.isdaily and timeframe.multiplier == 1) and not na(idxPrevDailyClose) and idxPrevDailyClose > 0

float araPercent = idxPrevDailyClose <= 200 ? araTier1Input : idxPrevDailyClose <= 5000 ? araTier2Input : araTier3Input
float araRaw = idxPrevDailyClose * (1 + araPercent / 100)
float arbRaw = idxPrevDailyClose * (1 - arbPercentInput / 100)
// the limit is rounded onto the tick grid on the side that stays within the limit
float araLevel = math.floor(araRaw / idxTick(araRaw)) * idxTick(araRaw)
// Rp 50 is the regular-board floor: a stock at or above it cannot print lower on that board. Below Rp 50
// (watch-list / FCA stocks) no floor is applied.
float arbLevel = math.max(math.ceil(arbRaw / idxTick(arbRaw)) * idxTick(arbRaw), idxPrevDailyClose >= 50 ? 50.0 : 0.0)

bool araTouched = idxLimitActive and high >= araLevel
// [L1] a limit at or above the previous close leaves no room to fall. That is the Rp 50 floor case: a stock
//      parked at Rp 50 used to be flagged ARB (and limit-locked) on every single bar.
bool arbTouched = idxLimitActive and arbLevel < idxPrevDailyClose and low <= arbLevel
// locked = closed AT the limit, so there was no two-sided market at the close
bool araLocked = araTouched and close >= araLevel
bool arbLocked = arbTouched and close <= arbLevel
// @variable            [IDX2] true when this bar must be kept out of OB selection and sweep detection
bool limitLockedBar = excludeLimitBarsInput and (araLocked or arbLocked)

// [L2] ARA/ARB markers are drawn as small labels further down, after the sweep update, see IDX LIMIT MARKERS

// @variable            [IDX1] EQH/EQL tolerance in price, floored at N ticks on IDX. `nz` keeps it a number on
//                      the first few bars, where even the ATR(14) fallback is still na.
equalTolerance(float price) =>
    float atrPart = equalHighsLowsThresholdInput * nz(atrMeasure)
    idxMode ? math.max(atrPart, eqhMinTicksInput * idxTick(price)) : atrPart

// we store current values into the arrays at each bar
parsedHighs.push(parsedHigh)
parsedLows.push(parsedLow)
highs.push(high)
lows.push(low)
opens.push(open)
closes.push(close)
times.push(time)
limitLockedBars.push(limitLockedBar)

// [D16] keep the storage arrays bounded. All of them are trimmed together so their positions stay aligned,
//       and arrayOffset records how far array position 0 has drifted away from bar_index 0.
// [I23] opens and closes joined this set when the Last Opposite Candle method was added. Every array here
//       must be shifted in lockstep or the shared position index silently points at the wrong bar.
if times.size() > MAX_STORED_BARS
    parsedHighs.shift()
    parsedLows.shift()
    highs.shift()
    lows.shift()
    opens.shift()
    closes.shift()
    times.shift()
    limitLockedBars.shift() // [IDX2] same lockstep rule
    arrayOffset += 1

// [E22] Displacement measured on the current bar, which is the breaking bar at the moment a structure
//       break is evaluated. Body is used rather than full range: a wide candle that closes near its open
//       is indecision, not displacement.
// [P3] Gap-aware body: measured from the previous close when the bar gapped in the break direction. On IDX
//      the pre-opening auction frequently gaps a stock through the level, leaving a small intraday body that
//      the old `close - open` test rejected. Because ta.crossover() does not re-arm while price stays above
//      the level, one such rejection could freeze the trend state for a long time.
bullishDisplacementATR = close - math.min(open, nz(close[1], open)) >= displacementFactorInput * atrMeasure
bearishDisplacementATR = math.max(open, nz(close[1], open)) - close >= displacementFactorInput * atrMeasure
// classic 3-candle gap, where the breaking bar is the third candle
bullishDisplacementFVG = low > high[2]
bearishDisplacementFVG = high < low[2]

bool bullishDisplacement = switch displacementFilterInput
    DISPLACEMENT_ATR => bullishDisplacementATR
    DISPLACEMENT_FVG => bullishDisplacementFVG
    DISPLACEMENT_BOTH => bullishDisplacementATR and bullishDisplacementFVG
    => true

bool bearishDisplacement = switch displacementFilterInput
    DISPLACEMENT_ATR => bearishDisplacementATR
    DISPLACEMENT_FVG => bearishDisplacementFVG
    DISPLACEMENT_BOTH => bearishDisplacementATR and bearishDisplacementFVG
    => true

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//USER-DEFINED FUNCTIONS
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// @function            Get the value of the current leg, it can be 0 (bearish) or 1 (bullish)
// @returns             int
leg(int size) =>
    var leg = 0
    newLegHigh = high[size] > ta.highest(size)
    newLegLow = low[size] < ta.lowest(size)

    if newLegHigh
        leg := BEARISH_LEG
    else if newLegLow
        leg := BULLISH_LEG
    leg

// @function            Identify whether the current value is the start of a new leg (swing)
// @returns             bool
startOfNewLeg(int leg) =>
    ta.change(leg) != 0

// @function            Identify whether the current level is the start of a new bearish leg (swing)
// @returns             bool
startOfBearishLeg(int leg) =>
    ta.change(leg) == -1

// @function            Identify whether the current level is the start of a new bullish leg (swing)
// @returns             bool
startOfBullishLeg(int leg) =>
    ta.change(leg) == +1

// @function            [G26] compact structural bias, written to be evaluated inside request.security()
// @param len           pivot lookback
// @returns             BULLISH, BEARISH, or 0 before the first break
//
// This is deliberately NOT the chart's structure engine. getCurrentStructure() mutates global pivot and
// trend objects, which would be corrupted if it were also run in a foreign timeframe context. This one is
// self-contained: its only state is its own `var` fields, which Pine keeps per evaluation context.
structuralBias(int len) =>
    var int bias = 0
    var float lastPivotHigh = na
    var float lastPivotLow = na

    float p_ivotHigh = ta.pivothigh(len, len)
    float p_ivotLow = ta.pivotlow(len, len)

    lastPivotHigh := na(p_ivotHigh) ? lastPivotHigh : p_ivotHigh
    lastPivotLow := na(p_ivotLow) ? lastPivotLow : p_ivotLow

    if not na(lastPivotHigh) and close > lastPivotHigh
        bias := BULLISH
    else if not na(lastPivotLow) and close < lastPivotLow
        bias := BEARISH
    bias

// @function            [G26] simple moving-average bias
// @returns             BULLISH, BEARISH, or 0
emaBias(int len) =>
    float e_ma = ta.ema(close, len)
    close > e_ma ? BULLISH : close < e_ma ? BEARISH : 0

// @function            [G26] the expression evaluated in the higher timeframe context
// @returns             int
// htfBiasMethodInput is an input and therefore constant for the whole run, so the same branch is taken on
// every bar. That is what keeps the selected function's internal `var` state continuous. The unused branch
// may go stale, which is harmless because its result is never read.
htfBiasExpression() =>
    htfBiasMethodInput == HTF_STRUCTURE ? structuralBias(htfBiasLengthInput) : emaBias(htfBiasLengthInput)

// [G26] Global, and placed here on purpose: displayStructure() reads these, and Pine requires a global to
//       be declared before the function that references it.
// [R2] `expr[1]` with lookahead_on is the standard non-repainting HTF idiom: every chart bar sees the bias of the
//      last CONFIRMED HTF bar, on history and live alike. The v4.2 form (lookahead_off, no offset) was leak-free
//      on history but read the still-forming HTF bar in real time, so live filtering differed from the history
//      the statistics table was measured on.
int htfBias = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htfBiasTimeframeInput, htfBiasExpression()[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
// @variable            true only when the filter is on AND the chosen timeframe is strictly higher
bool htfBiasEnabled = htfBiasFilterInput != HTF_OFF and timeframe.in_seconds(htfBiasTimeframeInput) > timeframe.in_seconds()

// @function            create a new label
// @returns             label ID
drawLabel(int labelTime, float labelPrice, string tag, color labelColor, string labelStyle) =>
    var label l_abel = na

    if modeInput == PRESENT
        l_abel.delete()

    l_abel := label.new(chart.point.new(labelTime, na, labelPrice), tag, xloc.bar_time, color = color(na), textcolor = labelColor, style = labelStyle, size = size.small)
    l_abel

// @variable            [V1] pool line style. getStyle() is defined further down, and Pine needs a function
//                      declared before its first use, so the style is resolved here once instead.
string poolLineStyle = poolLineStyleInput == SOLID ? line.style_solid : poolLineStyleInput == DASHED ? line.style_dashed : line.style_dotted

// @function            [F24] register an equal high / low as a resting liquidity pool
// @param poolLevel      price the stops are assumed to rest at
// @param poolTime       time of the pivot the pool sits on
// @param isHigh         true for buyside liquidity above equal highs
// @param tag            [C1] pool source for the sweep label ('' = generic BSL / SSL)
// @returns              void
registerLiquidityLevel(float poolLevel, int poolTime, bool isHigh, string tag = '') =>
    if showLiquiditySweepsInput and not na(poolLevel)
        color poolColor = isHigh ? liquidityBuysideColorInput : liquiditySellsideColorInput

        liquidityLevel pool = liquidityLevel.new(poolLevel, poolTime, bar_index, isHigh)
        pool.tag := tag
        // [V1] faded, and dotted by default: an open pool is context, it should not compete with the candles
        pool.l_ine := line.new(chart.point.new(poolTime, na, poolLevel), chart.point.new(last_bar_time, na, poolLevel), xloc = xloc.bar_time, color = color.new(poolColor, poolLineTransparencyInput), style = poolLineStyle, extend = extend.right)

        liquidityLevels.push(pool)

        // drop the oldest pools, drawings included, so the line and label budgets stay bounded
        while liquidityLevels.size() > liquiditySweepMaxInput
            liquidityLevel oldestPool = liquidityLevels.shift()
            oldestPool.l_ine.delete()
            oldestPool.l_abel.delete()

// @function            create a new line and label representing an EQH or EQL
// @returns             float
drawEqualHighLow(pivot p_ivot, float level, int size, bool equalHigh) =>
    // [B8] these alert flags were declared and wired to alertcondition() but never raised
    if equalHigh
        currentAlerts.equalHighs := true
    else
        currentAlerts.equalLows := true

    // [F24] The pool sits at the outer edge of the pair, which is where the stops actually rest.
    //       Registration is independent of the EQH/EQL drawing, since sweeps can be tracked with the
    //       EQH/EQL section switched off.
    registerLiquidityLevel(equalHigh ? math.max(p_ivot.currentLevel, level) : math.min(p_ivot.currentLevel, level), time[size], equalHigh)

    // [F24] drawing is now gated separately, because this function also runs when only sweeps are enabled
    if showEqualHighsLowsEffective
        equalDisplay e_qualDisplay = equalHigh ? equalHighDisplay : equalLowDisplay

        string tag = 'EQL'
        color equalColor = swingBullishColor
        string labelStyle = label.style_label_up

        if equalHigh
            tag := 'EQH'
            equalColor := swingBearishColor
            labelStyle := label.style_label_down

        if modeInput == PRESENT
            line.delete(e_qualDisplay.l_ine)
            label.delete(e_qualDisplay.l_abel)

        e_qualDisplay.l_ine := line.new(chart.point.new(p_ivot.barTime, na, p_ivot.currentLevel), chart.point.new(time[size], na, level), xloc = xloc.bar_time, color = equalColor, style = line.style_dotted)
        labelPosition = math.round(0.5 * (p_ivot.barIndex + bar_index - size))
        e_qualDisplay.l_abel := label.new(chart.point.new(na, labelPosition, level), tag, xloc.bar_index, color = color(na), textcolor = equalColor, style = labelStyle, size = equalHighsLowsSizeInput)
    0.0

// @function            [F24] advance every tracked liquidity pool by one bar
// @returns             void
//
// A pool resolves one of two ways once price trades beyond it:
//   SWEPT     price closes back on the original side within the rejection window. The raid is confirmed,
//             the line is frozen at the sweep bar and the pool is kept on the chart as a record.
//   DISCARDED price stays beyond the level past the window. That is acceptance, not a stop hunt, so the
//             pool and its drawings are removed.
updateLiquiditySweeps() =>
    if liquidityLevels.size() > 0
        // [B4] backwards, because resolved pools are removed inside the loop
        for index = liquidityLevels.size() - 1 to 0
            liquidityLevel pool = liquidityLevels.get(index)

            if not pool.swept
                bool discardPool = false
                // [IDX2] a limit-locked bar is a price-band artefact, not a stop hunt: it can neither pierce a
                //        pool nor confirm the rejection. The rejection window still counts it as elapsed.
                bool piercedNow = not limitLockedBar and (pool.isHigh ? high > pool.level : low < pool.level)
                bool rejected = not limitLockedBar and (pool.isHigh ? close < pool.level : close > pool.level)

                if not pool.pierced and piercedNow
                    pool.pierced := true
                    pool.piercedIndex := bar_index

                if pool.pierced
                    if rejected
                        pool.swept := true
                        color poolColor = pool.isHigh ? liquidityBuysideColorInput : liquiditySellsideColorInput

                        // [V1] a swept pool is a record, not a live level: frozen at the sweep bar and faded further
                        color sweptColor = color.new(poolColor, math.min(100, poolLineTransparencyInput + 20))
                        pool.l_ine.set_extend(extend.none)
                        pool.l_ine.set_second_point(chart.point.new(time, na, pool.level))
                        pool.l_ine.set_style(line.style_solid)
                        pool.l_ine.set_color(sweptColor)

                        if showSweepLabelsInput
                            // [V1] Placed at the WICK EXTREME (high for buyside, low for sellside) with the label
                            //      pointing at it, so the text sits just beyond the candle instead of on the wick
                            //      at the pool level. Compact text is the source only; the tooltip carries the rest.
                            string sourceTag = pool.tag != '' ? pool.tag : pool.isHigh ? 'BSL' : 'SSL'
                            string shortTag = sourceTag == 'Swing H' ? 'SH' : sourceTag == 'Swing L' ? 'SL' : sourceTag
                            string sweepText = sweepLabelStyleInput == 'Full' ? sourceTag + ' Sweep' : shortTag
                            string sweepTip = sourceTag + ' swept at ' + str.tostring(pool.level, format.mintick) + ', closed back ' + (pool.isHigh ? 'below' : 'above')

                            // [V1] one label per side per bar: a second pool swept by the same bar joins it
                            label sameBarLabel = na
                            if pool.isHigh and sweeps.buysideLabelIndex == bar_index
                                sameBarLabel := sweeps.buysideLabel
                            else if not pool.isHigh and sweeps.sellsideLabelIndex == bar_index
                                sameBarLabel := sweeps.sellsideLabel

                            if not na(sameBarLabel)
                                sameBarLabel.set_text(sameBarLabel.get_text() + ' · ' + sweepText)
                                0
                            else
                                pool.l_abel := label.new(chart.point.new(time, na, pool.isHigh ? high : low), sweepText, xloc.bar_time, color = color(na), textcolor = color.new(poolColor, math.max(0, poolLineTransparencyInput - 20)), style = pool.isHigh ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, size = size.tiny, tooltip = sweepTip)
                                if pool.isHigh
                                    sweeps.buysideLabel := pool.l_abel
                                    sweeps.buysideLabelIndex := bar_index
                                else
                                    sweeps.sellsideLabel := pool.l_abel
                                    sweeps.sellsideLabelIndex := bar_index
                                0

                        if pool.isHigh
                            currentAlerts.buysideLiquiditySweep := true
                            sweeps.lastBuysideIndex := bar_index
                        else
                            currentAlerts.sellsideLiquiditySweep := true
                            sweeps.lastSellsideIndex := bar_index
                        // same if/else return-type rule as in deleteFairValueGaps(): this branch would end
                        // on a series int and the next one on a series bool
                        0
                    else if bar_index - pool.piercedIndex >= sweepWindowInput
                        discardPool := true
                        0

                if discardPool
                    pool.l_ine.delete()
                    pool.l_abel.delete()
                    liquidityLevels.remove(index)

// @function            store current structure and trailing swing points, and also display swing points and equal highs/lows
// @returns             void
getCurrentStructure(int size, bool equalHighLow = false, bool internal = false) =>
    currentLeg = leg(size)
    newPivot = startOfNewLeg(currentLeg)
    pivotLow = startOfBullishLeg(currentLeg)
    pivotHigh = startOfBearishLeg(currentLeg)

    if newPivot
        if pivotLow
            pivot p_ivot = equalHighLow ? equalLow : internal ? internalLow : swingLow

            // [IDX1] tolerance floored at N ticks on IDX
            if equalHighLow and math.abs(p_ivot.currentLevel - low[size]) <= equalTolerance(low[size])
                drawEqualHighLow(p_ivot, low[size], size, false)

            p_ivot.lastLevel := p_ivot.currentLevel
            p_ivot.currentLevel := low[size]
            p_ivot.crossed := false
            p_ivot.barTime := time[size]
            p_ivot.barIndex := bar_index[size]

            if not equalHighLow and not internal
                trailing.bottom := p_ivot.currentLevel
                trailing.barTime := p_ivot.barTime
                trailing.barIndex := p_ivot.barIndex
                trailing.lastBottomTime := p_ivot.barTime

                // [C1] every confirmed swing low is resting sellside liquidity
                if sweepSwingPoolsInput
                    registerLiquidityLevel(p_ivot.currentLevel, p_ivot.barTime, false, 'Swing L')

            if showSwingsInput and not internal and not equalHighLow
                drawLabel(time[size], p_ivot.currentLevel, p_ivot.currentLevel < p_ivot.lastLevel ? 'LL' : 'HL', swingBullishColor, label.style_label_up)
        else
            pivot p_ivot = equalHighLow ? equalHigh : internal ? internalHigh : swingHigh

            if equalHighLow and math.abs(p_ivot.currentLevel - high[size]) <= equalTolerance(high[size])
                drawEqualHighLow(p_ivot, high[size], size, true)

            p_ivot.lastLevel := p_ivot.currentLevel
            p_ivot.currentLevel := high[size]
            p_ivot.crossed := false
            p_ivot.barTime := time[size]
            p_ivot.barIndex := bar_index[size]

            if not equalHighLow and not internal
                trailing.top := p_ivot.currentLevel
                trailing.barTime := p_ivot.barTime
                trailing.barIndex := p_ivot.barIndex
                trailing.lastTopTime := p_ivot.barTime

                // [C1] every confirmed swing high is resting buyside liquidity
                if sweepSwingPoolsInput
                    registerLiquidityLevel(p_ivot.currentLevel, p_ivot.barTime, true, 'Swing H')

            if showSwingsInput and not internal and not equalHighLow
                drawLabel(time[size], p_ivot.currentLevel, p_ivot.currentLevel > p_ivot.lastLevel ? 'HH' : 'LH', swingBearishColor, label.style_label_down)

// @function            draw line and label representing a structure
// @returns             float
drawStructure(pivot p_ivot, string tag, color structureColor, string lineStyle, string labelStyle, string labelSize) =>
    var line l_ine = line.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time)
    var label l_abel = label.new(na, na)

    if modeInput == PRESENT
        l_ine.delete()
        l_abel.delete()

    l_ine := line.new(chart.point.new(p_ivot.barTime, na, p_ivot.currentLevel), chart.point.new(time, na, p_ivot.currentLevel), xloc.bar_time, color = structureColor, style = lineStyle)
    l_abel := label.new(chart.point.new(na, math.round(0.5 * (p_ivot.barIndex + bar_index)), p_ivot.currentLevel), tag, xloc.bar_index, color = color(na), textcolor = structureColor, style = labelStyle, size = labelSize)
    0.0

// @function            raise the matching order block alert flag
// @param broken        [E29] false = mitigated (mean threshold reached), true = traded fully through
// @returns             void
setOrderBlockAlert(int bias, bool internal, bool broken) =>
    if broken
        if bias == BEARISH
            if internal
                currentAlerts.internalBearishOrderBlockBroken := true
            else
                currentAlerts.swingBearishOrderBlockBroken := true
        else
            if internal
                currentAlerts.internalBullishOrderBlockBroken := true
            else
                currentAlerts.swingBullishOrderBlockBroken := true
    else
        if bias == BEARISH
            if internal
                currentAlerts.internalBearishOrderBlock := true
            else
                currentAlerts.swingBearishOrderBlock := true
        else
            if internal
                currentAlerts.internalBullishOrderBlock := true
            else
                currentAlerts.swingBullishOrderBlock := true

// @function            calculate mitigation depth for order block
// @returns             float mitigation depth
// [A1] This used to read raw high/low unconditionally, which meant the "Order Block Mitigation" input
//      (Close vs High/Low) was only honoured by the legacy deletion path in updateOrderBlocks(). Since
//      the status-based system defaults ON, the input did nothing at all under stock settings.
//      It now uses the same two sources the legacy path does, so both modes agree:
//        High/Low - a wick into the zone counts (original behaviour, still the default)
//        Close    - the bar must close inside the zone
calculateMitigationDepth(orderBlock ob) =>
    float depth = 0.0
    float obRange = ob.barHigh - ob.barLow

    if ob.bias == BEARISH
        // For bearish OB, depth is how much price has penetrated upward from the bottom of the zone
        if bearishOrderBlockMitigationSource > ob.barLow
            depth := obRange > 0 ? (bearishOrderBlockMitigationSource - ob.barLow) / obRange : 0.0
    else if ob.bias == BULLISH
        // For bullish OB, depth is how much price has penetrated downward from the top of the zone
        if bullishOrderBlockMitigationSource < ob.barHigh
            depth := obRange > 0 ? (ob.barHigh - bullishOrderBlockMitigationSource) / obRange : 0.0

    math.max(0.0, depth)

// @function            update order block status based on price action
// @returns             void
updateOrderBlockStatus(orderBlock ob) =>
    // [B6] never evaluate an order block on the bar that created it. The displacement that
    //      produced the block would otherwise instantly "mitigate" it.
    if enableStatusBasedSystemInput and not na(ob.creationTime) and time > ob.creationTime
        // Check if price has entered the zone for the first time
        // [A1] Same two sources as calculateMitigationDepth(), so zone entry and mitigation depth cannot
        //      disagree about whether price is in the block. In High/Low mode the pair resolves to
        //      high/low and this is byte-for-byte the original test; in Close mode both resolve to close.
        //      NOTE: zoneEntered and entryTime are currently written but never read anywhere in the
        //      script. This is kept consistent so the fields are correct if they are ever wired up.
        if not ob.zoneEntered
            if bearishOrderBlockMitigationSource >= ob.barLow and bullishOrderBlockMitigationSource <= ob.barHigh
                ob.zoneEntered := true
                ob.entryTime := time

        // Calculate current mitigation depth (running maximum)
        currentDepth = calculateMitigationDepth(ob)
        ob.mitigationDepth := math.max(ob.mitigationDepth, currentDepth)

        // [B5] `else if` so PARTIAL survives at least one bar instead of collapsing straight to MITIGATED
        if ob.status == ACTIVE and ob.mitigationDepth > 0
            ob.status := PARTIAL
            // ICT mean threshold of the block. Computed here, not drawn yet.
            if na(ob.partialMitigationLevel)
                if ob.bias == BEARISH
                    ob.partialMitigationLevel := ob.barLow + (ob.barHigh - ob.barLow) * partialMitigationThresholdInput
                else
                    ob.partialMitigationLevel := ob.barHigh - (ob.barHigh - ob.barLow) * partialMitigationThresholdInput
            // normalise the branch value: this one would end on a float, the next on an int
            0
        else if ob.status == PARTIAL and ob.mitigationDepth >= partialMitigationThresholdInput
            ob.status := MITIGATED

        // [P5] Invalidation is judged by its own source, not by mitigation depth. With the default Close
        //      setting a wick straight through the block leaves it MITIGATED (depth can read above 100%),
        //      and only a close beyond the far edge makes it INVALID, regardless of the path taken.
        bool invalidated = ob.bias == BEARISH ? bearishOrderBlockInvalidationSource > ob.barHigh : bullishOrderBlockInvalidationSource < ob.barLow
        if invalidated
            ob.status := INVALID

// @function            order block management, handles both the status-based and the legacy deletion-based mode
// @param internal      true for internal order blocks
// @returns             void
// [B4]  iterates backwards so array.remove() cannot skip the following element
// [B12] replaces the old deleteOrderBlocks() + advancedUpdateOrderBlocks() pair
updateOrderBlocks(bool internal = false) =>
    array<orderBlock> orderBlocks = internal ? internalOrderBlocks : swingOrderBlocks

    if orderBlocks.size() > 0
        for index = orderBlocks.size() - 1 to 0
            orderBlock eachOrderBlock = orderBlocks.get(index)
            bool removeOrderBlock = false

            // [R1] Retest: a wick into the block and a close back out on its bias side. Checked against the
            //      status at the START of the bar, before this bar's mitigation is applied, so a retest that also
            //      mitigates (and removes) the block still counts. Never on the creation bar [B6], once per block,
            //      and only while the block is untouched or partially filled.
            bool retestEligible = not eachOrderBlock.retested and not na(eachOrderBlock.creationTime) and time > eachOrderBlock.creationTime and (eachOrderBlock.status == ACTIVE or eachOrderBlock.status == PARTIAL)
            if retestEligible
                if eachOrderBlock.bias == BULLISH and low <= eachOrderBlock.barHigh and close > eachOrderBlock.barHigh
                    eachOrderBlock.retested := true
                    currentAlerts.bullishOBRetest := true
                else if eachOrderBlock.bias == BEARISH and high >= eachOrderBlock.barLow and close < eachOrderBlock.barLow
                    eachOrderBlock.retested := true
                    currentAlerts.bearishOBRetest := true

            if enableStatusBasedSystemInput
                int previousStatus = eachOrderBlock.status
                updateOrderBlockStatus(eachOrderBlock)

                // Alert on the status TRANSITION, so it still fires when mitigated blocks are kept visible
                // [E29] mitigation and invalidation are now two distinct events
                if eachOrderBlock.status != previousStatus
                    if eachOrderBlock.status == MITIGATED
                        setOrderBlockAlert(eachOrderBlock.bias, internal, false)
                    else if eachOrderBlock.status == INVALID
                        setOrderBlockAlert(eachOrderBlock.bias, internal, true)

                if eachOrderBlock.status == INVALID or (eachOrderBlock.status == MITIGATED and not keepMitigatedOBInput)
                    removeOrderBlock := true
            else
                // the legacy path only triggers once price is entirely past the block, which is a break
                // [P5] a break is an invalidation, so it follows the invalidation source
                if bearishOrderBlockInvalidationSource > eachOrderBlock.barHigh and eachOrderBlock.bias == BEARISH
                    setOrderBlockAlert(BEARISH, internal, true)
                    removeOrderBlock := true
                else if bullishOrderBlockInvalidationSource < eachOrderBlock.barLow and eachOrderBlock.bias == BULLISH
                    setOrderBlockAlert(BULLISH, internal, true)
                    removeOrderBlock := true

            if removeOrderBlock
                orderBlocks.remove(index)

// @function            fetch and store order blocks
// @returns             void
storeOrderBlock(pivot p_ivot, bool internal = false, int bias) =>
    if not internal and showSwingOrderBlocksInput or internal and showInternalOrderBlocksInput

        // [D16] this is the only place that indexes the storage arrays by bar_index, so it is the only place
        //       that has to compensate for trimming. arrayOffset is 0 on any chart below MAX_STORED_BARS.
        //       startPos is clamped in case the pivot is older than the retained window.
        int startPos = math.max(0, p_ivot.barIndex - arrayOffset)
        int endPos = bar_index - arrayOffset

        if endPos > startPos
            // [P1][IDX2] Locate the leg extreme first: the highest high (bearish) or lowest low (bullish) between
            //            the pivot and the breaking bar. This is the origin of the impulse, and both detection
            //            methods now anchor on it. A manual scan replaces slice().max()/min() so limit-locked
            //            bars can be skipped; with no locked bars it returns the same bar (first occurrence).
            int extremePos = na
            for pos = startPos to endPos - 1
                if not limitLockedBars.get(pos)
                    if na(extremePos)
                        extremePos := pos
                    else if bias == BEARISH ? parsedHighs.get(pos) > parsedHighs.get(extremePos) : parsedLows.get(pos) < parsedLows.get(extremePos)
                        extremePos := pos

            int parsedPos = na
            bool useRawCandle = false

            // [I23][P1] Last Opposite Candle: the last candle whose body opposes the break, taken at the ORIGIN
            //           of the impulse. The walk-back starts OB_ORIGIN_LOOKAHEAD bars after the extreme (so a
            //           higher-low / lower-high opposing candle just before the impulse still qualifies) instead
            //           of at the breaking bar, which used to return a small pullback candle mid-impulse.
            if obDetectionInput == OB_LAST_OPPOSITE and not na(extremePos)
                int searchFrom = math.min(endPos - 1, extremePos + OB_ORIGIN_LOOKAHEAD)
                for pos = searchFrom to startPos
                    float candleOpen = opens.get(pos)
                    float candleClose = closes.get(pos)
                    bool opposite = bias == BULLISH ? candleClose < candleOpen : candleClose > candleOpen

                    if opposite and not limitLockedBars.get(pos)
                        parsedPos := pos
                        useRawCandle := true
                        break

            // Extreme Candle, and the fallback when no opposing candle was found
            if na(parsedPos)
                parsedPos := extremePos

            // [IDX2] na only when every bar of the leg was limit-locked: there is no tradeable candle, so no block
            if not na(parsedPos)
                // [I23] The Extreme Candle search relies on parsedHigh/parsedLow, whose high/low swap on
                //       volatile bars exists to steer that search away from spikes. Once a specific candle has
                //       been named by body direction that filtering is not wanted, so read its real range.
                // [B10] The parsed values can still come back swapped, giving barHigh < barLow and a negative
                //       range in calculateMitigationDepth(). Clamp either way.
                float rawA = useRawCandle ? highs.get(parsedPos) : parsedHighs.get(parsedPos)
                float rawB = useRawCandle ? lows.get(parsedPos) : parsedLows.get(parsedPos)
                float obHigh = math.max(rawA, rawB)
                float obLow = math.min(rawA, rawB)

                // [C2] placement filter: buy from discount, sell from premium, relative to the current swing
                //      dealing range. Skipped while the range is not yet defined.
                float equilibrium = math.avg(trailing.top, trailing.bottom)
                float obMid = math.avg(obHigh, obLow)
                bool placementOk = not obPremiumDiscountFilterInput or na(equilibrium) or (bias == BULLISH ? obMid <= equilibrium : obMid >= equilibrium)

                if placementOk
                    orderBlock o_rderBlock = orderBlock.new(obHigh, obLow, times.get(parsedPos), bias)
                    o_rderBlock.creationTime := time

                    array<orderBlock> orderBlocks = internal ? internalOrderBlocks : swingOrderBlocks

                    if orderBlocks.size() >= 100
                        orderBlocks.pop()
                    orderBlocks.unshift(o_rderBlock)

// @function            draw order blocks as boxes with status visualization
// @returns             void
drawOrderBlocks(bool internal = false) =>
    array<orderBlock> orderBlocks = internal ? internalOrderBlocks : swingOrderBlocks
    array<box> b_oxes = internal ? internalOrderBlocksBoxes : swingOrderBlocksBoxes
    array<box> c_onfirmationBoxes = internal ? internalOrderBlocksConfirmationBoxes : swingOrderBlocksConfirmationBoxes

    orderBlocksSize = orderBlocks.size()
    maxOrderBlocks = internal ? internalOrderBlocksSizeInput : swingOrderBlocksSizeInput
    int drawnCount = math.min(math.min(maxOrderBlocks, orderBlocksSize), b_oxes.size())

    if drawnCount > 0
        array<orderBlock> parsedOrdeBlocks = orderBlocks.slice(0, drawnCount)

        for [index, eachOrderBlock] in parsedOrdeBlocks
            // Base color selection
            baseColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? eachOrderBlock.bias == BEARISH ? color.new(MONO_BEARISH, 80) : color.new(MONO_BULLISH, 80) : internal ? eachOrderBlock.bias == BEARISH ? internalBearishOrderBlockColor : internalBullishOrderBlockColor : eachOrderBlock.bias == BEARISH ? swingBearishOrderBlockColor : swingBullishOrderBlockColor

            // Adjust color based on status
            color orderBlockColor = baseColor
            if enableStatusBasedSystemInput
                orderBlockColor := switch eachOrderBlock.status
                    PARTIAL => color.new(baseColor, 90)
                    MITIGATED => color.new(baseColor, 95)
                    INVALID => color.new(baseColor, 98)
                    => baseColor

            // Draw main order block
            box b_ox = b_oxes.get(index)
            b_ox.set_top_left_point(chart.point.new(eachOrderBlock.barTime, na, eachOrderBlock.barHigh))
            b_ox.set_bottom_right_point(chart.point.new(last_bar_time, na, eachOrderBlock.barLow))
            b_ox.set_border_color(internal ? na : orderBlockColor)
            b_ox.set_bgcolor(orderBlockColor)

            // [D9] Confirmation zone: a forward projection drawn only for blocks price has not touched yet.
            //      Now visible because the main box stops at the last bar. Uses bar_index, so its width is
            //      an exact bar count rather than a time span that a weekend would distort.
            if index < c_onfirmationBoxes.size()
                box c_ox = c_onfirmationBoxes.get(index)

                if showConfirmationZonesEffective and enableStatusBasedSystemInput and eachOrderBlock.status == ACTIVE
                    c_ox.set_lefttop(last_bar_index, eachOrderBlock.barHigh)
                    c_ox.set_rightbottom(last_bar_index + confirmationZoneExtensionInput, eachOrderBlock.barLow)
                    c_ox.set_border_color(na)
                    c_ox.set_bgcolor(color.new(baseColor, confirmationZoneOpacityInput))
                else
                    // [D17] a block that has since left ACTIVE would otherwise keep its old projection
                    c_ox.set_border_color(na)
                    c_ox.set_bgcolor(na)

            // [T2] Status is now TEXT INSIDE THE BOX instead of a floating label: one drawing fewer per block,
            //      no text hanging in mid-air over the candles, and the 500-label budget is freed for structure
            //      labels. Right-aligned so it sits at the live edge of the block, and tucked against the edge
            //      that faces away from price.
            if showOBStatusEffective and enableStatusBasedSystemInput
                string statusText = switch eachOrderBlock.status
                    ACTIVE => 'ACTIVE'
                    PARTIAL => 'PARTIAL'
                    MITIGATED => 'MITIGATED'
                    INVALID => 'INVALID'
                    => 'UNKNOWN'

                if eachOrderBlock.status == PARTIAL or eachOrderBlock.status == MITIGATED
                    depthPercent = math.round(eachOrderBlock.mitigationDepth * 100)
                    statusText := statusText + ' ' + str.tostring(depthPercent) + '%'

                b_ox.set_text(statusText)
                b_ox.set_text_size(size.tiny)
                b_ox.set_text_halign(text.align_right)
                b_ox.set_text_valign(eachOrderBlock.bias == BEARISH ? text.align_top : text.align_bottom)
                b_ox.set_text_color(color.new(eachOrderBlock.status == ACTIVE ? chart.fg_color : GRAY, 25))
            else
                b_ox.set_text('')

    // [C14][D17] hide every pooled drawing that no longer maps to a live order block. Without this they keep
    //            rendering at whatever coordinates they were last given, which is the ghost-box symptom.
    if drawnCount < b_oxes.size()
        for index = drawnCount to b_oxes.size() - 1
            box b_ox = b_oxes.get(index)
            b_ox.set_bgcolor(na)
            b_ox.set_border_color(na)
            b_ox.set_text('')

            if index < c_onfirmationBoxes.size()
                box c_ox = c_onfirmationBoxes.get(index)
                c_ox.set_bgcolor(na)
                c_ox.set_border_color(na)

// @function            [C3] extreme of the stored leg from `fromIndex` up to and including the current bar
// @param fromIndex     bar_index where the leg starts (the broken pivot)
// @param findHigh      true for the highest high, false for the lowest low
// @returns             [price, bar time, bar_index], all na when the leg is outside the retained window
legExtreme(int fromIndex, bool findHigh) =>
    // [D16] same trimming compensation as storeOrderBlock(). The current bar is already stored, so endPos is valid.
    int startPos = math.max(0, fromIndex - arrayOffset)
    int endPos = bar_index - arrayOffset
    float best = na
    int bestPos = na

    if endPos >= startPos
        for pos = startPos to endPos
            float value = findHigh ? highs.get(pos) : lows.get(pos)
            if na(best) or (findHigh ? value > best : value < best)
                best := value
                bestPos := pos

    [best, na(bestPos) ? int(na) : times.get(bestPos), na(bestPos) ? int(na) : bestPos + arrayOffset]

// @function            [C3] emit one structure break: alerts, trend state, sweep pairing, drawing, order block and
//                      the protected level of the new leg. Shared by the pivot breaks and the strict CHoCH, so the
//                      two paths cannot drift apart.
// @returns             int (0), so callers can use it as the last statement of an if branch
emitStructureBreak(pivot p_ivot, trend t_rend, bool internal, int bias, string tag, bool hide, color structureColor, string lineStyle, string labelSize) =>
    bool bull = bias == BULLISH

    // [G26] Hide mode suppresses the signal but not the state change below
    if not hide
        if internal
            if bull
                currentAlerts.internalBullishCHoCH := tag == CHOCH
                currentAlerts.internalBullishBOS := tag == BOS
            else
                currentAlerts.internalBearishCHoCH := tag == CHOCH
                currentAlerts.internalBearishBOS := tag == BOS
        else
            if bull
                currentAlerts.swingBullishCHoCH := tag == CHOCH
                currentAlerts.swingBullishBOS := tag == BOS
            else
                currentAlerts.swingBearishCHoCH := tag == CHOCH
                currentAlerts.swingBearishBOS := tag == BOS

    p_ivot.crossed := true
    t_rend.bias := bias

    // [F24] a CHoCH shortly after the opposite-side liquidity was raided is the reversal setup. The sweep is
    //       consumed so the alert fires once per raid rather than on every later CHoCH.
    if showLiquiditySweepsInput and not hide and tag == CHOCH
        if bull and not na(sweeps.lastSellsideIndex) and bar_index - sweeps.lastSellsideIndex <= sweepReversalWindowInput
            currentAlerts.bullishSweepReversal := true
            sweeps.lastSellsideIndex := na
        else if not bull and not na(sweeps.lastBuysideIndex) and bar_index - sweeps.lastBuysideIndex <= sweepReversalWindowInput
            currentAlerts.bearishSweepReversal := true
            sweeps.lastBuysideIndex := na

    // [A2] display filter per side and per structure type
    string showSide = bull ? (internal ? showInternalBullInput : showSwingBullInput) : (internal ? showInternalBearInput : showSwingBearInput)
    bool displayCondition = (internal ? showInternalsEffective : showStructureInput) and (showSide == ALL or showSide == BOS and tag != CHOCH or showSide == CHOCH and tag == CHOCH) and not hide

    if displayCondition
        drawStructure(p_ivot, tag, structureColor, lineStyle, bull ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up, labelSize)

    // [G26] parenthesised deliberately: `a and b or c and d` would otherwise bind `not hide` to the last term only
    if (internal and showInternalOrderBlocksInput or not internal and showSwingOrderBlocksInput) and not hide
        storeOrderBlock(p_ivot, internal, bias)

    // [C3] The protected level of the NEW trend leg: after a bullish break, the lowest low between the broken
    //      pivot and this bar (the origin of the move); after a bearish break, the highest high. Recomputed on
    //      every break, so a BOS in the same direction trails it forward.
    if strictChochInput
        [extremePrice, extremeTime, extremeIndex] = legExtreme(p_ivot.barIndex, not bull)
        if bull
            t_rend.protectedLow := extremePrice
            t_rend.protectedLowTime := extremeTime
            t_rend.protectedLowIndex := extremeIndex
        else
            t_rend.protectedHigh := extremePrice
            t_rend.protectedHighTime := extremeTime
            t_rend.protectedHighIndex := extremeIndex
    0

// @function            detect and draw structures, also detect and store order blocks
// @returns             void
//
// [E22] REJECTION SEMANTICS, worth understanding before enabling the displacement filter.
//       A break that fails the filter is rejected outright: the pivot is NOT marked as crossed, the trend
//       bias does NOT flip, no line or label is drawn, and no order block is stored. The level stays live.
//       Because ta.crossover() only fires on the transition, a rejected break is not re-evaluated on the
//       following bars while price stays above the level. It is re-armed if price drops back below and
//       crosses again, which is the desirable outcome: a retest that arrives with displacement counts,
//       while the first weak poke does not.
//       The alternative design (flip the trend but hide the drawing) was rejected because it would let a
//       break the filter just called invalid still decide whether the NEXT break is labelled BOS or CHoCH.
//
// [C3] STRICT CHoCH SEMANTICS. Against the trend, a pivot break that does not also close beyond the protected
//       level is a MINOR break: the pivot is consumed (crossed) so it cannot fire later, but nothing is emitted
//       and the trend does not flip. The CHoCH fires when price closes through the protected level itself,
//       drawn from that level, with its order block searched in the leg that starts there. With the filter on,
//       only a CHoCH can be minor; a BOS in the trend direction is unaffected.
displayStructure(bool internal = false) =>
    var bullishBar = true
    var bearishBar = true

    // [B7] the original compared the upper wick against math.min(close, open - low), mixing a price with a range.
    //      The ICT intent is upper wick vs lower wick.
    if internalFilterConfluenceInput
        bullishBar := high - math.max(close, open) > math.min(close, open) - low
        bearishBar := high - math.max(close, open) < math.min(close, open) - low

    pivot p_ivot = internal ? internalHigh : swingHigh
    trend t_rend = internal ? internalTrend : swingTrend

    lineStyle = internal ? line.style_dashed : line.style_solid
    labelSize = internal ? internalStructureSize : swingStructureSize

    // [C3] Evaluated unconditionally at the top, before either branch can move the protected levels, so the
    //      ta.* history of both calls stays continuous on every bar.
    bool protectedHighBroken = ta.crossover(close, t_rend.protectedHigh)
    bool protectedLowBroken = ta.crossunder(close, t_rend.protectedLow)

    // [E22] scope the displacement filter to internal, swing, or both
    bool filterApplies = displacementFilterInput != DISPLACEMENT_OFF and (displacementApplyToInput == APPLY_BOTH or internal and displacementApplyToInput == APPLY_INTERNAL or not internal and displacementApplyToInput == APPLY_SWING)
    bool bullishBreakAllowed = not filterApplies or bullishDisplacement
    bool bearishBreakAllowed = not filterApplies or bearishDisplacement

    // [G26] Counter-bias handling. Note these are NOT folded into extraCondition: unlike the displacement
    //       filter, a counter-bias break is still a real break and must update the structure state.
    bool bullishAgainstHTF = htfBiasEnabled and htfBias == BEARISH
    bool bearishAgainstHTF = htfBiasEnabled and htfBias == BULLISH
    bool hideBullish = bullishAgainstHTF and htfBiasFilterInput == HTF_HIDE
    bool hideBearish = bearishAgainstHTF and htfBiasFilterInput == HTF_HIDE

    extraCondition = (internal ? internalHigh.currentLevel != swingHigh.currentLevel and bullishBar : true) and bullishBreakAllowed
    bullishBaseColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? MONO_BULLISH : internal ? internalBullColorInput : swingBullColorInput
    // [G26] Dim mode only changes how it is drawn
    bullishColor = bullishAgainstHTF and htfBiasFilterInput == HTF_DIM ? color.new(bullishBaseColor, htfBiasDimOpacityInput) : bullishBaseColor

    // ta.crossover() stays the FIRST operand: v6 evaluates `and` lazily, and a ta.* call that is skipped on some
    // bars loses its history
    if ta.crossover(close, p_ivot.currentLevel) and not p_ivot.crossed and extraCondition
        // [C3] minor break: against a downtrend, short of the protected high
        bool minorBreak = strictChochInput and t_rend.bias == BEARISH and not na(t_rend.protectedHigh) and close <= t_rend.protectedHigh

        if minorBreak
            p_ivot.crossed := true
            0
        else
            emitStructureBreak(p_ivot, t_rend, internal, BULLISH, t_rend.bias == BEARISH ? CHOCH : BOS, hideBullish, bullishColor, lineStyle, labelSize)

    // [C3] strict CHoCH: the protected high itself is closed through while the trend is still bearish. A pivot
    //      break on this same bar has already flipped the trend above, so the two cannot both fire.
    if strictChochInput and protectedHighBroken and t_rend.bias == BEARISH and (internal ? bullishBar : true) and bullishBreakAllowed
        pivot protectedPivot = pivot.new(t_rend.protectedHigh, na, false, t_rend.protectedHighTime, t_rend.protectedHighIndex)
        emitStructureBreak(protectedPivot, t_rend, internal, BULLISH, CHOCH, hideBullish, bullishColor, lineStyle, labelSize)

    // [A1] the duplicated / truncated block that used to sit here has been removed
    p_ivot := internal ? internalLow : swingLow
    extraCondition := (internal ? internalLow.currentLevel != swingLow.currentLevel and bearishBar : true) and bearishBreakAllowed
    bearishBaseColor = styleInput == MONOCHROME ? MONO_BEARISH : internal ? internalBearColorInput : swingBearColorInput
    // [G26] Dim mode only changes how it is drawn
    bearishColor = bearishAgainstHTF and htfBiasFilterInput == HTF_DIM ? color.new(bearishBaseColor, htfBiasDimOpacityInput) : bearishBaseColor

    if ta.crossunder(close, p_ivot.currentLevel) and not p_ivot.crossed and extraCondition
        // [C3] minor break: against an uptrend, short of the protected low
        bool minorBreak = strictChochInput and t_rend.bias == BULLISH and not na(t_rend.protectedLow) and close >= t_rend.protectedLow

        if minorBreak
            p_ivot.crossed := true
            0
        else
            emitStructureBreak(p_ivot, t_rend, internal, BEARISH, t_rend.bias == BULLISH ? CHOCH : BOS, hideBearish, bearishColor, lineStyle, labelSize)

    // [C3] mirror of the bullish strict CHoCH
    if strictChochInput and protectedLowBroken and t_rend.bias == BULLISH and (internal ? bearishBar : true) and bearishBreakAllowed
        pivot protectedPivot = pivot.new(t_rend.protectedLow, na, false, t_rend.protectedLowTime, t_rend.protectedLowIndex)
        emitStructureBreak(protectedPivot, t_rend, internal, BEARISH, CHOCH, hideBearish, bearishColor, lineStyle, labelSize)

// @function            draw one fair value gap box (each fair value gap has two boxes)
// @returns             box ID
fairValueGapBox(leftTime, rightTime, topPrice, bottomPrice, boxColor) =>
    box.new(chart.point.new(leftTime, na, topPrice), chart.point.new(rightTime + fairValueGapsExtendInput * barDurationMs, na, bottomPrice), xloc = xloc.bar_time, border_color = boxColor, bgcolor = boxColor)

// @function            resolve fair value gaps that price has traded through
// @returns             void
// [B4]  iterates backwards so array.remove() cannot skip the following element
// [I25] A gap now has two lives. The first time price trades through it, it flips role and becomes an
//       inverse gap: bias reversed, recoloured, kept on the chart. Only the second invalidation, in its new
//       direction, removes it. With IFVG switched off the first invalidation deletes it as before.
//       Because the bias is flipped in place, the same invalidation test covers the second life too.
deleteFairValueGaps() =>
    if fairValueGaps.size() > 0
        for index = fairValueGaps.size() - 1 to 0
            fairValueGap eachFairValueGap = fairValueGaps.get(index)

            bool invalidate = (fairValueGapDownSource < eachFairValueGap.bottom and eachFairValueGap.bias == BULLISH) or (fairValueGapUpSource > eachFairValueGap.top and eachFairValueGap.bias == BEARISH)

            if invalidate
                if showIFVGInput and not eachFairValueGap.inverted
                    eachFairValueGap.inverted := true
                    eachFairValueGap.bias := eachFairValueGap.bias == BULLISH ? BEARISH : BULLISH
                    // [R1] a new role earns its own retest
                    eachFairValueGap.retested := false

                    color invertedColor = eachFairValueGap.bias == BULLISH ? ifvgBullishColorInput : ifvgBearishColorInput
                    eachFairValueGap.topBox.set_border_color(invertedColor)
                    eachFairValueGap.topBox.set_bgcolor(invertedColor)
                    eachFairValueGap.bottomBox.set_border_color(invertedColor)
                    eachFairValueGap.bottomBox.set_bgcolor(invertedColor)

                    if eachFairValueGap.bias == BULLISH
                        currentAlerts.bullishIFVG := true
                    else
                        currentAlerts.bearishIFVG := true
                    // Pine treats if/else as an expression whose value is the last statement of each
                    // branch, and both branches must agree on type. This one would end on a series bool,
                    // the other on the fairValueGap that array.remove() hands back. Discard both.
                    0
                else
                    eachFairValueGap.topBox.delete()
                    eachFairValueGap.bottomBox.delete()
                    fairValueGaps.remove(index)
                    0
            // [R1] Retest: a wick into the gap and a close back out on its bias side, once per role. Gaps created
            //      on this bar are only added later by drawFairValueGaps(), so the creation bar is never tested.
            //      Relies on top > bottom for both biases, see [R3].
            else if not eachFairValueGap.retested
                if eachFairValueGap.bias == BULLISH and low <= eachFairValueGap.top and close > eachFairValueGap.top
                    eachFairValueGap.retested := true
                    currentAlerts.bullishFVGRetest := true
                else if eachFairValueGap.bias == BEARISH and high >= eachFairValueGap.bottom and close < eachFairValueGap.bottom
                    eachFairValueGap.retested := true
                    currentAlerts.bearishFVGRetest := true
                0

// @function            draw fair value gaps
// @returns             void
drawFairValueGaps() =>
    // [E18] Two requests: the original, and one shifted back a whole HTF bar.
    //       `expr[1]` with lookahead_on is the standard non-repainting HTF idiom, so shifting every index
    //       by one reads only CONFIRMED bars. Selecting between two ready-made tuples avoids putting a
    //       variable historical offset inside request.security(), which is fragile.
    [repClose, repOpen, repTime, repHigh, repLow, repCurTime, rep2High, rep2Low] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, fairValueGapsTimeframeInput, [close[1], open[1], time[1], high[0], low[0], time[0], high[2], low[2]], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
    [nrpClose, nrpOpen, nrpTime, nrpHigh, nrpLow, nrpCurTime, nrp2High, nrp2Low] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, fairValueGapsTimeframeInput, [close[2], open[2], time[2], high[1], low[1], time[1], high[3], low[3]], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)

    // no leak exists when the gap timeframe is the chart timeframe, so the toggle is a no-op there
    bool useNonRepainting = fairValueGapsNonRepaintingInput and fairValueGapsTimeframeInput != ''

    lastClose = useNonRepainting ? nrpClose : repClose
    lastOpen = useNonRepainting ? nrpOpen : repOpen
    lastTime = useNonRepainting ? nrpTime : repTime
    currentHigh = useNonRepainting ? nrpHigh : repHigh
    currentLow = useNonRepainting ? nrpLow : repLow
    currentTime = useNonRepainting ? nrpCurTime : repCurTime
    last2High = useNonRepainting ? nrp2High : rep2High
    last2Low = useNonRepainting ? nrp2Low : rep2Low

    barDeltaPercent = (lastClose - lastOpen) / (lastOpen * 100)
    newTimeframe = timeframe.change(fairValueGapsTimeframeInput)

    // [A2] The auto threshold is the mean absolute bar delta, doubled. The numerator only ever accumulates
    //      on bars where the FVG timeframe changes, but the divisor used to be bar_index, which counts
    //      CHART bars. The mean was therefore diluted by the ratio between the two timeframes: reading 4H
    //      gaps on a 15m chart divided a 1-in-16 sum by all 16, so the threshold came out roughly 16x too
    //      small and rejected nothing. Auto Threshold was silently inert on every higher timeframe.
    //      Numerator and divisor now count the same population, so the threshold means what it says.
    //      UNCHANGED when the FVG timeframe is the chart timeframe, where newTimeframe is true on every
    //      bar and tfBarCount tracked bar_index anyway.
    //      VISIBLE CHANGE: with a higher FVG timeframe and Auto Threshold on, noticeably fewer gaps.
    var int tfBarCount = 0
    var float tfDeltaSum = 0.0

    if newTimeframe
        tfBarCount += 1
        tfDeltaSum += math.abs(nz(barDeltaPercent))

    threshold = fairValueGapsThresholdInput and tfBarCount > 0 ? tfDeltaSum / tfBarCount * 2 : 0.0

    // [IDX1] on IDX a gap must span at least N ticks of the price grid at its own level
    bool bullishWideEnough = not idxMode or currentLow - last2High >= fvgMinTicksInput * idxTick(last2High)
    bool bearishWideEnough = not idxMode or last2Low - currentHigh >= fvgMinTicksInput * idxTick(currentHigh)

    bullishFairValueGap = currentLow > last2High and lastClose > last2High and barDeltaPercent > threshold and newTimeframe and bullishWideEnough
    bearishFairValueGap = currentHigh < last2Low and lastClose < last2Low and -barDeltaPercent > threshold and newTimeframe and bearishWideEnough

    if bullishFairValueGap
        currentAlerts.bullishFairValueGap := true
        fairValueGaps.unshift(fairValueGap.new(currentLow, last2High, BULLISH, fairValueGapBox(lastTime, currentTime, currentLow, math.avg(currentLow, last2High), fairValueGapBullishColor), fairValueGapBox(lastTime, currentTime, math.avg(currentLow, last2High), last2High, fairValueGapBullishColor)))
    if bearishFairValueGap
        currentAlerts.bearishFairValueGap := true
        // [R3] top/bottom are stored as upper/lower edge, as for bullish gaps. v4.2 stored them the other way
        //      round, so a bearish gap was "invalidated" by the first touch of its lower edge, and the IFVG it
        //      flipped into was deleted again on the next bar.
        fairValueGaps.unshift(fairValueGap.new(last2Low, currentHigh, BEARISH, fairValueGapBox(lastTime, currentTime, currentHigh, math.avg(currentHigh, last2Low), fairValueGapBearishColor), fairValueGapBox(lastTime, currentTime, math.avg(currentHigh, last2Low), last2Low, fairValueGapBearishColor)))

    // [D15] discard the oldest gaps once the cap is reached, deleting their boxes so the 500 box budget
    //       is not silently consumed by drawings the array still holds references to
    while fairValueGaps.size() > fairValueGapsMaxInput
        fairValueGap oldestFairValueGap = fairValueGaps.pop()
        oldestFairValueGap.topBox.delete()
        oldestFairValueGap.bottomBox.delete()

// @function            get line style from string
// @returns             string
getStyle(string style) =>
    switch style
        SOLID => line.style_solid
        DASHED => line.style_dashed
        DOTTED => line.style_dotted

// @function            draw MultiTimeFrame levels
// @returns             void
// [D21] parameter renamed from `timeframe`, which shadowed the built-in namespace of the same name
drawLevels(string tf, bool sameTimeframe, string style, color levelColor) =>
    [topLevel, bottomLevel, leftTime, rightTime] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, tf, [high[1], low[1], time[1], time], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)

    float parsedTop = sameTimeframe ? high : topLevel
    float parsedBottom = sameTimeframe ? low : bottomLevel

    int parsedLeftTime = sameTimeframe ? time : leftTime
    int parsedRightTime = sameTimeframe ? time : rightTime

    int parsedTopTime = time
    int parsedBottomTime = time

    if not sameTimeframe
        // [D16] binary_search_rightmost returns -1 when the target predates the retained window, and after
        //       trimming that is now reachable. Clamp both ends so the slices can never be negative or reversed.
        int leftIndex = math.max(0, times.binary_search_rightmost(parsedLeftTime))
        int rightIndex = math.max(leftIndex, times.binary_search_rightmost(parsedRightTime))

        array<int> timeArray = times.slice(leftIndex, rightIndex)
        array<float> topArray = highs.slice(leftIndex, rightIndex)
        array<float> bottomArray = lows.slice(leftIndex, rightIndex)

        parsedTopTime := timeArray.size() > 0 ? timeArray.get(topArray.indexof(topArray.max())) : initialTime
        parsedBottomTime := timeArray.size() > 0 ? timeArray.get(bottomArray.indexof(bottomArray.min())) : initialTime

    var line topLine = line.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time, color = levelColor, style = getStyle(style))
    var line bottomLine = line.new(na, na, na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time, color = levelColor, style = getStyle(style))
    var label topLabel = label.new(na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time, text = str.format('P{0}H', tf), color = color(na), textcolor = levelColor, size = size.small, style = label.style_label_left)
    var label bottomLabel = label.new(na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time, text = str.format('P{0}L', tf), color = color(na), textcolor = levelColor, size = size.small, style = label.style_label_left)

    // [D9b] stable 20-bar projection instead of 20 * (time - time[1])
    int rightEdgeTime = last_bar_time + 20 * barDurationMs

    topLine.set_first_point(chart.point.new(parsedTopTime, na, parsedTop))
    topLine.set_second_point(chart.point.new(rightEdgeTime, na, parsedTop))
    topLabel.set_point(chart.point.new(rightEdgeTime, na, parsedTop))

    bottomLine.set_first_point(chart.point.new(parsedBottomTime, na, parsedBottom))
    bottomLine.set_second_point(chart.point.new(rightEdgeTime, na, parsedBottom))
    bottomLabel.set_point(chart.point.new(rightEdgeTime, na, parsedBottom))

// @function            true if chart timeframe is higher than provided timeframe
// @returns             bool
// [D21] parameter renamed from `timeframe`, which shadowed the built-in namespace it also calls into
higherTimeframe(string tf) =>
    timeframe.in_seconds() > timeframe.in_seconds(tf)

// @function            update trailing swing points
// @returns             int
updateTrailingExtremes() =>
    trailing.top := math.max(high, trailing.top)
    trailing.lastTopTime := trailing.top == high ? time : trailing.lastTopTime
    trailing.bottom := math.min(low, trailing.bottom)
    trailing.lastBottomTime := trailing.bottom == low ? time : trailing.lastBottomTime
    trailing.lastBottomTime

// @function            draw trailing swing points
// @returns             void
drawHighLowSwings() =>
    var line topLine = line.new(na, na, na, na, color = swingBearishColor, xloc = xloc.bar_time)
    var line bottomLine = line.new(na, na, na, na, color = swingBullishColor, xloc = xloc.bar_time)
    var label topLabel = label.new(na, na, color = color(na), textcolor = swingBearishColor, xloc = xloc.bar_time, style = label.style_label_down, size = size.tiny)
    var label bottomLabel = label.new(na, na, color = color(na), textcolor = swingBullishColor, xloc = xloc.bar_time, style = label.style_label_up, size = size.tiny)

    // [D9b] stable 20-bar projection instead of 20 * (time - time[1])
    rightTimeBar = last_bar_time + 20 * barDurationMs

    topLine.set_first_point(chart.point.new(trailing.lastTopTime, na, trailing.top))
    topLine.set_second_point(chart.point.new(rightTimeBar, na, trailing.top))
    topLabel.set_point(chart.point.new(rightTimeBar, na, trailing.top))
    topLabel.set_text(swingTrend.bias == BEARISH ? 'Strong High' : 'Weak High')

    bottomLine.set_first_point(chart.point.new(trailing.lastBottomTime, na, trailing.bottom))
    bottomLine.set_second_point(chart.point.new(rightTimeBar, na, trailing.bottom))
    bottomLabel.set_point(chart.point.new(rightTimeBar, na, trailing.bottom))
    bottomLabel.set_text(swingTrend.bias == BULLISH ? 'Strong Low' : 'Weak Low')

// @function            [G26] one reused label at the right edge showing the current higher timeframe bias
// @returns             void
drawHTFBiasLabel() =>
    var label biasLabel = label.new(na, na, xloc = xloc.bar_time, color = color(na), style = label.style_label_left, size = size.small)

    string biasText = htfBias == BULLISH ? 'HTF Bullish' : htfBias == BEARISH ? 'HTF Bearish' : 'HTF Neutral'
    color biasColor = htfBias == BULLISH ? swingBullishColor : htfBias == BEARISH ? swingBearishColor : GRAY
    string biasTf = htfBiasTimeframeInput == '' ? timeframe.period : htfBiasTimeframeInput
    // reported even when the filter is off, so the bias can be read before committing to filtering on it
    string biasSuffix = htfBiasEnabled ? '' : ' [inactive]'

    biasLabel.set_point(chart.point.new(last_bar_time + 20 * barDurationMs, na, close))
    biasLabel.set_text(biasText + ' (' + biasTf + ')' + biasSuffix)
    biasLabel.set_textcolor(biasColor)

// @function            draw a zone with a label and a box
// @returns             void
drawZone(float labelLevel, int labelIndex, float top, float bottom, string tag, color zoneColor, string style) =>
    var label l_abel = label.new(na, na, text = tag, color = color(na), textcolor = zoneColor, style = style, size = size.small)
    var box b_ox = box.new(na, na, na, na, bgcolor = color.new(zoneColor, 80), border_color = color(na), xloc = xloc.bar_time)

    b_ox.set_top_left_point(chart.point.new(trailing.barTime, na, top))
    b_ox.set_bottom_right_point(chart.point.new(last_bar_time, na, bottom))

    l_abel.set_point(chart.point.new(na, labelIndex, labelLevel))

// @function            draw premium/discount zones
// @returns             void
drawPremiumDiscountZones() =>
    drawZone(trailing.top, math.round(0.5 * (trailing.barIndex + last_bar_index)), trailing.top, 0.95 * trailing.top + 0.05 * trailing.bottom, 'Premium', premiumZoneColor, label.style_label_down)

    equilibriumLevel = math.avg(trailing.top, trailing.bottom)
    drawZone(equilibriumLevel, last_bar_index, 0.525 * trailing.top + 0.475 * trailing.bottom, 0.525 * trailing.bottom + 0.475 * trailing.top, 'Equilibrium', equilibriumZoneColorInput, label.style_label_left)

    drawZone(trailing.bottom, math.round(0.5 * (trailing.barIndex + last_bar_index)), 0.95 * trailing.bottom + 0.05 * trailing.top, trailing.bottom, 'Discount', discountZoneColor, label.style_label_up)

// @function            [H-MA] pick a simple or exponential average
// @returns             float
movingAverage(float src, int len, string maType) =>
    // both computed on every bar: ta.* calls inside a ternary trigger a consistency warning
    float smaValue = ta.sma(src, len)
    float emaValue = ta.ema(src, len)
    maType == MA_SMA ? smaValue : emaValue

// @function            [H-MA] Kaufman's Adaptive Moving Average
// @param src           price series
// @param len           efficiency ratio lookback
// @param fastLen       fast bound, converted to a smoothing constant
// @param slowLen       slow bound, converted to a smoothing constant
// @returns             float
//
// The efficiency ratio is net movement over the lookback divided by the sum of the individual moves. Near
// 1 the market is travelling in a straight line and the average tracks price closely; near 0 it is chopping
// and the average flattens.
kama(float src, int len, int fastLen, int slowLen) =>
    // same reasoning as the regime block: prefer ta.change() over a variable history index
    float netMove = math.abs(nz(ta.change(src, len)))
    float totalMove = math.sum(math.abs(nz(ta.change(src))), len)
    float efficiencyRatio = totalMove != 0 ? netMove / totalMove : 0.0

    float fastAlpha = 2.0 / (fastLen + 1)
    float slowAlpha = 2.0 / (slowLen + 1)
    float smoothingConstant = math.pow(efficiencyRatio * (fastAlpha - slowAlpha) + slowAlpha, 2)

    var float kamaValue = na
    kamaValue := na(kamaValue[1]) ? src : nz(kamaValue[1]) + smoothingConstant * (src - nz(kamaValue[1]))
    kamaValue

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//MUTABLE VARIABLES & EXECUTION
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
parsedOpen = showTrendInput ? open : na
candleColor = internalTrend.bias == BULLISH ? swingBullishColor : swingBearishColor
plotcandle(parsedOpen, high, low, close, color = candleColor, wickcolor = candleColor, bordercolor = candleColor)

// [C2] the OB placement filter reads the same trailing range, so it needs it kept current too
if showHighLowSwingsInput or showPremiumDiscountZonesInput or obPremiumDiscountFilterInput
    updateTrailingExtremes()

    if showHighLowSwingsInput
        drawHighLowSwings()

    if showPremiumDiscountEffective
        drawPremiumDiscountZones()

if showFairValueGapsInput
    deleteFairValueGaps()

getCurrentStructure(swingsLengthInput, false)
getCurrentStructure(internalStructureLengthInput, false, true) // [E20] was a hardcoded 5

// [F24] sweeps depend on EQH/EQL detection, so either option is enough to run it. The drawing itself
//       stays gated on showEqualHighsLowsInput inside drawEqualHighLow().
if showEqualHighsLowsInput or showLiquiditySweepsInput
    getCurrentStructure(equalHighsLowsLengthInput, true)

// [C1] Previous day / week pools, registered on the first bar of each new period. `[1]` with lookahead_on is
//      the confirmed prior period, the same idiom drawLevels() uses. Registered BEFORE the sweep update so an
//      opening gap through PDH/PWH on that very bar is seen as a pierce.
[prevDayHigh, prevDayLow, prevDayTime] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, 'D', [high[1], low[1], time[1]], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
[prevWeekHigh, prevWeekLow, prevWeekTime] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, 'W', [high[1], low[1], time[1]], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_on)
bool newDayBar = timeframe.change('D')
bool newWeekBar = timeframe.change('W')

if showLiquiditySweepsInput
    if sweepDailyPoolsInput and timeframe.isintraday and newDayBar
        registerLiquidityLevel(prevDayHigh, prevDayTime, true, 'PDH')
        registerLiquidityLevel(prevDayLow, prevDayTime, false, 'PDL')

    if sweepWeeklyPoolsInput and (timeframe.isintraday or timeframe.isdaily) and newWeekBar
        registerLiquidityLevel(prevWeekHigh, prevWeekTime, true, 'PWH')
        registerLiquidityLevel(prevWeekLow, prevWeekTime, false, 'PWL')

// [F24] must run before displayStructure(), so a sweep detected on this bar can be paired with a CHoCH
//       that happens on the same bar
if showLiquiditySweepsInput
    updateLiquiditySweeps()

// [L2] IDX LIMIT MARKERS. Tidy by construction:
//      - label.new at size.tiny, which is smaller than plotshape text (whose font size cannot be set), faded
//      - yloc.abovebar / belowbar, so the marker sits clear of the candle at any zoom
//      - ONE label per streak: consecutive limit days update the first label to "ARB×3" instead of printing
//        "ARBARBARB" side by side
//      - on a bar where a sweep label already sits on the same side, the limit tag is appended to it
//        ("SSL · ARB") instead of drawing a second label on top of it
//      - on bars carrying a limit marker, the MFI triangle on that side is not drawn (see [J-MFI]); a limit day
//        is a money-flow extreme by definition, so nothing is lost
//      - only the most recent N markers are kept, so they cannot eat the 500-label budget that structure
//        labels depend on
var array<label> limitLabels = array.new<label>()
var label araRunLabel = na
var label arbRunLabel = na
var int araRun = 0
var int arbRun = 0

if showLimitBarsInput
    if araTouched
        if araTouched[1] and not na(araRunLabel)
            araRun += 1
            araRunLabel.set_text('ARA×' + str.tostring(araRun))
            araRunLabel.set_tooltip('ARA on ' + str.tostring(araRun) + ' consecutive bars')
        else
            araRun := 1
            araRunLabel := na
            if sweeps.buysideLabelIndex == bar_index and not na(sweeps.buysideLabel)
                sweeps.buysideLabel.set_text(sweeps.buysideLabel.get_text() + ' · ARA')
            else
                araRunLabel := label.new(bar_index, high, 'ARA', xloc.bar_index, yloc.abovebar, color(na), label.style_label_down, color.new(araColorInput, 25), size.tiny, tooltip = 'Upper auto rejection limit reached (ARA ' + str.tostring(araLevel, format.mintick) + ')')
                limitLabels.push(araRunLabel)

    if arbTouched
        if arbTouched[1] and not na(arbRunLabel)
            arbRun += 1
            arbRunLabel.set_text('ARB×' + str.tostring(arbRun))
            arbRunLabel.set_tooltip('ARB on ' + str.tostring(arbRun) + ' consecutive bars')
        else
            arbRun := 1
            arbRunLabel := na
            if sweeps.sellsideLabelIndex == bar_index and not na(sweeps.sellsideLabel)
                sweeps.sellsideLabel.set_text(sweeps.sellsideLabel.get_text() + ' · ARB')
            else
                arbRunLabel := label.new(bar_index, low, 'ARB', xloc.bar_index, yloc.belowbar, color(na), label.style_label_up, color.new(arbColorInput, 25), size.tiny, tooltip = 'Lower auto rejection limit reached (ARB ' + str.tostring(arbLevel, format.mintick) + ')')
                limitLabels.push(arbRunLabel)

    while limitLabels.size() > maxLimitLabelsInput
        label.delete(limitLabels.shift())

if showInternalsInput or showInternalOrderBlocksInput or showTrendInput
    displayStructure(true)

if showStructureInput or showSwingOrderBlocksInput or showHighLowSwingsInput
    displayStructure()

// [B12] one entry point for both mitigation modes
if showInternalOrderBlocksInput
    updateOrderBlocks(true)

if showSwingOrderBlocksInput
    updateOrderBlocks()

if showFairValueGapsInput
    drawFairValueGaps()

if barstate.islastconfirmedhistory or barstate.islast
    if showInternalOrderBlocksInput
        drawOrderBlocks(true)

    if showSwingOrderBlocksInput
        drawOrderBlocks()

    // [G26] single reused label, so this is cheap even on every realtime tick
    if showHTFBiasLabelInput
        drawHTFBiasLabel()

lastBarIndex := currentBarIndex
currentBarIndex := bar_index
newBar = currentBarIndex != lastBarIndex

if barstate.islastconfirmedhistory or barstate.isrealtime and newBar
    if showDailyLevelsInput and not higherTimeframe('D')
        drawLevels('D', timeframe.isdaily, dailyLevelsStyleInput, dailyLevelsColorInput)

    if showWeeklyLevelsInput and not higherTimeframe('W')
        drawLevels('W', timeframe.isweekly, weeklyLevelsStyleInput, weeklyLevelsColorInput)

    if showMonthlyLevelsInput and not higherTimeframe('M')
        drawLevels('M', timeframe.ismonthly, monthlyLevelsStyleInput, monthlyLevelsColorInput)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//MOVING AVERAGES
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [H-MA] Every helper is called unconditionally. kama() carries `var` recursion state, and
//        calling a stateful function on only some bars would corrupt it, so the show-toggles are applied to
//        the plotted value instead. That costs a little computation while the lines are switched off, which
//        is the correct trade against a silently wrong average.
//        None of these feed structure, order blocks, filters or alerts. They are display only.
float ma1Value = movingAverage(maSourceInput, ma1LengthInput, ma1TypeInput)
float ma2Value = movingAverage(maSourceInput, ma2LengthInput, ma2TypeInput)
float ma3Value = movingAverage(maSourceInput, ma3LengthInput, ma3TypeInput)

float kamaLine = kama(maSourceInput, kamaLengthInput, kamaFastInput, kamaSlowInput)

plot(showMA1Input ? ma1Value : na, 'MA 1', color = ma1ColorInput, linewidth = 1)
plot(showMA2Input ? ma2Value : na, 'MA 2', color = ma2ColorInput, linewidth = 1)
plot(showMA3Input ? ma3Value : na, 'MA 3', color = ma3ColorInput, linewidth = 1)

plot(showKamaInput ? kamaLine : na, 'KAMA', color = kamaColorInput, linewidth = 2)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//MONEY FLOW INDEX
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [J-MFI] Display and alerts only. Nothing in the structure, order block, sweep, gap or filter logic reads
//         this, so switching it on cannot alter any existing signal.
//         Markers are plotshape rather than label.new: they are unlimited and cheap, whereas labels would
//         compete for the 500 label budget already shared by structure and order block labels.
float mfiValue = ta.mfi(mfiSourceInput, mfiLengthInput)

bool mfiOverbought = mfiValue > mfiOverboughtInput
bool mfiOversold = mfiValue < mfiOversoldInput

// A symbol with no volume data yields na here, and an na comparison is false, so no markers appear.
bool mfiOverboughtSignal = showMfiSignalsInput and (mfiOnlyOnEntryInput ? mfiOverbought and not mfiOverbought[1] : mfiOverbought)
bool mfiOversoldSignal = showMfiSignalsInput and (mfiOnlyOnEntryInput ? mfiOversold and not mfiOversold[1] : mfiOversold)

// [L2] not drawn where an ARA/ARB marker occupies the same side of the bar; the alerts below are unaffected
// [KRI1] STACKED CANDLE MARKERS. All three indicators share one price-based step, so they sit in fixed slots
//        that keep their order at any zoom (location.abovebar is a pixel offset and cannot be mixed reliably
//        with price offsets):
//          slot 1  MFI triangle   high + 1 step   /  low - 1 step
//          slot 2  RSC dot        high + 2 steps  /  low - 2 steps
//          slot 3  KRI label      text starts beyond high + 3 steps  /  low - 3 steps
float markerStep = nz(ta.atr(14)) * markerSpacingInput

// [T3] ONE MARKER PER SIDE PER BAR. MFI and the optional RSC dots now share a single plotchar per side, with
//      MFI taking priority: nothing stacks on the same bar any more, and the pair costs two plots instead of
//      three, which is what makes room for the RSC curve under Pine's 64-plot limit. The glyph is fixed per
//      plot (Pine requires a const char), so an RSC dot drawn through this slot also appears as a triangle,
//      in the RSC colour and one step further out. Its usual look is the curve.
bool mfiOverboughtMarker = mfiOverboughtSignal and not (showLimitBarsInput and araTouched)
bool mfiOversoldMarker = mfiOversoldSignal and not (showLimitBarsInput and arbTouched)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//RELATIVE STRENGTH COMPARISON
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [RS1] Ratio of this symbol to a benchmark (IDX:LQ45 by default), normalised to 0-100 against its own lookback.
//       Same timeframe as the chart and lookahead_off, so it cannot see the future. Bars where the benchmark has
//       no data (a stock trading on a day the index did not print, or before the index existed) yield na and
//       simply draw nothing. Display, Data Window and alerts only: no signal path reads it.
float rscBenchmarkClose = request.security(rscBenchmarkInput, timeframe.period, close, lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
float rscRatio = rscBenchmarkClose > 0 ? close / rscBenchmarkClose : na
float rscStochRaw = ta.stoch(rscRatio, rscRatio, rscRatio, rscLengthInput)
float rscPercentileRaw = ta.percentrank(rscRatio, rscLengthInput)
// both computed on every bar, then selected: ta.* calls inside a ternary trigger a consistency warning
float rscValue = ta.sma(rscMethodInput == RSC_STOCH ? rscStochRaw : rscPercentileRaw, rscSmoothInput)

bool rscStrong = rscValue > rscUpperInput
bool rscWeak = rscValue < rscLowerInput
bool rscStrongSignal = showRscInput and (rscOnlyOnEntryInput ? rscStrong and not rscStrong[1] : rscStrong)
bool rscWeakSignal = showRscInput and (rscOnlyOnEntryInput ? rscWeak and not rscWeak[1] : rscWeak)

// [T3] THE CURVE. Relative strength is a state that lasts for weeks, so it is drawn as one line that follows
//      the candles (an EMA of the typical price) and is coloured by the zone: strong, weak, or neutral between
//      them. No object is added per bar, and the line doubles as a trend reference. The dots remain available.
// [T3b] ZONE CURVE. Drawn only while RSC is at an extreme, and on the side that says which extreme it is:
//       above the highs when the stock is outperforming the benchmark, below the lows when it is lagging.
//       Between the levels nothing is drawn, so an empty stretch is itself the message. One plot, because the
//       price series simply switches sides and the colour follows it; style_circles gives thin dots rather
//       than a solid line, so it never competes with the price line or the moving averages.
// [T3c] Two smoothing passes: the first follows the highs / lows, the second takes the kinks out of it, so the
//       dotted line reads as one calm arc instead of a jagged copy of the wicks. The offset is generous by
//       default (3 x ATR) to keep the dots clear of structure labels and order block edges near the candles.
float rscCurveOffset = nz(ta.atr(14)) * rscCurveOffsetInput
float rscCurveAbove = ta.ema(ta.ema(high, rscCurveLengthInput), rscCurveSmoothInput) + rscCurveOffset
float rscCurveBelow = ta.ema(ta.ema(low, rscCurveLengthInput), rscCurveSmoothInput) - rscCurveOffset
float rscCurveValue = rscStrong ? rscCurveAbove : rscWeak ? rscCurveBelow : na
color rscCurveColor = rscStrong ? color.new(rscStrongColorInput, 20) : color.new(rscWeakColorInput, 20)

plot(showRscInput and rscCurveInput ? rscCurveValue : na, 'RSC Zone Curve', color = rscCurveColor, style = plot.style_circles, linewidth = 1)

// [T3] one marker per side per bar. The RSC dots are gone: the zone curve above carries that information now,
//      so these slots belong to MFI alone.
plotchar(mfiOverboughtMarker ? high + markerStep : na, title = 'MFI Overbought', char = '▾', location = location.absolute, color = color.new(mfiOverboughtColorInput, 30), size = size.tiny)
plotchar(mfiOversoldMarker ? low - markerStep : na, title = 'MFI Oversold', char = '▴', location = location.absolute, color = color.new(mfiOversoldColorInput, 30), size = size.tiny)
// value for the Data Window only, so no line is drawn on the price scale
plot(showRscInput ? rscValue : na, 'RSC', color = rscStrongColorInput, display = display.data_window)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//KAIRI RELATIVE INDEX
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [KRI1] KRI = (close - MA) / MA x 100. "Flop" marks the bar KRI enters the overbought zone, "Flip" the bar it
//        enters the oversold zone: one label per excursion, since a stretched stock can sit in the zone for many
//        bars. Labels (not plots) are used so they cost nothing against the 64-plot limit, sit in slot 3 of the
//        marker stack above the MFI triangle and the RSC dot, and are capped at the last N.
// ta.* calls computed on every bar and selected afterwards, for the same consistency reason as movingAverage()
float kriSma = ta.sma(close, kriLengthInput)
float kriEma = ta.ema(close, kriLengthInput)
float kriBase = kriMaTypeInput == MA_SMA ? kriSma : kriEma
float kriValue = kriBase != 0 ? (close - kriBase) / kriBase * 100 : na

float kriAdaptiveUpper = ta.percentile_linear_interpolation(kriValue, kriAdaptiveLookbackInput, kriPercentileInput)
float kriAdaptiveLower = ta.percentile_linear_interpolation(kriValue, kriAdaptiveLookbackInput, 100 - kriPercentileInput)
float kriUpper = kriModeInput == KRI_FIXED ? kriOverboughtInput : kriAdaptiveUpper
float kriLower = kriModeInput == KRI_FIXED ? kriOversoldInput : kriAdaptiveLower

bool kriOverbought = not na(kriValue) and not na(kriUpper) and kriValue >= kriUpper
bool kriOversold = not na(kriValue) and not na(kriLower) and kriValue <= kriLower
bool kriFlop = showKriInput and kriOverbought and not kriOverbought[1]
bool kriFlip = showKriInput and kriOversold and not kriOversold[1]

var array<label> kriLabels = array.new<label>()
if kriFlop
    kriLabels.push(label.new(bar_index, high + 3 * markerStep, 'Flop', xloc.bar_index, yloc.price, color(na), label.style_label_down, color.new(kriFlopColorInput, 15), size.tiny, tooltip = 'KRI ' + str.tostring(kriValue, '0.0') + '% entered overbought (' + str.tostring(kriUpper, '0.0') + '%)'))
if kriFlip
    kriLabels.push(label.new(bar_index, low - 3 * markerStep, 'Flip', xloc.bar_index, yloc.price, color(na), label.style_label_up, color.new(kriFlipColorInput, 15), size.tiny, tooltip = 'KRI ' + str.tostring(kriValue, '0.0') + '% entered oversold (' + str.tostring(kriLower, '0.0') + '%)'))
while kriLabels.size() > kriMaxLabelsInput
    label.delete(kriLabels.shift())

// [KRI1] alert() rather than alertcondition(): alertcondition counts as a plot and the script is at the limit.
//        Create the alert with "Any alert() function call". Confirmed bars only, so it cannot fire and un-fire.
if kriFlop and barstate.isconfirmed
    alert(syminfo.ticker + ' KRI Flop: ' + str.tostring(kriValue, '0.0') + '% entered overbought', alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
if kriFlip and barstate.isconfirmed
    alert(syminfo.ticker + ' KRI Flip: ' + str.tostring(kriValue, '0.0') + '% entered oversold', alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//WYCKOFF PHASE
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [W1] Two steps. Everything is on the chart timeframe and uses confirmed history only, so there is no
//      request.security and no repainting surface beyond the usual live-bar update.
//
// STEP 1, TREND OR TRADING RANGE. Kaufman's efficiency ratio (the persistence axis of v4.3), ranked against
//         its own history with hysteresis. A trending market is MARKUP or MARKDOWN by the sign of the net move.
// ta.change() rather than close[regimeErLengthInput]: a raw history index with a variable offset can
// trip "insufficient bars back", whereas a built-in declares its own buffer requirement.
float regimeNetChange = nz(ta.change(close, regimeErLengthInput))
float regimeTotalMove = math.sum(math.abs(nz(ta.change(close))), regimeErLengthInput)
float regimeEfficiency = regimeTotalMove != 0 ? math.abs(regimeNetChange) / regimeTotalMove : 0.0
float regimeEfficiencySmooth = ta.ema(regimeEfficiency, regimeErSmoothInput)
// early bars fall back to 50, inside the hysteresis dead band, so they change nothing
float regimeEfficiencyRank = nz(ta.percentrank(regimeEfficiencySmooth, regimeLookbackInput), 50)
float regimeDirection = ta.ema(regimeNetChange, regimeErSmoothInput)

var bool regimeTrending = false
regimeTrending := regimeEfficiencyRank >= regimeEnterInput ? true : regimeEfficiencyRank <= regimeExitInput ? false : regimeTrending

// STEP 2, WHICH KIND OF TRADING RANGE. Four votes, +1 = accumulation, -1 = distribution.
//   Prior trend  a range after a markdown is where accumulation happens, after a markup distribution
//   Location     where the close sits in the long-term range: bottom 40% / top 40%
//   Effort       up-close volume vs down-close volume over the trading range window (Wyckoff's effort vs
//                result). Symbols without volume score 0 here rather than breaking the vote.
//   Events       a recent SPRING (low pierces the range low, close back inside) or UPTHRUST (the mirror)
float wyRangeHigh = ta.highest(high, wyRangeLengthInput)
float wyRangeLow = ta.lowest(low, wyRangeLengthInput)
// the boundaries a spring / upthrust must pierce are those of the range BEFORE this bar
bool wySpring = not na(wyRangeLow[1]) and low < wyRangeLow[1] and close > wyRangeLow[1]
bool wyUpthrust = not na(wyRangeHigh[1]) and high > wyRangeHigh[1] and close < wyRangeHigh[1]

float wyLocationHigh = ta.highest(high, wyLocationLengthInput)
float wyLocationLow = ta.lowest(low, wyLocationLengthInput)
float wyLocation = wyLocationHigh > wyLocationLow ? (close - wyLocationLow) / (wyLocationHigh - wyLocationLow) : 0.5

float wyUpVolume = math.sum(close > close[1] ? nz(volume) : 0.0, wyRangeLengthInput)
float wyDownVolume = math.sum(close < close[1] ? nz(volume) : 0.0, wyRangeLengthInput)
float wyVolumeBalance = wyUpVolume + wyDownVolume > 0 ? (wyUpVolume - wyDownVolume) / (wyUpVolume + wyDownVolume) : 0.0

var string wyPhase = na
var int wyLastTrend = 0
var int wyLastSpringIndex = na
var int wyLastUpthrustIndex = na

// events only count inside a range: a new low in a markdown is not a spring
if not regimeTrending
    if wySpring
        wyLastSpringIndex := bar_index
    if wyUpthrust
        wyLastUpthrustIndex := bar_index

bool wySpringRecent = not na(wyLastSpringIndex) and bar_index - wyLastSpringIndex <= wyEventMemoryInput
bool wyUpthrustRecent = not na(wyLastUpthrustIndex) and bar_index - wyLastUpthrustIndex <= wyEventMemoryInput

int wyPriorVote = wyLastTrend == -1 ? 1 : wyLastTrend == 1 ? -1 : 0
int wyLocationVote = wyLocation < 0.4 ? 1 : wyLocation > 0.6 ? -1 : 0
int wyEffortVote = wyVolumeBalance > 0.1 ? 1 : wyVolumeBalance < -0.1 ? -1 : 0
int wyEventVote = (wySpringRecent ? 1 : 0) - (wyUpthrustRecent ? 1 : 0)
int wyScore = wyPriorVote + wyLocationVote + wyEffortVote + wyEventVote

string wyPreviousPhase = wyPhase

if regimeTrending
    wyPhase := regimeDirection >= 0 ? WY_MARKUP : WY_MARKDOWN
    wyLastTrend := regimeDirection >= 0 ? 1 : -1
else
    bool wyEnteringRange = na(wyPhase) or wyPhase == WY_MARKUP or wyPhase == WY_MARKDOWN
    if wyEnteringRange
        // a tie falls back to location, the most direct of the four
        wyPhase := wyScore > 0 ? WY_ACCUMULATION : wyScore < 0 ? WY_DISTRIBUTION : wyLocation < 0.5 ? WY_ACCUMULATION : WY_DISTRIBUTION
    // hysteresis inside the range: switch only when the evidence clearly turns against the current label
    else if wyPhase == WY_ACCUMULATION and wyScore <= -2
        wyPhase := WY_DISTRIBUTION
    else if wyPhase == WY_DISTRIBUTION and wyScore >= 2
        wyPhase := WY_ACCUMULATION

// kept under the v4.3 names so the label, background and alert plumbing below read the same
string regimeText = wyPhase
color regimeColor = wyPhase == WY_ACCUMULATION ? wyAccumulationColorInput : wyPhase == WY_MARKUP ? wyMarkupColorInput : wyPhase == WY_DISTRIBUTION ? wyDistributionColorInput : wyMarkdownColorInput

bool regimeChanged = showRegimeInput and not na(wyPreviousPhase) and wyPhase != wyPreviousPhase
bool wyEnteredAccumulation = regimeChanged and wyPhase == WY_ACCUMULATION
bool wyEnteredMarkup = regimeChanged and wyPhase == WY_MARKUP
bool wyEnteredDistribution = regimeChanged and wyPhase == WY_DISTRIBUTION
bool wyEnteredMarkdown = regimeChanged and wyPhase == WY_MARKDOWN
bool wyInRange = wyPhase == WY_ACCUMULATION or wyPhase == WY_DISTRIBUTION

// [W1] Trading range boxes. One box per accumulation / distribution range, opened over the trailing range
//      window (the range was already forming before the efficiency ratio could confirm it), grown with price
//      while the range lasts, and recoloured if the phase flips inside it.
// [W2] The boxes come from the bottom-layer pool allocated on the first bar (see [W2] near the top). A new
//      range takes the next slot in rotation, which recycles the oldest box, so the last N ranges stay drawn.
var box wyCurrentBox = na
var int wyBoxSlot = -1
var float wyBoxTop = na
var float wyBoxBottom = na

if showRegimeInput and showWyRangeBoxEffective and wyBoxes.size() > 0
    if wyInRange
        color boxColor = regimeColor
        bool wyWasInRange = wyPreviousPhase == WY_ACCUMULATION or wyPreviousPhase == WY_DISTRIBUTION
        if not wyWasInRange or na(wyCurrentBox)
            wyBoxSlot := (wyBoxSlot + 1) % wyBoxes.size()
            wyCurrentBox := wyBoxes.get(wyBoxSlot)
            wyBoxTop := wyRangeHigh
            wyBoxBottom := wyRangeLow
            wyCurrentBox.set_lefttop(math.max(0, bar_index - wyRangeLengthInput + 1), wyBoxTop)
            wyCurrentBox.set_rightbottom(bar_index, wyBoxBottom)
            wyCurrentBox.set_text(wyPhase)
            wyCurrentBox.set_text_color(color.new(boxColor, 30))
            wyCurrentBox.set_border_color(color.new(boxColor, 55))
            wyCurrentBox.set_bgcolor(color.new(boxColor, 93))
        else
            wyBoxTop := math.max(wyBoxTop, high)
            wyBoxBottom := math.min(wyBoxBottom, low)
            wyCurrentBox.set_right(bar_index)
            wyCurrentBox.set_top(wyBoxTop)
            wyCurrentBox.set_bottom(wyBoxBottom)
            if wyPhase != wyPreviousPhase
                wyCurrentBox.set_text(wyPhase)
                wyCurrentBox.set_text_color(color.new(boxColor, 30))
                wyCurrentBox.set_border_color(color.new(boxColor, 55))
                wyCurrentBox.set_bgcolor(color.new(boxColor, 93))
    else
        wyCurrentBox := na

// [W1] optional spring / upthrust markers, tiny and bounded like the ARA/ARB markers [L2]
var array<label> wyEventLabels = array.new<label>()
if showRegimeInput and showWyEventsInput and not regimeTrending
    if wySpring
        wyEventLabels.push(label.new(bar_index, low, 'Spring', xloc.bar_index, yloc.belowbar, color(na), label.style_label_up, color.new(wyAccumulationColorInput, 20), size.tiny, tooltip = 'Wyckoff spring: pierced the ' + str.tostring(wyRangeLengthInput) + '-bar range low and closed back inside'))
    if wyUpthrust
        wyEventLabels.push(label.new(bar_index, high, 'UT', xloc.bar_index, yloc.abovebar, color(na), label.style_label_down, color.new(wyDistributionColorInput, 20), size.tiny, tooltip = 'Wyckoff upthrust: pierced the ' + str.tostring(wyRangeLengthInput) + '-bar range high and closed back inside'))
    while wyEventLabels.size() > 20
        label.delete(wyEventLabels.shift())

// @function            [W2] signed vote for the phase readout
// @returns             string
wyVote(int v) =>
    v > 0 ? '+' + str.tostring(v) : str.tostring(v)

// [W2] Phase readout at the right edge, stacked below the HTF bias label as before. It is TEXT IN A BORDERLESS
//      BOX from the bottom-layer pool, not a label, so order blocks, their confirmation zones, gaps and every
//      label draw over it. Boxes carry no tooltip, so the four votes are printed on a second line instead.
if showRegimeInput and showRegimeLabelInput and not na(wyPhaseTextBox) and not na(wyPhase) and (barstate.islast or barstate.islastconfirmedhistory)
    // compact on purpose, the box has to hold it: P = prior trend, L = location, V = volume effort, E = event
    string voteLine = wyInRange ? 'score ' + wyVote(wyScore) + '  (P' + wyVote(wyPriorVote) + ' L' + wyVote(wyLocationVote) + ' V' + wyVote(wyEffortVote) + ' E' + wyVote(wyEventVote) + ')' : 'trend, ER rank ' + str.tostring(regimeEfficiencyRank, '0') + '%'
    float textY = close - 1.5 * atrMeasure
    int textLeft = last_bar_time + 20 * barDurationMs
    // Kept generously large so the text always fits. NOTE: the box is 100% transparent rather than na. A box
    // whose border AND background are both na is not rendered at all, and its text disappears with it.
    wyPhaseTextBox.set_lefttop(textLeft, textY + atrMeasure)
    wyPhaseTextBox.set_rightbottom(textLeft + 120 * barDurationMs, textY - atrMeasure)
    wyPhaseTextBox.set_text('Wyckoff: ' + regimeText + '\n' + voteLine)
    wyPhaseTextBox.set_text_color(regimeColor)

// [RS1] the Wyckoff tint is drawn by the single background call in IDX SESSION WINDOWS below (64-plot limit)


//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//REVERSAL CONFLUENCE
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [B1] Nothing new is measured here that the chart does not already show. The block only asks whether several
//      independent pieces of evidence line up on the same swing, and it costs no plots: the marker is a label
//      and the alerts go through alert(), because the script sits on Pine's 64-plot limit.
//
//      WHY THESE FOUR. KRI says price is stretched from its own average, MFI says the money flow behind the
//      move is spent, RSC says whether the weakness belongs to this stock or to the whole market, and the
//      divergence says the last swing was made with less force than the one before it. The first three judge
//      ONE bar; only divergence compares two swings, which is why it is worth two votes.
//
//      HONEST COST. Every vote you demand multiplies the filtering: five conditions that each pass 60% of the
//      time leave about 8% of the signals. Fewer signals is not the same as better signals, which is why the
//      composite gets its own statistics row instead of a promise.

// --- DIVERGENCE ENGINE -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Confirmed pivots only, so a divergence is recognised divPivotLengthInput bars after the swing it describes.
// That lag is unavoidable with real pivots and is the same lag the structure engine lives with.
float divPivotLow = ta.pivotlow(low, divPivotLengthInput, divPivotLengthInput)
float divPivotHigh = ta.pivothigh(high, divPivotLengthInput, divPivotLengthInput)

var float divPrevLowPrice = na
var float divPrevLowMfi = na
var float divPrevLowRsc = na
var int divPrevLowIndex = na
var float divLastLowPrice = na
var float divLastLowMfi = na
var float divLastLowRsc = na
var int divLastLowIndex = na

var float divPrevHighPrice = na
var float divPrevHighMfi = na
var float divPrevHighRsc = na
var int divPrevHighIndex = na
var float divLastHighPrice = na
var float divLastHighMfi = na
var float divLastHighRsc = na
var int divLastHighIndex = na

var int lastBullDivIndex = na
var int lastBearDivIndex = na
var string lastBullDivSource = ''
var string lastBearDivSource = ''

if not na(divPivotLow)
    divPrevLowPrice := divLastLowPrice
    divPrevLowMfi := divLastLowMfi
    divPrevLowRsc := divLastLowRsc
    divPrevLowIndex := divLastLowIndex
    divLastLowPrice := divPivotLow
    divLastLowMfi := mfiValue[divPivotLengthInput]
    divLastLowRsc := rscValue[divPivotLengthInput]
    divLastLowIndex := bar_index - divPivotLengthInput

    if not na(divPrevLowPrice) and divLastLowIndex - divPrevLowIndex <= divMaxGapInput and divLastLowPrice < divPrevLowPrice
        bool mfiBullDiv = not na(divLastLowMfi) and not na(divPrevLowMfi) and divLastLowMfi > divPrevLowMfi
        bool rscBullDiv = not na(divLastLowRsc) and not na(divPrevLowRsc) and divLastLowRsc > divPrevLowRsc
        if mfiBullDiv or rscBullDiv
            lastBullDivIndex := bar_index
            lastBullDivSource := mfiBullDiv and rscBullDiv ? 'MFI+RSC' : mfiBullDiv ? 'MFI' : 'RSC'

if not na(divPivotHigh)
    divPrevHighPrice := divLastHighPrice
    divPrevHighMfi := divLastHighMfi
    divPrevHighRsc := divLastHighRsc
    divPrevHighIndex := divLastHighIndex
    divLastHighPrice := divPivotHigh
    divLastHighMfi := mfiValue[divPivotLengthInput]
    divLastHighRsc := rscValue[divPivotLengthInput]
    divLastHighIndex := bar_index - divPivotLengthInput

    if not na(divPrevHighPrice) and divLastHighIndex - divPrevHighIndex <= divMaxGapInput and divLastHighPrice > divPrevHighPrice
        bool mfiBearDiv = not na(divLastHighMfi) and not na(divPrevHighMfi) and divLastHighMfi < divPrevHighMfi
        bool rscBearDiv = not na(divLastHighRsc) and not na(divPrevHighRsc) and divLastHighRsc < divPrevHighRsc
        if mfiBearDiv or rscBearDiv
            lastBearDivIndex := bar_index
            lastBearDivSource := mfiBearDiv and rscBearDiv ? 'MFI+RSC' : mfiBearDiv ? 'MFI' : 'RSC'

// --- VOLUME CLIMAX -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Effort against result: heavy volume that closes back against the move it came from. Symbols without volume
// score 0 here rather than breaking the vote.
float confBarRange = high - low
float confCloseLocation = confBarRange > 0 ? ((close - low) - (high - close)) / confBarRange : 0.0
float climaxAverageVolume = ta.sma(nz(volume), climaxLookbackInput)
bool volumeClimaxBar = climaxAverageVolume > 0 and nz(volume) >= climaxVolumeMultInput * climaxAverageVolume
bool bullishClimax = volumeClimaxBar and confCloseLocation >= 0.25
bool bearishClimax = volumeClimaxBar and confCloseLocation <= -0.25

// --- RECENCY TRACKERS --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each vote records WHEN it last happened, so the score can ask "within the window" instead of "on this exact
// bar", which no two indicators ever agree on.
var int lastBullClimaxIndex = na
var int lastBearClimaxIndex = na
var int lastKriOversoldIndex = na
var int lastKriOverboughtIndex = na
var int lastMfiOversoldIndex = na
var int lastMfiOverboughtIndex = na
var int lastSellsideSweepIndex = na
var int lastBuysideSweepIndex = na

// [B3] ZONE RESET. An oscillator that sits in its zone for twenty bars used to hand out a free vote on every
//      one of them, which is exactly what happens in a strong trend. A zone now only registers on the bar it
//      is ENTERED, and only if it had been outside for at least the reset window first. One excursion, one
//      vote, and the recency window then counts from that entry.
var int barsOutsideKriOversold = 9999
var int barsOutsideKriOverbought = 9999
var int barsOutsideMfiOversold = 9999
var int barsOutsideMfiOverbought = 9999

if kriOversold
    if barsOutsideKriOversold >= confluenceZoneResetInput
        lastKriOversoldIndex := bar_index
    barsOutsideKriOversold := 0
else
    barsOutsideKriOversold += 1

if kriOverbought
    if barsOutsideKriOverbought >= confluenceZoneResetInput
        lastKriOverboughtIndex := bar_index
    barsOutsideKriOverbought := 0
else
    barsOutsideKriOverbought += 1

if mfiOversold
    if barsOutsideMfiOversold >= confluenceZoneResetInput
        lastMfiOversoldIndex := bar_index
    barsOutsideMfiOversold := 0
else
    barsOutsideMfiOversold += 1

if mfiOverbought
    if barsOutsideMfiOverbought >= confluenceZoneResetInput
        lastMfiOverboughtIndex := bar_index
    barsOutsideMfiOverbought := 0
else
    barsOutsideMfiOverbought += 1

if bullishClimax
    lastBullClimaxIndex := bar_index
if bearishClimax
    lastBearClimaxIndex := bar_index
// the sweep flags in `sweeps` are consumed by the CHoCH pairing, so the score keeps its own copy
if currentAlerts.sellsideLiquiditySweep
    lastSellsideSweepIndex := bar_index
if currentAlerts.buysideLiquiditySweep
    lastBuysideSweepIndex := bar_index

// @function            [B1] true when an event happened within the confluence window
// @returns             bool
confRecent(int eventIndex) =>
    not na(eventIndex) and bar_index - eventIndex <= confluenceWindowInput

// --- SCORE -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// [B2] RSC VOTES ON DIRECTION, NOT LEVEL. A stock that leads the market sits at RSC 100 for weeks: the LEVEL
//      says nothing new there, and reading it as "overbought" is how a trend manufactures false reversals.
//      What does carry information is the TURN: relative strength improving while price is still falling is
//      early accumulation, and the mirror is early distribution. So the vote is the slope of RSC over the
//      slope window, in RSC points.
float rscSlope = nz(rscValue - rscValue[rscSlopeLengthInput])
bool bullVoteRsc = rscSlope >= rscSlopeThresholdInput
bool bearVoteRsc = rscSlope <= -rscSlopeThresholdInput

bool bullVoteKri = confRecent(lastKriOversoldIndex)
bool bullVoteMfi = confRecent(lastMfiOversoldIndex)
bool bullVoteClimax = confRecent(lastBullClimaxIndex)
bool bullVoteDiv = confRecent(lastBullDivIndex)
bool bullVoteLiquidity = confRecent(lastSellsideSweepIndex) or confRecent(wyLastSpringIndex)
int bullScore = (bullVoteKri ? 1 : 0) + (bullVoteMfi ? 1 : 0) + (bullVoteClimax ? 1 : 0) + (bullVoteDiv ? 2 : 0) + (bullVoteLiquidity ? 1 : 0) + (bullVoteRsc ? 1 : 0)

bool bearVoteKri = confRecent(lastKriOverboughtIndex)
bool bearVoteMfi = confRecent(lastMfiOverboughtIndex)
bool bearVoteClimax = confRecent(lastBearClimaxIndex)
bool bearVoteDiv = confRecent(lastBearDivIndex)
bool bearVoteLiquidity = confRecent(lastBuysideSweepIndex) or confRecent(wyLastUpthrustIndex)
int bearScore = (bearVoteKri ? 1 : 0) + (bearVoteMfi ? 1 : 0) + (bearVoteClimax ? 1 : 0) + (bearVoteDiv ? 2 : 0) + (bearVoteLiquidity ? 1 : 0) + (bearVoteRsc ? 1 : 0)

// Context uses the same location measure the Wyckoff votes use: bottom half of the long-term range for a
// bullish reversal, top half for a bearish one, or the matching Wyckoff phase.
bool bullContext = not confluenceContextInput or wyLocation < 0.5 or wyPhase == WY_ACCUMULATION
bool bearContext = not confluenceContextInput or wyLocation > 0.5 or wyPhase == WY_DISTRIBUTION

// [B4] TREND REGIME GATE. The efficiency ratio percentile the Wyckoff module already computes says whether
//      price is travelling in a straight line. Exhaustion readings are least reliable exactly there: a strong
//      trend keeps every oscillator pinned, so "overbought" stops being evidence. In that regime the bar is
//      raised - the threshold goes up, and optionally a divergence becomes mandatory, since a divergence is
//      the one vote that compares two swings instead of judging one bar.
bool confStrongTrend = regimeEfficiencyRank >= confluenceTrendGateInput
int confluenceThresholdEffective = confluenceThresholdInput + (confStrongTrend ? confluenceTrendPenaltyInput : 0)
bool bullDivergenceOk = not confStrongTrend or not confluenceRequireDivInTrendInput or bullVoteDiv
bool bearDivergenceOk = not confStrongTrend or not confluenceRequireDivInTrendInput or bearVoteDiv

// The trigger is the script's own structure break: evidence alone never fires a signal, price has to turn.
bool bullTrigger = currentAlerts.internalBullishCHoCH or currentAlerts.swingBullishCHoCH
bool bearTrigger = currentAlerts.internalBearishCHoCH or currentAlerts.swingBearishCHoCH

currentAlerts.bullishConfluence := showConfluenceInput and bullTrigger and bullContext and bullDivergenceOk and bullScore >= confluenceThresholdEffective
currentAlerts.bearishConfluence := showConfluenceInput and bearTrigger and bearContext and bearDivergenceOk and bearScore >= confluenceThresholdEffective

// --- MARKER ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A label, not a plot: the script is at the 64-plot limit, and this way the votes fit in a tooltip. Slot 4 of
// the marker stack, beyond the KRI label, so it never lands on the MFI triangle or the KRI text.
var array<label> confluenceLabels = array.new<label>()

if currentAlerts.bullishConfluence
    string bullTip = 'Score ' + str.tostring(bullScore) + '/' + str.tostring(confluenceThresholdEffective) + (confStrongTrend ? ' (strong trend)' : '') + '\n' + (bullVoteDiv ? '+2 divergence (' + lastBullDivSource + ')\n' : '') + (bullVoteKri ? '+1 KRI oversold\n' : '') + (bullVoteMfi ? '+1 MFI oversold\n' : '') + (bullVoteClimax ? '+1 volume climax\n' : '') + (bullVoteLiquidity ? '+1 sellside taken\n' : '') + (bullVoteRsc ? '+1 RSC turning up\n' : '') + 'Trigger: bullish CHoCH'
    confluenceLabels.push(label.new(bar_index, low - 4 * markerStep, '▲', xloc.bar_index, yloc.price, color(na), label.style_label_up, color.new(confluenceBullColorInput, 10), size.small, tooltip = bullTip))

if currentAlerts.bearishConfluence
    string bearTip = 'Score ' + str.tostring(bearScore) + '/' + str.tostring(confluenceThresholdEffective) + (confStrongTrend ? ' (strong trend)' : '') + '\n' + (bearVoteDiv ? '+2 divergence (' + lastBearDivSource + ')\n' : '') + (bearVoteKri ? '+1 KRI overbought\n' : '') + (bearVoteMfi ? '+1 MFI overbought\n' : '') + (bearVoteClimax ? '+1 volume climax\n' : '') + (bearVoteLiquidity ? '+1 buyside taken\n' : '') + (bearVoteRsc ? '+1 RSC turning down\n' : '') + 'Trigger: bearish CHoCH'
    confluenceLabels.push(label.new(bar_index, high + 4 * markerStep, '▼', xloc.bar_index, yloc.price, color(na), label.style_label_down, color.new(confluenceBearColorInput, 10), size.small, tooltip = bearTip))

while confluenceLabels.size() > confluenceMaxLabelsInput
    label.delete(confluenceLabels.shift())

// [B1] alert() rather than alertcondition(): alertcondition counts as a plot. Use "Any alert() function call".
if currentAlerts.bullishConfluence and barstate.isconfirmed
    alert(syminfo.ticker + ' bullish reversal confluence, score ' + str.tostring(bullScore), alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)
if currentAlerts.bearishConfluence and barstate.isconfirmed
    alert(syminfo.ticker + ' bearish reversal confluence, score ' + str.tostring(bearScore), alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//IDX SESSION WINDOWS
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [C4] Display only. Session strings carry their weekdays (1 = Sunday ... 7 = Saturday), because Friday's second
//      session starts 30 minutes later. time() returns na outside the window.
IDX_TZ = 'Asia/Jakarta'
bool idxShowSessions = showIdxSessionsEffective and idxMode and timeframe.isintraday
bool inIdxOpening = not na(time(timeframe.period, idxOpenSessionInput + ':23456', IDX_TZ))
bool inIdxSession2 = not na(time(timeframe.period, idxSession2InputMonThu + ':2345', IDX_TZ)) or not na(time(timeframe.period, idxSession2InputFri + ':6', IDX_TZ))
bool inIdxPreClosing = not na(time(timeframe.period, idxCloseSessionInput + ':23456', IDX_TZ))

color idxSessionColor = not idxShowSessions ? color(na) : inIdxOpening ? idxOpenColorInput : inIdxSession2 ? idxSession2ColorInput : inIdxPreClosing ? idxCloseColorInput : color(na)
// [RS1] one bgcolor for both tints to stay under the 64-plot limit. An intraday session window takes priority over the Wyckoff phase tint.
color wyTintColor = showRegimeInput and showRegimeBackgroundInput and not na(wyPhase) ? color.new(regimeColor, regimeBackgroundOpacityInput) : color(na)
bgcolor(not na(idxSessionColor) ? idxSessionColor : wyTintColor, title = 'Sessions / Wyckoff Tint')

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//SIGNAL STATISTICS
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
// [L-STAT] Measures how each signal type actually performed on this chart. Nothing here feeds a signal, a
//          drawing, a filter or an alert: this section reads currentAlerts and writes a table. Deleting it,
//          the openTrade type and the STATS_GROUP inputs removes the feature completely.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Every filter in this script -- displacement [E22], HTF bias [G26], the confluence filter,
// and anything added later -- is a HYPOTHESIS about which signals are worth keeping. Without a measurement
// the only way to judge one is to scroll a chart and remember the trades that agreed with you. The rows
// below count the signals that survive the CURRENT settings, so the intended workflow is: note the numbers,
// toggle one filter, and compare. That is also the answer to "is this indicator accurate" -- accurate at
// what, against which alternative, is a question only a table like this can settle.
//
// WHAT A "TRADE" IS. Deliberately crude, and IDENTICAL for every row so the rows can be compared:
//   Entry    close of the bar the signal fired on, which is the moment its alert fires.
//   Risk     1R = statsRiskAtrMultInput x ATR(statsAtrLengthInput), frozen at entry so that later
//            volatility cannot quietly rescale a trade that is already open.
//   Stop     entry -/+ 1R.                Target  entry +/- statsTargetRInput R.
//   Horizon  statsHorizonInput bars, after which the trade is closed at the market.
//
// HONESTY RULES, because a table like this is trivial to rig and most published ones are:
//   - Evaluation starts strictly AFTER the entry bar. The signal bar can neither win nor lose. This is the
//     same rule [B6] applies to order blocks, and for the same reason: the bar that creates a thing must
//     not also be allowed to resolve it.
//   - When a single bar spans BOTH the stop and the target, the intrabar order is unknown and it counts as
//     a LOSS. Any other choice inflates the win rate, by an amount that GROWS with the target multiple --
//     which is exactly how a rigged table makes a high R:R look free.
//   - Trades open only on confirmed bars, so a signal that forms and un-forms inside a live bar never
//     enters the statistics. The table therefore reports state as of the last CLOSED bar.
//   - Win% is over RESOLVED trades only (target or stop). Timeouts get their own column instead of being
//     quietly folded in with the winners or the losers.
//   - Expectancy is over ALL closed trades, timeouts included at their actual result, so a signal type that
//     mostly goes nowhere cannot hide behind a flattering Win%.
//   - N counts signals actually TRACKED. One dropped at the concurrency cap is not counted at all, so no
//     rate is ever reported over a population that was not measured.
//
// ROWS OVERLAP, ON PURPOSE. A sweep reversal [F24] is by definition also a CHoCH, so it is counted in both
// rows, and an internal break often coincides with a swing break. Each row answers "how did THIS signal
// type do", which is the comparison worth making. The consequence is that the ALL row is a weighted blend
// of overlapping populations rather than a clean portfolio result -- read the rows, not the total.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOT. Not a backtest. No spread, no slippage, no position sizing, no trade management, no
// compounding, and one volatility-normalised stop for every signal type rather than the structural stop an
// SMC trader would actually use. It ranks signal types against each other on this instrument under these
// settings. It does not tell you what an account would have done.
//
// HOW TO READ IT. Break-even win rate is 1 / (1 + Target), so at the default 2R target a 40% Win% is good,
// not bad. The Win% colour is measured against that line, not against 50%. A high T/O% is a finding in its
// own right: a signal that produces no movement is worse than one that is wrong quickly, because it ties up
// attention and risk for nothing. Treat any row with N below about 30 as noise.

STAT_TYPES = 16
STAT_INT_BOS_BULL = 0
STAT_INT_BOS_BEAR = 1
STAT_INT_CHOCH_BULL = 2
STAT_INT_CHOCH_BEAR = 3
STAT_SWING_BOS_BULL = 4
STAT_SWING_BOS_BEAR = 5
STAT_SWING_CHOCH_BULL = 6
STAT_SWING_CHOCH_BEAR = 7
STAT_SWEEP_REV_BULL = 8
STAT_SWEEP_REV_BEAR = 9
// [R1] zone retests. Bearish constants stay odd, which statsIsExitRow() relies on.
STAT_OB_RETEST_BULL = 10
STAT_OB_RETEST_BEAR = 11
STAT_FVG_RETEST_BULL = 12
STAT_FVG_RETEST_BEAR = 13
// [B1] the composite reversal signal gets its own row so it can be compared with CHoCH and the retests
STAT_CONFLUENCE_BULL = 14
STAT_CONFLUENCE_BEAR = 15

// @variable            [L-STAT] signals still waiting on a target, a stop or the horizon
var array<openTrade> statsOpenTrades = array.new<openTrade>()
// @variable            [L-STAT] per-type tallies, all indexed by the STAT_* constants above
var array<int> statsCount = array.new<int>(STAT_TYPES, 0)
var array<int> statsWins = array.new<int>(STAT_TYPES, 0)
var array<int> statsLosses = array.new<int>(STAT_TYPES, 0)
var array<int> statsTimeouts = array.new<int>(STAT_TYPES, 0)
var array<float> statsSumR = array.new<float>(STAT_TYPES, 0.0)
var array<float> statsSumMFE = array.new<float>(STAT_TYPES, 0.0)
var array<string> statsNames = array.from('Internal BOS ▲', 'Internal BOS ▼', 'Internal CHoCH ▲', 'Internal CHoCH ▼', 'Swing BOS ▲', 'Swing BOS ▼', 'Swing CHoCH ▲', 'Swing CHoCH ▼', 'Sweep Reversal ▲', 'Sweep Reversal ▼', 'OB Retest ▲', 'OB Retest ▼', 'FVG Retest ▲', 'FVG Retest ▼', 'Confluence Rev ▲', 'Confluence Rev ▼')

// @variable            [L-STAT] risk unit source. ta.atr() carries state, so like the [H-MA] helpers it is
//                      computed unconditionally and the enable toggle is applied to the consumer instead.
float statsAtr = ta.atr(statsAtrLengthInput)

// @variable            [IDX3] bearish rows are EXIT signals rather than short trades
bool statsLongOnly = statsLongOnlyInput == IDX_ON or statsLongOnlyInput == IDX_AUTO and syminfo.prefix == 'IDX'
// @variable            [IDX3] round-trip cost as a fraction of price
float statsRoundTripFee = (statsBuyFeeInput + statsSellFeeInput) / 100.0

// @function            [IDX3] true for a bearish row that is measured as an exit signal
// @returns             bool
statsIsExitRow(int typeIndex) =>
    // every bearish STAT_* constant is odd
    statsLongOnly and typeIndex % 2 == 1

// @function            [L-STAT] start tracking a signal that fired on this bar
// @returns             void
statsOpenTrade(int typeIndex, int bias) =>
    float risk = statsRiskAtrMultInput * statsAtr

    // a signal arriving at the concurrency cap is ignored outright rather than counted-but-untracked,
    // so N never overstates the measured population
    // [IDX3] a bar that CLOSED locked at the limit in the trade direction could not have been filled at that
    //        close: nobody sells at ARA, nobody buys at ARB. Such a signal is not taken, and not counted.
    bool unfillable = bias == BULLISH ? araLocked : arbLocked

    if statsOpenTrades.size() < statsMaxOpenInput and not na(risk) and risk > 0 and not unfillable
        float entryPrice = close
        float stopPrice = bias == BULLISH ? entryPrice - risk : entryPrice + risk
        float targetPrice = bias == BULLISH ? entryPrice + statsTargetRInput * risk : entryPrice - statsTargetRInput * risk

        statsOpenTrades.push(openTrade.new(typeIndex, bias, entryPrice, stopPrice, targetPrice, risk, bar_index))
        statsCount.set(typeIndex, statsCount.get(typeIndex) + 1)

// @function            [L-STAT] advance every open trade by one bar and tally the ones that finished
// @returns             void
// [B4] iterates backwards so array.remove() cannot skip the following element
statsUpdateTrades() =>
    if statsOpenTrades.size() > 0
        for index = statsOpenTrades.size() - 1 to 0
            openTrade t_rade = statsOpenTrades.get(index)

            // the entry bar itself can neither win nor lose
            if bar_index > t_rade.openIndex
                float favorable = t_rade.bias == BULLISH ? high - t_rade.entry : t_rade.entry - low
                t_rade.maxFavorableR := math.max(t_rade.maxFavorableR, favorable / t_rade.riskUnit)

                bool stopHit = t_rade.bias == BULLISH ? low <= t_rade.stop : high >= t_rade.stop
                bool targetHit = t_rade.bias == BULLISH ? high >= t_rade.target : low <= t_rade.target
                bool expired = bar_index - t_rade.openIndex >= statsHorizonInput

                // the pessimistic tie rule lives in these two lines: stopHit wins every comparison
                bool isWin = targetHit and not stopHit
                bool isTimeout = expired and not stopHit and not targetHit

                if stopHit or targetHit or expired
                    int t_ype = t_rade.typeIndex
                    // [IDX3] A stop is filled at the stop price only if the bar traded there. A bar that GAPS
                    //        through it fills at the open, and a bar that closes locked at the limit against the
                    //        trade fills no better than its close. Losses can therefore exceed -1R. The target
                    //        keeps its exact price, so a favourable gap is never counted.
                    float stopFill = t_rade.bias == BULLISH ? math.min(open, t_rade.stop) : math.max(open, t_rade.stop)
                    if t_rade.bias == BULLISH and arbLocked
                        stopFill := math.min(stopFill, close)
                    else if t_rade.bias == BEARISH and araLocked
                        stopFill := math.max(stopFill, close)
                    float stopR = (t_rade.bias == BULLISH ? stopFill - t_rade.entry : t_rade.entry - stopFill) / t_rade.riskUnit
                    float grossR = stopHit ? stopR : targetHit ? statsTargetRInput : (t_rade.bias == BULLISH ? close - t_rade.entry : t_rade.entry - close) / t_rade.riskUnit
                    // [IDX3] round-trip costs in R. Exit rows are not trades, so they carry no cost.
                    float feeR = statsIsExitRow(t_rade.typeIndex) ? 0.0 : statsRoundTripFee * t_rade.entry / t_rade.riskUnit
                    float resultR = grossR - feeR

                    if isWin
                        statsWins.set(t_ype, statsWins.get(t_ype) + 1)
                    else if isTimeout
                        statsTimeouts.set(t_ype, statsTimeouts.get(t_ype) + 1)
                    else
                        statsLosses.set(t_ype, statsLosses.get(t_ype) + 1)

                    statsSumR.set(t_ype, statsSumR.get(t_ype) + resultR)
                    statsSumMFE.set(t_ype, statsSumMFE.get(t_ype) + t_rade.maxFavorableR)
                    statsOpenTrades.remove(index)
                    // same if/else return-type normalisation used in deleteFairValueGaps()
                    0

// @function            [L-STAT] percentage, or an em dash when there is nothing to divide by
// @returns             string
statsPercent(int numerator, int denominator) =>
    denominator > 0 ? str.tostring(100.0 * numerator / denominator, '0.0') + '%' : '—'

// @function            [L-STAT] average, or an em dash when there is nothing to divide by
// @returns             string
statsAverage(float total, int denominator) =>
    denominator > 0 ? str.tostring(total / denominator, '0.00') : '—'

// [L-STAT] Confirmed bars only. Open trades are advanced BEFORE new ones are opened, though the
//          `bar_index > openIndex` guard inside statsUpdateTrades() makes the order harmless either way.
if statsEnabledInput and barstate.isconfirmed
    statsUpdateTrades()

    if currentAlerts.internalBullishBOS
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_INT_BOS_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.internalBearishBOS
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_INT_BOS_BEAR, BEARISH)
    if currentAlerts.internalBullishCHoCH
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_INT_CHOCH_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.internalBearishCHoCH
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_INT_CHOCH_BEAR, BEARISH)
    if currentAlerts.swingBullishBOS
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_SWING_BOS_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.swingBearishBOS
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_SWING_BOS_BEAR, BEARISH)
    if currentAlerts.swingBullishCHoCH
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_SWING_CHOCH_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.swingBearishCHoCH
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_SWING_CHOCH_BEAR, BEARISH)
    if currentAlerts.bullishSweepReversal
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_SWEEP_REV_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.bearishSweepReversal
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_SWEEP_REV_BEAR, BEARISH)
    // [R1] zone retests, the entries BOS/CHoCH only confirm after the fact
    if currentAlerts.bullishOBRetest
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_OB_RETEST_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.bearishOBRetest
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_OB_RETEST_BEAR, BEARISH)
    if currentAlerts.bullishFVGRetest
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_FVG_RETEST_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.bearishFVGRetest
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_FVG_RETEST_BEAR, BEARISH)
    // [B1] composite reversal signal
    if currentAlerts.bullishConfluence
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_CONFLUENCE_BULL, BULLISH)
    if currentAlerts.bearishConfluence
        statsOpenTrade(STAT_CONFLUENCE_BEAR, BEARISH)

string statsTablePosition = statsTablePositionInput == 'Top Right' ? position.top_right : statsTablePositionInput == 'Middle Right' ? position.middle_right : statsTablePositionInput == 'Bottom Right' ? position.bottom_right : statsTablePositionInput == 'Top Left' ? position.top_left : statsTablePositionInput == 'Bottom Left' ? position.bottom_left : position.bottom_center

// [T1] Compact: 4 columns, short names, no grid lines, translucent background. MFE and T/O% move into the
//      tooltip of each row's name cell, so nothing is lost. Full: the original 6-column gridded table.
bool statsCompact = statsTableStyleInput == 'Compact'
var table statsTable = table.new(statsTablePosition, 6, STAT_TYPES + 2, border_width = statsCompact ? 0 : 1, border_color = color.new(GRAY, 60), frame_width = statsCompact ? 0 : 1, frame_color = color.new(GRAY, 60))
// short row names for the compact style, same order as statsNames
var array<string> statsShortNames = array.from('iBOS ▲', 'iBOS ▼', 'iCHoCH ▲', 'iCHoCH ▼', 'BOS ▲', 'BOS ▼', 'CHoCH ▲', 'CHoCH ▼', 'Sweep ▲', 'Sweep ▼', 'OB RT ▲', 'OB RT ▼', 'FVG RT ▲', 'FVG RT ▼', 'Conf ▲', 'Conf ▼')

// the settings this table was produced under, so a screenshot of it is self-describing
string statsMethodology = 'Entry at the signal bar close. 1R = ' + str.tostring(statsRiskAtrMultInput, '0.0') + ' x ATR(' + str.tostring(statsAtrLengthInput) + '). Target ' + str.tostring(statsTargetRInput, '0.0') + 'R. Horizon ' + str.tostring(statsHorizonInput) + ' bars.\n\nWin% is over resolved trades only (target or stop); timeouts are counted separately in T/O%. Expectancy and MFE are over all closed trades including timeouts.\n\nA bar that spans both the stop and the target counts as a LOSS, because the intrabar order is unknown.\n\nBreak-even win rate at this target is ' + str.tostring(100.0 / (1.0 + statsTargetRInput), '0.0') + '%, which is the line the Win% colour is measured against.\n\nStops that gap through fill at the open (and no better than the close on a limit-locked bar), so a loss can exceed -1R. Round-trip fees of ' + str.tostring(statsBuyFeeInput + statsSellFeeInput, '0.00') + '% are subtracted in R. Signals on a bar locked at the limit in the trade direction are not taken.' + (statsLongOnly ? '\n\nLONG-ONLY: rows marked "exit" are bearish signals measured as exits. Win = price fell ' + str.tostring(statsTargetRInput, '0.0') + 'R after the exit, loss = it rallied 1R. They carry no fees and are excluded from ALL.' : '') + '\n\nRows count only the signals that survive your current filter settings. This is a signal-quality measure, not a backtest.'

if statsTableEffective and (barstate.islast or barstate.islastconfirmedhistory)
    // [T1] Remove every cell before redrawing. The old code "blanked" leftover rows by writing '' into them,
    //      which CREATES empty cells, and empty cells still render with their borders and frame: that was the
    //      grid of blank rows left behind by "Hide Signal Types With No Data". A cleared cell does not exist,
    //      so a row with no cells collapses and the table is exactly as tall as its content.
    statsTable.clear(0, 0, 5, STAT_TYPES + 1)

    color statsHeaderBg = statsCompact ? color.new(chart.bg_color, 10) : color.new(GRAY, 75)
    color statsRowBg = statsCompact ? color.new(chart.bg_color, 35) : color(na)
    color statsTextColor = chart.fg_color
    color statsMutedColor = color.new(chart.fg_color, 45)
    float statsBreakeven = 100.0 / (1.0 + statsTargetRInput)

    statsTable.cell(0, 0, statsCompact ? 'Signal ⓘ' : 'Signal', text_color = statsMutedColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, text_halign = text.align_left, tooltip = statsMethodology)
    statsTable.cell(1, 0, 'N', text_color = statsMutedColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, tooltip = statsMethodology)
    statsTable.cell(2, 0, 'Win%', text_color = statsMutedColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, tooltip = statsMethodology)
    statsTable.cell(3, 0, statsCompact ? 'R' : 'Exp R', text_color = statsMutedColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, tooltip = statsMethodology)
    if not statsCompact
        statsTable.cell(4, 0, 'MFE R', text_color = statsMutedColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, tooltip = statsMethodology)
        statsTable.cell(5, 0, 'T/O%', text_color = statsMutedColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, tooltip = statsMethodology)

    int totalSignals = 0
    int totalWins = 0
    int totalLosses = 0
    int totalTimeouts = 0
    float totalR = 0.0
    float totalMFE = 0.0
    int r_ow = 0

    for i = 0 to STAT_TYPES - 1
        int n = statsCount.get(i)
        int w = statsWins.get(i)
        int l = statsLosses.get(i)
        int t_o = statsTimeouts.get(i)
        int resolved = w + l
        int closed = w + l + t_o

        // [IDX3] exit rows are not trades, so they stay out of the ALL total
        if not statsIsExitRow(i)
            totalSignals += n
            totalWins += w
            totalLosses += l
            totalTimeouts += t_o
            totalR += statsSumR.get(i)
            totalMFE += statsSumMFE.get(i)

        if n > 0 or not statsHideEmptyRowsInput
            r_ow += 1
            float winRate = resolved > 0 ? 100.0 * w / resolved : na
            float expectancy = closed > 0 ? statsSumR.get(i) / closed : na

            // [T1] compact rows use the short name, mark exit rows with ✕, and carry the dropped columns in a tooltip
            string rowName = statsCompact ? statsShortNames.get(i) + (statsIsExitRow(i) ? '✕' : '') : statsNames.get(i) + (statsIsExitRow(i) ? ' exit' : '')
            string rowTooltip = statsNames.get(i) + (statsIsExitRow(i) ? ' (exit signal)' : '') + '\nMFE ' + statsAverage(statsSumMFE.get(i), closed) + 'R · Timeout ' + statsPercent(t_o, closed) + '\nWins ' + str.tostring(w) + ' · Losses ' + str.tostring(l) + ' · Open ' + str.tostring(n - closed)

            statsTable.cell(0, r_ow, rowName, text_color = statsTextColor, bgcolor = statsRowBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, text_halign = text.align_left, tooltip = rowTooltip)
            statsTable.cell(1, r_ow, str.tostring(n), text_color = statsTextColor, bgcolor = statsRowBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
            statsTable.cell(2, r_ow, statsPercent(w, resolved), text_color = na(winRate) ? statsTextColor : winRate >= statsBreakeven ? GREEN : RED, bgcolor = statsRowBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
            statsTable.cell(3, r_ow, statsAverage(statsSumR.get(i), closed), text_color = na(expectancy) ? statsTextColor : expectancy > 0 ? GREEN : RED, bgcolor = statsRowBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
            if not statsCompact
                statsTable.cell(4, r_ow, statsAverage(statsSumMFE.get(i), closed), text_color = statsTextColor, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
                statsTable.cell(5, r_ow, statsPercent(t_o, closed), text_color = statsTextColor, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)

    int totalResolved = totalWins + totalLosses
    int totalClosed = totalWins + totalLosses + totalTimeouts
    float totalWinRate = totalResolved > 0 ? 100.0 * totalWins / totalResolved : na
    float totalExpectancy = totalClosed > 0 ? totalR / totalClosed : na

    r_ow += 1
    string totalTooltip = 'All ' + (statsLongOnly ? 'long ' : '') + 'signal types combined (rows overlap, read the rows rather than this total)\nMFE ' + statsAverage(totalMFE, totalClosed) + 'R · Timeout ' + statsPercent(totalTimeouts, totalClosed)
    statsTable.cell(0, r_ow, statsLongOnly ? (statsCompact ? 'ALL ▲' : 'ALL (long)') : 'ALL', text_color = statsTextColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput, text_halign = text.align_left, tooltip = totalTooltip)
    statsTable.cell(1, r_ow, str.tostring(totalSignals), text_color = statsTextColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
    statsTable.cell(2, r_ow, statsPercent(totalWins, totalResolved), text_color = na(totalWinRate) ? statsTextColor : totalWinRate >= statsBreakeven ? GREEN : RED, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
    statsTable.cell(3, r_ow, statsAverage(totalR, totalClosed), text_color = na(totalExpectancy) ? statsTextColor : totalExpectancy > 0 ? GREEN : RED, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
    if not statsCompact
        statsTable.cell(4, r_ow, statsAverage(totalMFE, totalClosed), text_color = statsTextColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
        statsTable.cell(5, r_ow, statsPercent(totalTimeouts, totalClosed), text_color = statsTextColor, bgcolor = statsHeaderBg, text_size = statsTableSizeInput)
    // [T1] nothing to blank below the ALL row any more: the clear() at the top already removed it

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//ALERTS
//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------{
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBullishBOS, 'Internal Bullish BOS', 'Internal Bullish BOS formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBullishCHoCH, 'Internal Bullish CHoCH', 'Internal Bullish CHoCH formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBearishBOS, 'Internal Bearish BOS', 'Internal Bearish BOS formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBearishCHoCH, 'Internal Bearish CHoCH', 'Internal Bearish CHoCH formed')

// [B13] these four used to carry the internal-structure wording
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBullishBOS, 'Bullish BOS', 'Bullish BOS formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBullishCHoCH, 'Bullish CHoCH', 'Bullish CHoCH formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBearishBOS, 'Bearish BOS', 'Bearish BOS formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBearishCHoCH, 'Bearish CHoCH', 'Bearish CHoCH formed')

// [E29] mitigation and invalidation used to share one alert worded "Price broke ...", which fired on a
//       50% fill. They are now separate events with accurate wording.
//       TITLES CHANGED: alerts bound to the old "... OB Breakout" titles must be recreated.
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBullishOrderBlock, 'Bullish Internal OB Mitigated', 'Price mitigated a bullish internal OB')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBearishOrderBlock, 'Bearish Internal OB Mitigated', 'Price mitigated a bearish internal OB')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBullishOrderBlock, 'Bullish Swing OB Mitigated', 'Price mitigated a bullish swing OB')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBearishOrderBlock, 'Bearish Swing OB Mitigated', 'Price mitigated a bearish swing OB')

alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBullishOrderBlockBroken, 'Bullish Internal OB Broken', 'Price traded fully through a bullish internal OB')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.internalBearishOrderBlockBroken, 'Bearish Internal OB Broken', 'Price traded fully through a bearish internal OB')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBullishOrderBlockBroken, 'Bullish Swing OB Broken', 'Price traded fully through a bullish swing OB')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.swingBearishOrderBlockBroken, 'Bearish Swing OB Broken', 'Price traded fully through a bearish swing OB')

alertcondition(currentAlerts.equalHighs, 'Equal Highs', 'Equal highs detected')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.equalLows, 'Equal Lows', 'Equal lows detected')

alertcondition(currentAlerts.bullishFairValueGap, 'Bullish FVG', 'Bullish FVG formed')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bearishFairValueGap, 'Bearish FVG', 'Bearish FVG formed')

// [I25] fired when a gap flips role; the bias reported is the NEW one
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bullishIFVG, 'Bullish IFVG', 'Bearish FVG inverted into bullish support')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bearishIFVG, 'Bearish IFVG', 'Bullish FVG inverted into bearish resistance')

// [J-MFI] these follow the same "Only Mark Zone Entry" setting as the on-chart markers
alertcondition(mfiOverboughtSignal, 'MFI Overbought', 'Money Flow Index reached the overbought level')
alertcondition(mfiOversoldSignal, 'MFI Oversold', 'Money Flow Index reached the oversold level')

// [RS1] these follow the same "Only Mark Zone Entry" setting as the RSC diamonds
alertcondition(rscStrongSignal, 'RSC Strong', 'Relative strength vs the benchmark is above the strong level')
alertcondition(rscWeakSignal, 'RSC Weak', 'Relative strength vs the benchmark is below the weak level')

// [W1] alertcondition messages cannot be dynamic, so there is one alert per phase (no generic "changed" alert: the script is at the 64-plot limit)
alertcondition(wyEnteredAccumulation, 'Wyckoff Accumulation', 'Price entered a Wyckoff accumulation range')
alertcondition(wyEnteredMarkup, 'Wyckoff Markup', 'Wyckoff markup phase started')
alertcondition(wyEnteredDistribution, 'Wyckoff Distribution', 'Price entered a Wyckoff distribution range')
alertcondition(wyEnteredMarkdown, 'Wyckoff Markdown', 'Wyckoff markdown phase started')
// [RS1] spring and upthrust share one alert (64-plot limit); the chart markers tell them apart
alertcondition(showRegimeInput and (wySpring or wyUpthrust) and not regimeTrending, 'Wyckoff Spring / Upthrust', 'Spring or upthrust: price pierced the trading range edge and closed back inside')

// [F24] the sweep alerts are context, the reversal alerts are the actual setup
alertcondition(currentAlerts.buysideLiquiditySweep, 'Buyside Liquidity Sweep', 'Buyside liquidity swept above equal highs')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.sellsideLiquiditySweep, 'Sellside Liquidity Sweep', 'Sellside liquidity swept below equal lows')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bullishSweepReversal, 'Bullish Sweep Reversal', 'Sellside sweep followed by a bullish CHoCH')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bearishSweepReversal, 'Bearish Sweep Reversal', 'Buyside sweep followed by a bearish CHoCH')

// [R1] zone retests. Use "Once Per Bar Close": the close back out of the zone is part of the definition.
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bullishOBRetest, 'Bullish OB Retest', 'Price wicked into a bullish order block and closed back above it')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bearishOBRetest, 'Bearish OB Retest', 'Price wicked into a bearish order block and closed back below it')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bullishFVGRetest, 'Bullish FVG Retest', 'Price wicked into a bullish FVG and closed back above it')
alertcondition(currentAlerts.bearishFVGRetest, 'Bearish FVG Retest', 'Price wicked into a bearish FVG and closed back below it')

//---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------}